Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can walk through this and give practical, step-by-step guidance — but I’m not a certified tax professional. This is general information based on 2023 U.S. federal rules; please consult a CPA or tax preparer for definitive advice on your specific return.

Summary answer up front
- Likely best to take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) unless you have additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, significant state/local taxes or medical expenses) that push total itemized deductions above $27,700.
- You can deduct student loan interest up to $2,500 (an above-the-line deduction) if your modified AGI is below the phaseout range; based on the income numbers you gave you’d likely qualify for the full $2,500 (but see questions below).
- Your 6-client marketing income is self-employment income and should be reported on Schedule C. Business expenses (including a home-office allocation and qualifying business expenses) reduce that income.
- Home-office: you can claim a business-use-of-home deduction for the self-employment work. Using the actual-expense method here (your rent share) likely gives a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses for a W-2 job are not deductible for 2018–2025 (so those $1,350 are not deductible if they’re unreimbursed expenses from your W-2 job). If they were business expenses related to your marketing/self-employment work, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Details, calculations, and how to treat each item

1) Income you must report
- W-2 wages: $79,400 — reported on Form 1040 (box details from your W-2).
- Self-employment (marketing) income: $22,100 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210 — report as interest income (Form 1040; you’ll get 1099‑INT / 1099‑B as appropriate).
- Even if you don’t receive 1099s for the marketing work, you must still report the income.

2) Self-employment tax and related items
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self-employment tax (Schedule SE). SE tax is ~15.3% on net earnings (Social Security up to the wage base + Medicare). You may deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- You must also pay income tax on the net profit after deductions.

3) Home-office deduction (self-employed)
You have two options: simplified or actual.

- Simplified method: $5 per sq. ft. up to 300 sq. ft.
  - 130 sq ft × $5 = $650 deduction.

- Actual-expense method: allocate a percentage of eligible home expenses to the business.
  - Your allocation: 130 sq ft ÷ 620 sq ft = 0.209677 ≈ 21.0%.
  - Rent: $1,720/mo × 12 = $20,640. Business portion = 21.0% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330.
  - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc., add their business percentages as well.
  - You generally use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute and support the deduction if using actual expenses.

Because the actual method applied to your rent (≈$4,330) is larger than the simplified result ($650), the actual method will likely produce a larger Schedule C expense. Use the actual method if you have good records and no complexity (e.g., if you later sell a home you owned and claimed depreciation, other rules apply). Keep receipts and a floor-plan or documentation showing exclusive, regular use.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800 you reported)
- You can deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) for 2023, subject to phaseouts based on MAGI.
- The 2023 MAGI phaseout for married filing jointly starts at $145,000 and phases out to $175,000 (above $175k no deduction). Based on your income numbers below, you likely fall below the phaseout and can take the full $2,500 (but see question about which years the interest was paid).
- Important: only interest paid in the tax year (2023) is deductible. If the $4,800 is interest paid in prior years (2016–2020), those payments do NOT affect your 2023 deduction. Check your 1098‑E for interest paid in 2023 and confirm the amount.

5) Charitable contribution: $1,350
- Cash contribution to a qualified charity is deductible only if you itemize. You must have written receipt for amounts over $250 (you said you have the receipt).
- If you take the standard deduction instead, you do not separately deduct this $1,350 on the federal return.

6) Unreimbursed expenses: $1,350
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job (e.g., travel, supplies for the employer), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under the current law.
- If they are business expenses for your marketing/self-employed activity, they are deductible on Schedule C — list them as ordinary and necessary business expenses and keep receipts.

7) Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Itemizable deductions include state and local taxes (SALT, capped at $10,000), mortgage interest, charitable contributions, medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, etc.
- Based on the amounts you listed (charity $1,350 and no mortgage interest or property tax numbers provided), your itemized deductions as shown would be far below $27,700 — so the standard deduction will almost certainly be better.
- If you paid substantial mortgage interest or $10k SALT (state income tax + property tax), you might reach or exceed the standard deduction; tell me those amounts if you want a recalculation.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main individual return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, deduction for one-half of self-employment tax, other adjustments).
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — report your marketing/self-employment income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — calculate self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — expenses for business use of your home (if you use the actual-expense method for the home office).
  - If you use the simplified method, you do not complete Form 8829 — you simply claim the simplified amount on Schedule C.
- Keep Form W-2 from Riverside Hospital and any 1099-NEC, 1099-MISC, 1099-INT, 1099-B, and 1098-E (student loan interest statement for 2023).
- If you itemize, you’d use Schedule A as well — but you’ll likely take the standard deduction given the figures you listed.
- State: Illinois Form IL‑1040 (Illinois uses federal AGI as a starting point; file state return if required).

9) Records to gather (action list)
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital.
- Any 1099s for the marketing work, or records of payments received (invoices, bank deposits).
- 1099-INT from Chase and brokerage statements from TD Ameritrade (1099-B if you had sales; 1099-INT for interest).
- 1098-E for interest paid in 2023 (student loan interest statement for 2023).
- Receipts/documentation for:
  - Charitable contribution ($1,350 receipt you mentioned).
  - Business expenses and the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses (if they relate to the self-employment work).
  - Rent statements/lease showing $1,720/mo and home square footage.
- Any state/local tax or property tax statements if you think SALT might push you into itemizing.

10) Estimated/other tax considerations
- Because you have self-employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (Form 1040‑ES) if you expect to owe $1,000 or more when you file.
- You’ll owe self-employment tax on net earnings (Schedule SE). You can deduct one-half of that SE tax on Schedule 1.
- If you want a ballpark of expected federal tax liability, I can run a rough estimate if you confirm:
  - whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are self-employment expenses,
  - whether the $4,800 in student interest is for 2023 or earlier years,
  - whether there is any other income (spouse’s income, unemployment, dividends, capital gains),
  - whether you paid state/local taxes or mortgage interest.

Questions for you (so I can give a precise answer)
1. Is the $4,800 in student loan interest paid in 2023 (i.e., will you receive a 2023 1098‑E showing $4,800)? Or is that interest paid in prior years (2016–2020)? Only interest paid in 2023 counts on your 2023 return.
2. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your self-employment marketing work, or unreimbursed expenses related to your W-2 job? (If they’re for self-employment, they’re deductible on Schedule C; if for W‑2 employment, they’re not deductible.)
