Thanks — I can walk through how to report these items, which deductions you can take, whether you’ll likely itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I need two quick clarifications before finalizing amounts:

- Is the $4,800 in student loan interest paid in tax year 2023, or is that interest you paid in earlier years (2016–2020)? Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible on your 2023 return.
- Is the $22,100 “marketing” income self‑employment income (you were an independent contractor / sole proprietor), or was it W‑2 wages from another employer? Also, are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to that marketing work (i.e., expenses you paid for your own business/clients)?

Below is the guidance based on the usual interpretations and the numbers you gave. I’ll note assumptions and give an example calculation.

1) Which income to report
- W‑2 wages from Riverside Hospital: report on Form 1040 (W‑2 box amounts).
- Marketing $22,100: if self‑employment/independent contractor, report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If it was W‑2, it’s on your W‑2 and not Schedule C.
- Interest/dividends: $920 (Chase savings) → report as interest (Form 1099‑INT); $290 (TD Ameritrade) → likely dividends or interest (Form 1099‑DIV/1099‑INT). Report on Form 1040.

2) Self‑employment tax and business expenses
- If the $22,100 is self‑employment income, you must file Schedule C and Schedule SE.
- Business expenses directly related to the marketing work (the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses, if they’re business expenses) are deductible on Schedule C and reduce your self‑employment net income.
- You pay self‑employment tax (Social Security & Medicare) on net self‑employment income. You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.

Example (using your numbers and assuming marketing income = self‑employment, unreimbursed expenses are business expenses):
- Gross self‑employment income: $22,100
- Business expenses: $1,350 (unreimbursed) + home office deduction (see next)
- Net self‑employment income (example after home office) — used to compute SE tax and net income.

3) Home office rules and calculation
- You meet the basic test if the 130 sq ft area is used regularly AND exclusively for the business and it is your principal place of business (or used to meet clients). That makes it eligible for the home office deduction if you are self‑employed.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650 (flat, no Form 8829).
  - Actual (usually larger here): allocate a percentage of allowable home expenses to the business: percentage = 130 / 620 = 20.9677% (≈21.0%).
    - You said you pay $1,720/month rent → annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
    - Home‑office portion of rent = 20.9677% × $20,640 ≈ $4,328.
    - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., add their home‑office portion too. Use Form 8829 if you choose actual method.
  - Choose the method that gives the larger deduction; actual method here (≈$4,328) likely beats the simplified $650.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- Only interest paid in the tax year (2023) is deductible on your 2023 return. If your $4,800 was paid in 2016–2020, it cannot be deducted for 2023.
- The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 (adjustment to income) and is subject to MAGI phaseouts for joint filers (phaseout for 2023 applies starting at $145,000 MAGI and phases out completely at $175,000 — if your MAGI is below $145k you can generally claim up to $2,500).
- The deduction is taken on Form 1040 as an adjustment to income (you’ll usually receive Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer showing interest paid if > $600, but you can still claim the deduction if you paid interest even without a 1098‑E; keep your receipt).

5) Charitable contribution ($1,350)
- Cash donations to qualified charities are deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). Habitat for Humanity is a qualified charitable organization; keep your receipt.
- If you take the standard deduction (see below), you cannot separately deduct this contribution on Schedule A.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- If you are a W‑2 employee and these are unreimbursed employee expenses, they are generally NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 because of the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions. If they are business expenses for self‑employment, they ARE deductible on Schedule C.

7) Standard deduction vs itemize (recommendation)
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly for 2023: $27,700.
- Known potential itemized deductions you listed: charitable $1,350. You did not list mortgage interest, large state & local tax payments, or other amounts that would raise itemized deductions above $27,700.
- Conclusion: With the information provided, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction ($27,700) rather than itemizing (unless you have significant additional itemizable amounts — e.g., mortgage interest, large property taxes, large state income taxes, unreimbursed medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI, etc.).
- Note: The home office deduction does NOT force you to itemize — it’s reported on Schedule C and reduces your business income regardless of whether you itemize.

8) Forms you will likely need for 2023 federal return
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest and deductible part of SE tax are reported here as adjustments.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — to report the $22,100 marketing income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax on Schedule C net income.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual home office method (don’t use this if you use the simplified method).
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (probably not based on what you told me).
- Supporting forms you should collect: W‑2 from Riverside Hospital; 1099‑NEC or records of the $22,100; 1099‑INT (Chase $920), 1099‑DIV (TD Ameritrade $290) or year‑end statements; Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) if issued; receipts for charitable donations and business expenses; proof of rent payments and related home expenses for home office calculation.
- Illinois state return: IL individual income tax form (IL‑1040). State rules differ; Illinois has its own treatment of deductions and income.

9) Quick example tax-impact sketch (approximate)
Assumptions:
- Self‑employment income = $22,100; business expenses = $1,350 + actual home office $4,328 = $5,678.
- Net self‑employment income ≈ $22,100 − $5,678 = $16,422.
- Self‑employment tax base = 92.35% × $16,422 ≈ $15,171 → SE tax ≈ $15,171 × 15.3% ≈ $2,323. Half of that (~$1,162) is an above‑the‑line deduction.
- Total income = $79,400 + $22,100 + $920 + $290 = $102,710
- Adjusted gross income (AGI) ≈ $102,710 − $1,162 (half SE tax) − (student loan interest if eligible, up to $2,500) = roughly $101,548 before student interest deduction.
- Then subtract standard deduction $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $73,848 (rough rough numbers; actual tax depends on final adjustments/credits).

10) Next steps I recommend
- Confirm the two clarifying items I asked at the top: whether the $4,800 interest was paid in 2023 and whether the marketing income/unreimbursed expenses are self‑employment related.
- Gather documents: W‑2, any 1099s, rent receipts, receipts for business expenses, charitable receipt, 1098‑E (student loan interest).
- If you want, I can:
  - Run a more precise sample calculation (estimate federal tax and SE tax) if you confirm the clarifications and want me to use the actual home office vs simplified method.
  - Draft a checklist of documents to hand to a tax preparer or to upload to tax software.
  - Draft an email to your tax preparer summarizing these items and asking for any missing info.

Caveat: This is general guidance and not a substitute for professional tax advice. For complex issues (large itemizable expenses, depreciation for a home office if you own the home, state‑specific questions, or if you think you might owe estimated taxes), consult a CPA or tax preparer.

Tell me the two clarifications above and whether you want a detailed estimated tax calculation now; I’ll run the numbers and list exact forms to attach.

