Short summary / likely outcome
- You’ll report: W-2 wages ($79,400), self‑employment (marketing) income (~$22,100), and interest income ($920 + $290 = $1,210).  
- The home office deduction is eligible only for the self‑employment income; you can choose the simplified method ($5 × 130 sq ft = $650) or the actual method (your pro‑rata share of rent/utilities). Based on the rent you gave, the actual method (≈ $4,329) likely gives a larger deduction.  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for W‑2 employment) are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under the TCJA. If the $1,350 is a business expense related to your self‑employment, it is deductible on Schedule C.  
- Student loan interest: you can only deduct interest paid in the tax year (2023). The maximum student loan interest deduction is up to $2,500 (and it phases out at higher incomes). If the $4,800 represents interest paid in earlier years (2016–2020), those amounts are not deductible on your 2023 return (you’d need to amend the earlier returns if you missed deductions then).  
- Charitable contribution $1,350 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).  
- Given the standard deduction for married filing jointly in 2023 is $27,700, you will likely take the standard deduction unless you have substantial additional itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, property taxes, large medical expenses, etc.). From what you listed, itemizing probably will NOT beat the standard deduction.

Detailed breakdown and calculations

1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Form 1040)  
- Self‑employment income: $22,100 (Schedule C). Note: if you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include those amounts; income is taxable whether or not you received a 1099.  
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210. (Schedule B is only required if total interest/dividend > $1,500; you’re under that.)

2) Home office deduction (you gave: exclusive 130 sq ft; total 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/month)
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.  
- Business percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 0.20968 (≈ 21.0%).  
- Actual method (your share of rent): 21.0% × $20,640 ≈ $4,329. (If you pay utilities or other shared home costs, include your pro‑rata share of those too; if you have mortgage interest and depreciation those get included too — but you said you pay $1,720/month, which looks like rent.)  
- Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650.  
- Which to use: actual method gives a larger immediate deduction here (~$4,329 vs $650). If you use the actual method you’ll typically compute business portion on Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). If you use the simplified method you don’t use Form 8829 — you just record the simplified deduction on Schedule C.

Important note: home office deduction only reduces your self‑employment income. If your Schedule C net goes to zero, home office and other business deductions can’t create a net loss beyond certain limits (loss rules can get complex).

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job: they are not deductible (suspended through 2025 for most taxpayers).  
- If these are expenses of your marketing/self‑employed work, they are deductible on Schedule C and reduce your self‑employment income.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800, Sallie Mae, 2016–2020)
- You can deduct interest paid in the tax year (2023) up to $2,500 (subject to income limits and phaseouts). If the $4,800 is interest you paid in 2016–2020, those are not deductible on the 2023 return — you would have had to deduct them on each year’s return, or amend those prior year returns if needed. Check whether you received a Form 1098‑E for 2023 showing the interest you paid in 2023. If you actually paid student loan interest in 2023, you may be able to deduct up to $2,500 (and it’s an adjustment to income — it reduces AGI). The deduction phases out at higher MAGI for MFJ.

5) Charitable contribution $1,350
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). No above-the-line charitable deduction for 2023 (the special COVID-era above-the-line gift rules are no longer in effect).

6) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- Self‑employment net profit (Schedule C) is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). Half of the self‑employment tax is deductible as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1). This reduces your AGI.

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction, Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.  
- From the items you listed, itemized deductions would be small (charity $1,350 + maybe state taxes/property taxes if you paid any — you didn’t list those). So standard deduction is almost certainly larger. Use Schedule A only if your total itemized deductions exceed $27,700.

Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).  
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — for student loan interest adjustment or other adjustments (if applicable).  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 marketing income and business expenses (including business share of home office if you use either method).  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute self‑employment tax on net Schedule C profit.  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual expense method for home office; not needed for the simplified method.  
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize.  
- Possibly Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) or Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (for clients) — you don’t attach these, but you’ll use the amounts they report. Schedule B only if interest > $1,500.  
- If you paid estimated taxes for 2023, track Form 1040‑ES payments or payments on Form 1040; if you owe self‑employment tax you may have estimated tax obligations in future years.

Practical example (quick illustration using your numbers and assuming the $1,350 is a business expense)
- Gross self‑employment receipts: $22,100  
- Less business expense (unreimbursed but business-related): − $1,350 → $20,750  
- Less home office (actual method): − $4,329 → Schedule C net income ≈ $16,421  
- Net income subject to self‑employment tax = 92.35% × $16,421 ≈ $15,165 (used to compute SE tax)  
- Half of SE tax is deductible (adjustment), and the Schedule C net (after expenses) flows to Form 1040.  
- Then you compare taxable income after standard deduction ($27,700) vs itemized. With the numbers you gave, standard almost certainly wins.

Questions I need to finalize exact guidance and run numbers for you
1. Is the $4,800 student loan interest amount paid in 2023, or is that total interest paid in the past (2016–2020)? Did you receive a 2023 Form 1098‑E from Sallie Mae (shows interest paid in 2023)?  
2. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your marketing/self‑employment work, or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job?  
3. Do you pay mortgage interest or property taxes (do you own the home)? You gave a rent amount ($1,720/month) — is that rent for an apartment (tenant) or mortgage payment? (If you own, mortgage interest and property tax could change the itemize vs standard decision.)  
4. Do you have any other deductible items for 2023 (state income tax paid or state withholding, property taxes, large medical expenses, gambling losses, large charitable gifts, etc.)?  
5. Do you want me to run a side‑by‑side estimated tax calculation (approximate tax owed/refund) using the actual method vs simplified method and standard vs itemize?

