Short answer / bottom line
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) unless you have significant additional itemizable expenses (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, big medical expenses, or much larger charity). With only the $1,350 charitable gift shown, itemizing will be far below the standard deduction.
- The home-office deduction is available for your self-employment income (the $22,100). Because you rent, the regular method (direct allocation of rent/expenses) will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified $5/sq ft method. That deduction is taken on Schedule C (or Form 8829 if using the regular method) and reduces your self-employment income.
- Student loan interest may be deductible (up to $2,500) as an “above‑the‑line” adjustment to income, but eligibility depends on the year the interest was paid and your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI). I need clarification on the $4,800 you reported.
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (post‑2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act) are not deductible for 2018–2025. If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are instead business expenses for your freelance/consulting/self‑employment work, those are deductible on Schedule C.

Details, rules, and calculations

1) Income summary (from what you provided)
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self‑employment/marketing income: $22,100
- Interest/dividends: $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210
Estimated total gross income = $102,710 (before business & other adjustments).

2) Home office (you said: 130 sq ft exclusive, home = 620 sq ft, rent $1,720/mo)
- Percentage of home used for business = 130 / 620 = ~20.97% (~21%).
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
- Regular (actual-cost) method: rent portion allocable to home office ≈ 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330. This reduces Schedule C net profit.
- Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction.
- Recommendation: Use the regular method here (≈ $4,330) because that is much larger than the simplified $650. If you have utilities or renter’s insurance that are business-related, their allocable portions would further increase the deduction.

Notes: home office deduction is only allowed if the office is used regularly and exclusively for business and is your principal place of business (or used to meet clients). Because you’re self‑employed on the $22,100, you can claim it. If the 6‑client work is actually as an employee (W‑2) and not self‑employment, you cannot take the home office deduction.

3) Student loan interest
- You listed $4,800 (Sallie Mae, 2016–2020). For 2023 tax filing you can only deduct interest you actually paid in tax year 2023 (up to $2,500), and the deduction phases out based on MAGI for married filing jointly. Also the loan must be a qualified student loan and you must be legally obligated to pay it.
- Clarify: Did you pay $4,800 in student loan interest in 2023, or is that an aggregate for earlier years (2016–2020)? If it’s paid in 2023, you may be able to deduct up to $2,500 (subject to phaseouts). If those payments were in earlier years, they’re not deductible on the 2023 return.

4) Charitable contribution
- $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity — deductible if you itemize. (If you don’t itemize you get no tax benefit on the federal return.)

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses (for a job where you received a W‑2), they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 under current law.
- If they are ordinary and necessary business expenses related to your self‑employment (the $22,100), they belong on Schedule C and are deductible.

6) Self‑employment tax and related items
- Net profit from self‑employment (Schedule C) is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) and income tax. You pay self‑employment tax via Schedule SE. You may deduct one-half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income.
- If you have net self‑employment earnings ≥ $400, you must file Schedule SE and generally owe self‑employment tax.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Section 199A) on qualified self‑employment income — whether you can take it and how much depends on taxable income and the nature of the business (complex rules).

7) Itemize vs Standard deduction (MFJ standard deduction for 2023 = $27,700)
- Itemizable items you reported: charitable $1,350. Student loan interest is an adjustment (above-the-line), not an itemized deduction. Home office is taken against business income, not as an itemized deduction. You likely need a lot more itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large medical expenses > 7.5% of AGI, large SALT up to $10k) to exceed $27,700.
- Conclusion: Based on what you’ve told me so far, standard deduction is almost surely the better choice.

8) Forms you’ll likely need for federal 2023
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — to report adjustments to income and certain additional income items (business income is reported on Schedule C and flows through; certain adjustments such as half of self-employment tax, student loan interest if applicable).
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report the $22,100 and ordinary business expenses, including home office).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if you use the regular/actual expense method for the home office). If you use the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829.
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (to compute SE tax).
- Schedule A (Form 1040) — only if you decide to itemize (probably not in your case).
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — possible use if you qualify for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (complex — we can check eligibility).
- Form 1040-ES — if you need to make estimated tax payments (recommended if you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file).
- Illinois: IL-1040 for your state income tax return (and any state forms related to business income).

