I can walk you through what’s likely deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and what IRS forms you’ll need — with a few notes and a couple quick clarifying questions at the end so I can be more precise.

High-level summary (based on the numbers you gave)
- Total gross income (approx): W-2 $79,400 + self-employment $22,100 + interest $920 + $290 = $102,710.
- Because you’re self-employed for the $22,100, you’ll report that on Schedule C and will owe self-employment (SE) tax in addition to income tax.
- Student loan interest deduction: the maximum allowable deduction in a single year is $2,500 (subject to income phase-out rules). You listed $4,800 — only up to $2,500 may be deductible for the 2023 return (subject to MAGI rules).
- Charitable gift $1,350: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Unreimbursed expenses $1,350: whether these are deductible depends on whether they are business expenses for your self-employment (deductible on Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses from a W-2 job (not deductible for 2018–2025).
- Home office: you can likely take a home-office deduction for the self-employment work. Using the information you gave, the actual-method home-office deduction (based on rent) is substantially larger than the simplified method.

Detailed treatment and calculations (approximate)
1) Self-employment income and expenses
- Report the $22,100 marketing income on Schedule C.
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for your marketing work, include them on Schedule C (they are deductible there). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.

2) Home-office deduction
- You said the home office is 130 sq ft exclusive use in a 620 sq ft apartment, and you pay $1,720/month rent.
- Percentage of home used for business = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97% (≈21%).
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
- Actual-method home-office deduction (rent portion) ≈ $20,640 × 130/620 ≈ $4,328.
- Simplified method would be $5 × 130 sq ft = $650.
- Recommendation: use the actual method (Form 8829) because the rent-based share (~$4,328) is much larger than the simplified amount. Report this on Schedule C (either directly or via Form 8829 depending on the calculation method).
- Keep documentation: lease, rent checks/Bank statements, floor plan showing square footage, photos, utility bills if you allocate any utilities, and notes showing exclusive use and business purpose.

3) Self-employment tax
- After business expenses (including home office and other business expenses), compute net profit on Schedule C.
- SE tax is computed on Schedule SE (roughly 15.3% of ~92.35% of net self-employment earnings). You get an above-the-line deduction equal to one-half of SE tax on Form 1040 (this reduces AGI).

Example (approximate) using your numbers and assuming the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses:
- Self-employment gross: $22,100
- Less home office (actual-method): ≈ $4,328
- Less other business expenses: $1,350
- Net SE income ≈ $16,422
- SE tax base ≈ 0.9235 × $16,422 ≈ $15,176
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × $15,176 ≈ $2,323
- Deductible portion (above-the-line) ≈ $1,161

4) Student loan interest
- The maximum student loan interest deduction in a single tax year is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phase-out and other requirements). If you really paid $4,800 in interest in 2023, only up to $2,500 may be deductible on your 2023 return (and it phases out at higher incomes). Student loan interest is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1 / Form 1040), not an itemized deduction.

5) Charitable contribution
- $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized deduction on Schedule A if you itemize. (Keep the receipt.)
- If your only itemizable items are this $1,350 and no mortgage interest/property taxes or large state taxes, it is very likely your total itemized deductions will be far below the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly, so you would take the standard deduction.

6) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is large (so most people in your situation take the standard deduction).
- Likely outcome: standard deduction will be larger than your itemized deductions (you reported only $1,350 charitable; if you have no mortgage interest, large state & local taxes, or big medical expenses, itemizing won’t beat the standard deduction). So you will probably take the standard deduction.

7) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $22,100 and business expenses including home office allocation.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use actual method for home office; results feed into Schedule C.
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute SE tax.
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments to income (e.g., student loan interest and half SE tax deduction feed here).
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (likely you will not).
- Keep W-2 from Riverside Hospital and any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC or records for the $22,100 self-employment income; 1099-INT for $920 and $290 if provided (interest is reported on Form 1040; Schedule B only required if interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500).
- State return: Illinois individual income tax return (IL-1040) — Illinois taxes income separately; check state-specific rules. Chicago has no separate city income tax.

Other practical notes
- If you will owe additional tax because of SE tax / reduced withholding, consider making estimated tax payments (Form 1040-ES) or increasing withholding to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Keep thorough records: receipts, bank statements, mileage logs (if you have vehicle business use), leases, invoices, and receipts for charitable donations.
- If you have retirement plans options (SEP IRA, Solo 401(k)), you can reduce taxable income and save for retirement — consider these for future years.

Questions I need to be precise / next steps
1. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to your self-employment (marketing clients) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job? (If business, they’re deductible on Schedule C; if employee, they aren’t deductible for 2018–2025.)
2. The $4,800 student loan interest — was that paid in 2023 (i.e., interest paid during 2023), or is that an aggregate across multiple years? (The deduction is limited to interest paid in the tax year and has a $2,500 annual cap subject to MAGI phase-out.)
3. Do you have any other potential itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state income tax paid, medical expenses >7.5% AGI, or other charitable gifts) that weren’t listed? That would affect the itemize vs standard decision.
4. Did you receive any 1099s (1099-NEC, 1099-MISC, 1099-INT) for the self-employment and interest amounts?

If you want, I can:
- Run the numbers into a rough draft tax calculation (estimate your federal tax and self-employment tax) — I’ll need answers to the clarifying questions above.
- Or prepare a checklist of documents to gather for preparing the return or giving to a CPA.

Disclaimer: I’m not a CPA or tax attorney. This is general guidance based on the facts you provided. For complex situations or to confirm phase-out thresholds and exact figures, consult a tax professional or trusted tax software.

