Detailed treatment and calculations

1) Income to report
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 (Form W-2).
- Self‑employment/marketing income: $22,100 — report on Schedule C (even if you didn’t receive 1099s).
- Interest / investment amounts: $920 (savings) and $290 (TD Ameritrade) — report on Form 1040 (1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B as mailed to you).

2) Business (self‑employment) items
- Report the 22,100 on Schedule C. Subtract ordinary and necessary business expenses (advertising, supplies, phone portion, mileage, etc.). If your $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” were actually business expenses for this marketing work, include them on Schedule C.
- Self‑employment tax: net profit from Schedule C is subject to SE tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of net profit). You can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040.
- Estimated taxes: if you expect to owe > $1,000 tax when you file, consider quarterly estimated payments for 2024 (and check if you already made 2023 estimated payments).

3) Home office (important)
- You meet the basic test only for self‑employed taxpayers: part of home used regularly and exclusively for business, and it is your principal place of business.
- You have two methods:

  Regular method (allocation of actual expenses)
  - Office size: 130 sq ft; whole home 620 sq ft → business percentage = 130 / 620 = 21.0% (≈0.2097).
  - Rent: $1,720/month → annual rent = $20,640.
  - Deductible portion of rent = 20,640 × 0.209677 ≈ $4,327.
  - You may also deduct your share of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (business portion), and other allocable expenses. If you own the home you could also depreciate the business portion (Form 8829). If you are renting, you generally cannot take depreciation on the property, but you can deduct the allocated rent/utilities.
  - To use this method to calculate home office deduction for Schedule C, you normally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home).
  Simplified method
  - $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 (no Form 8829; report on Schedule C).
