6) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($1,350)
- If these are for your W‑2 job (employee unreimbursed expenses), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 for most employees.
- If they are business expenses for your self‑employment clients, include them on Schedule C.

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
- For 2023, the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $27,700.
- Which is better? Compare your itemizable expenses (Schedule A) to $27,700. From the details you gave:
  - Known possible Schedule A items: charitable $1,350. Student loan interest is NOT on Schedule A (it’s an above‑the‑line adjustment). Unreimbursed employee expenses generally do not go on Schedule A. You didn’t list mortgage interest, big property taxes, or other large deductible items.
  - Unless you have large state & local taxes + mortgage interest + significant medical expenses, your itemized total will almost certainly be well under $27,700.
  - Conclusion (based on the info provided): It is very likely better to take the standard deduction ($27,700) and claim the home office/business deductions on Schedule C so they reduce your AGI directly.

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income, e.g., student loan interest if eligible; and reporting certain other income/adjustments).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report the 22,100 and business expenses.
