I can walk through this and give practical guidance and an estimate, but I’m not a CPA. For anything final or if you have unusual facts, please confirm with a tax professional.

Summary answer up front
- You should report the $22,100 as self‑employment income (Schedule C). That lets you deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (including a home‑office share and the $1,350 of unreimbursed expenses if those were business expenses for those clients). Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) are not deductible on your federal return under current law.
- The home‑office deduction is available for your self‑employment activity (not for W‑2 work). Using your actual‑expense method looks better than the simplified method based on the numbers you gave.
- Student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 (not $4,800). Your income level (based on the amounts shown) should allow the full $2,500, but MAGI rules apply.
- Charitable gift $1,350 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). The 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $27,700, so with only $1,350 of known itemized deductions you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Forms you’ll use: Form 1040, Schedule C (profit/loss from self‑employment), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Schedule 1 (adjustments such as student loan interest), and Schedule A only if you choose to itemize. State return: IL‑1040 for Illinois.

Detailed breakdown + rough numbers (based on the amounts you gave)
Inputs you gave:
- W‑2 wages: $79,400
- Self‑employment (marketing) gross: $22,100
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210
- Rent: $1,720/month → annual rent = $20,640
- Home size: 130 sq ft exclusive office / 620 sq ft total
- Student loan interest paid: $4,800 (but federal limit is $2,500)
- Charitable gifts: $1,350 (you have receipt)
- Unreimbursed expenses: $1,350 (treatment depends on whether these are business expenses for the self‑employment)

Home office calculation (actual‑expense method)
- Office percentage = 130 / 620 = 20.9677% ≈ 21.0%
- Rent portion allocable to office = 20.9677% × $20,640 ≈ $4,327
- If you have other allocable home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs) you would pro‑rate those too and add them to the home office deduction. If you prefer the simplified method it would be 130 sq ft × $5 = $650 — much smaller than the actual rent share above.

Self‑employment net income (example assuming unreimbursed expenses are business expenses)
- Gross self‑employment: $22,100
- Less home office (≈ $4,327) and business expenses ($1,350) → net ≈ $16,423
- Self‑employment tax: computed on 92.35% of net profit → SE taxable base ≈ $15,165; SE tax ≈ $2,322
- Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $1,161 (above‑the‑line deduction)

Adjustments and AGI (approximate)
- Total income = 79,400 + 16,423 + 1,210 ≈ $97,033
- Less half SE tax ≈ $1,161 and student loan interest deduction (max) $2,500 → total adjustments ≈ $3,661
- Estimated AGI ≈ $93,372

Standard vs itemize
- 2023 standard deduction (MFJ) = $27,700.
- Known itemized amount you listed = charitable $1,350 (plus any state & local taxes or mortgage interest you haven’t listed). With only $1,350 of known Schedule A deductions, standard deduction will be much larger. Therefore you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- Note: home‑office and business deductions reduce your self‑employment income (they are on Schedule C), but they are not items on Schedule A — so they do not affect the standard vs itemize comparison directly beyond lowering AGI.

Student loan interest
- Max federal deduction is $2,500 (not $4,800). Income phaseouts apply (for 2023 MFJ phaseout roughly $145k–$175k MAGI); based on the numbers above you should be under the phaseout and can likely take the full $2,500. Keep your Form 1098‑E from Sallie Mae as proof.

Unreimbursed expenses
- If those $1,350 were expenses you paid in running the marketing business (supplies, advertising, travel/phone proportion, etc.), include them on Schedule C as business expenses.
- If they are unreimbursed employee business expenses tied only to your W‑2 job, they are not deductible on federal 2023 returns (suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions).

Other tax items and documents you’ll need
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital
- Form(s) 1099‑NEC or other records for the $22,100 (report all income even if no 1099 issued)
- Form 1099‑INT from Chase and 1099 (brokerage 1099) from TD Ameritrade for interest/dividends
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from Sallie Mae
- Receipts/documentation for charitable gift(s) (you have it)
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses (including those $1,350 and any utilities/renter’s insurance if you’ll allocate them to the home office)
- Proof of rent payments (to document rent if you use actual home‑office method)
- If you sell investments or receive dividends, gather any Form 1099‑B / 1099‑DIV

Forms to prepare/file
- Federal: Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — student loan interest adjustment shows here
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $22,100, business expenses, home office deduction entered here
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given numbers)
- State: IL‑1040 (Illinois) — report all income; IL has a flat rate (check current rate and rules). Illinois does not have the same itemized vs standard interplay as federal; check state specifics.
- Keep all supporting docs in case of audit.

Other practical items / suggestions
- Make sure to track self‑employment quarterly estimated tax payments for future years if you expect similar self‑employment income to avoid underpayment penalties.
- The home‑office deduction cannot create a loss for tax shelters beyond business profit: if your Schedule C ends with a loss, the home office deduction may be limited to the business profit (excess may carry forward under certain rules).
- Consider using tax software (most handle Schedule C & home‑office) or consult a CPA for filing if this is your first year with self‑employment income or you want to be sure about the home‑office allocation and SE tax computations.
- Keep your receipts and a short log of how the office space is used (exclusive, regular use, and principal place of business), and keep copies of rent and utility bills.

If you want, I can:
- Prepare a more exact calculation if you give me any additional deductible home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs), or any state taxes/property taxes you paid in 2023.
- Draft a checklist of documents to gather before you file.
- Prepare a sample Schedule C/SE worksheet with the numbers you provided so you can review before filing.

Which of those would you like me to do next?

