Summary of what you can/don’t can deduct (based on typical rules)
- W‑2 wages ($79,400): reported on Form 1040 using your W‑2.
- Self‑employment income ($22,100): report on Schedule C; you may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including home office if it’s for the business).
- Interest income ($920 + $290 = $1,210): report these on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INT / 1099s).
- Student loan interest: deductible up to $2,500 as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1 if you meet the income limits (phaseout for MFJ in 2023 begins at MAGI $145,000 and fully phases out at $175,000). If the $4,800 is not 2023 payments, you cannot deduct past‑year interest now — only 2023 interest paid is deductible.
- Charitable gift $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). No “above‑the‑line” deduction for ordinary cash gifts to charities for non‑itemizers in 2023.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job): not deductible for 2018–2025. If those $1,350 are business expenses related to your self‑employment, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home‑office: deductible only if you use the space regularly and exclusively for business. For self‑employed taxpayers the deduction is claimed on Schedule C (either the simplified method or actual-expense method). If you are an employee using it for your W‑2 job, it’s generally not deductible.

Home office — calculations and which method is better here
You said: home office 130 sq ft exclusively; total home 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/month.

- Business‑use percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 21.0%

Two options:
1) Simplified method: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650. Easy, no Form 8829.
2) Actual (regular) method: allocate actual home expenses to the business. Using rent only (annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640):
   - Rent portion deductible = 21.0% × $20,640 ≈ $4,328 (you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have them).
   - If you use the actual method you generally file Form 8829 (attached/part of Schedule C work) for home office.

Which looks better with your numbers
- If your self‑employment gross = $22,100 and business expenses (other deductible items) = $1,350 (assuming those are business-related), the math:

  A) Using simplified home‑office ($650):
   - Schedule C net profit ≈ 22,100 − 1,350 − 650 = 20,100

  B) Using actual home‑office (rent portion ≈ $4,328):
   - Schedule C net profit ≈ 22,100 − 1,350 − 4,328 = 16,422

- Self‑employment tax (approx): SE tax ≈ 15.3% on 92.35% of net profit (effectively about 14.13% of net profit).
  - A) SE tax ≈ 20,100 × 0.1413 ≈ $2,840 (half deductible = $1,420)
  - B) SE tax ≈ 16,422 × 0.1413 ≈ $2,321 (half deductible = $1,160)

So using the actual method (allocating rent) yields a much larger business deduction (~$4,328 vs $650) and lowers both your income tax and SE tax, if you have only rent as the housing expense. If you have additional eligible home expenses to allocate, that favors the actual method even more. If you are a renter (no depreciation), the actual method is simpler because you don’t have to worry about depreciation recapture; you still use Form 8829 or equivalent reporting for the actual method.

Standard deduction vs itemize
- Standard deduction for 2023 for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your known itemizable amounts (from what you provided) appear small:
  - Charitable gifts: $1,350
  - Possible SALT (state income tax or property tax) — not provided; subject to $10,000 SALT cap
  - Mortgage interest or large medical expenses: not reported
- The student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) is an above‑the‑line adjustment (reduces AGI) and does not depend on itemizing.
Conclusion: With the data you’ve given, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction ($27,700) because your itemizable deductions (charity $1,350 and whatever SALT/mortgage interest you might have) won’t exceed $27,700. If you do have sizable mortgage interest or significant state/local taxes or large charitable giving beyond what you listed, itemizing might become favorable.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — for student loan interest deduction (adjustment) and other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report $22,100 self‑employment income and business expenses (including home office deduction if self‑employed)
