1) How your income will be reported (overview)
- W-2 wages -> Form 1040, wages line (W‑2).
- Self‑employment income -> Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Net profit from Schedule C flows to Form 1040 and is subject to self‑employment tax.
- Interest income -> Report on Form 1040 (and attach 1099‑INT if issued).
- You may get 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients; all self‑employment income is taxable whether or not you received 1099s.

2) Home office treatment (self‑employment)
- Because your home office is used exclusively and regularly for your marketing business, you can claim a home office deduction on Schedule C.
- Two methods:
  - Actual method (recommended here): allocate a share of actual rent/utilities/etc. by square footage. Your allocation: 130 / 620 = 0.2097 (≈20.97%).
    - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
    - Home‑office share of rent ≈ 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,329 (this goes on Schedule C as part of business expenses; you can also allocate utilities if you have amounts).
    - If you use actual method and you have other direct/indirect home costs, you’d complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home).
