- You should keep receipts and records for all business expenses.

4) Self‑employment tax and adjustment to income
- Net self‑employment income = $22,100 − (home office $4,329 + business expenses $1,350 if they are business) = about $16,421 (example calculation).
- You pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on that net income; you calculate it on Schedule SE.
- You may deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces your AGI).

5) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction applies to interest paid during the tax year being filed (2023), and the maximum deduction is $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts).
- You wrote $4,800 and listed years 2016–2020. Interest paid in prior years (2016–2020) is not deductible on your 2023 return. To claim the 2023 deduction you would need interest actually paid in 2023 (and Form 1098‑E from the servicer or records).
- Phaseout: the deduction phases out at higher AGI levels for MFJ — based on your reported income, you are likely under the phaseout threshold for 2023, but I can confirm once we finalize AGI.
- If you did pay interest in 2023, you can claim up to $2,500 (or less, depending on your 2023 AGI).

6) Charitable contributions
- Your $1,350 gift to Habitat for Humanity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- There is no above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2023 (the special universal deduction that existed for some pandemic years is expired).
- You must have the receipt for the contribution.

7) Standard deduction vs itemizing (recommendation)
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly: $27,700.
