What is deductible (federal)
- Self-employment business expenses: If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for your marketing work (advertising, supplies, travel for clients, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W-2 job), they are generally NOT deductible for tax years through 2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- Home-office deduction (self-employed): Because you are self-employed for the marketing work and you have an exclusive-use office of 130 sq ft, you can take a home-office deduction. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650.
  - Actual (recommended here if you only provided rent): allocate allowable home expenses by the business percentage (130/620 ≈ 20.97%). With annual rent $20,640, the allocable rent portion is about $20,640 × 0.209677 ≈ $4,327. If you have utilities, insurance, repairs, or other eligible home expenses, they increase the deductible amount under the actual method. Because $4,327 >> $650, the actual method will likely give a larger deduction. Actual-method home-office deduction for self-employed filers is calculated on Form 8829 and flows to Schedule C.
- Student loan interest: The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line adjustment (up to $2,500) on Schedule 1 if you paid qualifying interest in the tax year. It’s phased out by MAGI (2023 phaseout for MFJ begins around $155,000 and is fully phased out near $185,000). Important: the deduction is for interest paid in the tax year (2023); interest paid in prior years (2016–2020) is not deductible on the 2023 return. You should have Form 1098-E from your servicer for interest paid in 2023.
- Charitable contribution: $1,350 is an itemized deduction on Schedule A, but it only helps if your total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction.
- Interest/dividends (Chase, TD Ameritrade): Reportable on Form 1040 (interest/ordinary dividends lines) — taxable unless investments are tax-exempt.
- Half of self-employment tax: Deductible as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (calculated from Schedule SE).

What you generally cannot deduct
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for W-2 work) — not deductible for 2023.
- Personal living expenses, rent for non-business portion of home, etc., are not deductible.

Whether to itemize or take the standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly: $27,700.
- Your only known itemized amount is the $1,350 charitable gift. Unless you also have substantial state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses, or other itemizable items that push total above $27,700, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction on the federal return.
- Note: home-office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it reduces Schedule C business income regardless of whether you itemize.

Other important items you’ll likely need to handle
- Self-employment tax: Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You must file Schedule SE to calculate that. You then deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A): You may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction of qualified business income from your Schedule C. That deduction has rules and thresholds, but with the income levels you gave, you may be able to take some QBI deduction. It’s reported using Form 8995 (or 8995-A).
- Estimated tax: If you expect tax to be owed after withholding, you may need to pay quarterly estimated taxes (Form 1040-ES) during the year to avoid penalties. Because you have W-2 withholding, you may or may not need to make estimates — depends on final tax liability.
- Illinois state tax: Illinois has a flat rate (around 4.95% for 2023) and you must file IL-1040. State rules differ; make sure to include all income on the state return. Illinois allows a standard exemption and has its own treatment of deductions (SALT cap on federal does not change state filing — but state tax is an itemized deduction on the federal return subject to the $10K SALT limit).

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main tax form)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — student loan interest deduction and deduction for half of SE tax appear here)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) for your marketing income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual home-office method (to compute allowable home-office deduction)
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) for the Qualified Business Income deduction (if applicable)
- Schedule A (if you choose to itemize — unlikely based on what you told me unless you have other itemizable items)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if you received them from clients) — attach/enter amounts on Schedule C
- 1099-INT (from Chase), 1099-DIV / 1099-B (TD Ameritrade) as applicable — report on 1040
- IL-1040 for Illinois state return

