Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer / bottom line
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) — your listed itemizable deductions (charity $1,350) are far below that unless you also have significant mortgage interest, property tax or other large deductible items you haven’t listed.
- Important exceptions/benefits regardless of itemizing:
  - If the student loan interest you paid was in 2023 (not 2016–2020), you can claim up to $2,500 as an above-the-line deduction (subject to income phaseouts).
  - Your 6-client marketing income is self-employment income and you should report it on Schedule C. That allows business expense deductions (including home office and the unreimbursed expenses if they were business expenses).
- Home office: because you have exclusive use of a 130 sq ft workspace in a 620 sq ft home, you can claim a home-office deduction for your self-employment work. Using the actual-expense allocation of rent (recommended here) will give a larger deduction than the simplified method.

Detailed breakdown and numbers (illustrative)
Assumptions: W-2 $79,400; self-employment gross $22,100; interest income $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210; rent $1,720/mo = $20,640/yr; home-office = 130/620 = 20.9677% of home.

1) Home office — two methods
- Actual-expense allocation (rent portion only, as an example): 20,640 * (130/620) = $4,330.32 deductible as a Schedule C business expense.
- Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650.

Using actual-expense allocation plus $1,350 of business unreimbursed expenses (assuming those are business expenses and not employee unreimbursed expenses) gives:
- Net self-employment income ≈ 22,100 − 4,330.32 − 1,350 = $16,419.68.
- Self-employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% of net = ≈ $2,322 (Schedule SE).
- Half of SE tax (≈ $1,161) is an above-the-line deduction.

Compare simplified method (for reference) — net SE income would be higher and tax higher; in your case the actual-expense allocation of rent gives a larger benefit than the simplified $650.

2) Student loan interest
- Only interest paid in tax year 2023 is eligible for the up-to-$2,500 student loan interest deduction (Form 1098-E documents this). Interest paid in 2016–2020 is not deductible on your 2023 return. If you did pay interest in 2023, it reduces your AGI (and you can take it even if you take the standard deduction) provided your MAGI is below the phaseout threshold (for MFJ 2023 the phaseout starts at $145,000 and ends at $175,000).

3) Charitable contribution
- $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). For most taxpayers in your situation that amount is too small to beat the $27,700 standard deduction unless you have other large itemized items (mortgage interest, property taxes, larger state and local taxes up to $10k cap, big medical expenses, etc.).

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are business expenses for your self-employment work, include them on Schedule C (deductible).
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — e.g., student loan interest, half SE deduction flows here)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses (home office, the $1,350 if business-related, other business expenses)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — for the self-employment tax calculation and to compute the half-SE deduction
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual-expense method for home office (optional if using simplified method)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (to claim the $1,350 charity and other itemized amounts)
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement (to support an interest deduction for 2023)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC or your own records of the 6 client payments (report all self-employment income even if you didn’t get 1099s)
- 1099-INT (Chase), 1099-DIV / 1099-B or 1099-INT (TD Ameritrade) as applicable
- Illinois state return (IL-1040) — IL has its own filing; Chicago has no local income tax

Other practical notes
- Keep receipts and proof for the home-office square footage, lease/rent payments, charitable donation receipts, business expense receipts, and 1098-E.
- If your net self-employment income (after expenses) is $400+ you must file Schedule SE and pay self-employment tax.
- If you owe tax (after withholding and credits) because of self-employment income, you may owe estimated taxes; consider whether you should have made estimated payments during 2023.

Questions I need to finalize this for you
- Did you pay any student loan interest in 2023 (not earlier years)? Do you have a 2023 Form 1098-E from Sallie Mae?
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your marketing/self-employment work, or are they employee unreimbursed expenses tied to your W-2 job?
- Do you rent (I assumed rent from your $1,720/mo) or own the home?
- Do you have other deductible items (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state tax payments, large medical expenses) not listed?

If you want, I can:
- Run the numbers again using your answers to the questions above and give an estimated taxable income / estimated federal tax.
- Draft a checklist or an email you can send to a tax preparer listing the documents to bring.

Disclaimer: I’m not a CPA or tax preparer. This is general information based on 2023 U.S. federal rules; for definitive advice tailored to your full situation, consult a tax professional.

