- Unreimbursed employee business expenses: Generally NOT deductible for employees (TCJA suspended these for 2018–2025). If the $1,350 is unreimbursed employee expenses from a W-2 job, it is not deductible on your federal return.
- Business expenses (self-employment): If that $1,350 is actually business expenses related to your $22,100 marketing/self-employed work, then it is deductible on Schedule C and reduces your self-employment net income.
- Home office: deductible if you are self-employed and use the space exclusively and regularly for your business (which you said). You can use either:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 130 sq ft x $5 = $650 deduction.
  - Regular method: prorate actual home expenses by business-use percentage. Your business-use % = 130 / 620 = 20.97% (≈21%). Annual rent $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,330 would be the share of rent allocable to the home office (plus allocable utilities if you pay them). Because you rent (not own), you generally won’t have home depreciation to worry about. The regular method will likely yield a larger deduction in your case, but it requires Form 8829 (unless you choose the simplified method).
- Self-employment tax: You will owe self-employment (SE) tax on net profit from Schedule C (generally 15.3% on ~92.35% of net profit). Half of the SE tax is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Interest and investment income: Report the $920 and $290 on the 1040 (you should receive 1099-INT / 1099-B / 1099-DIV as applicable).

2) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Your likely itemized deductions (from the facts you gave) look small: $1,350 charitable + whatever SALT (state income tax + property tax) you actually paid (SALT is capped at $10,000 total). Because your reported deductible items are far below $27,700, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger — unless you have other significant itemizable expenses (mortgage interest, large property taxes, large medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI, etc.) you didn’t list.
- Recommendation: Most likely take standard deduction. However, if you actually paid significant state/local taxes or other itemizable expenses you haven’t listed, re-check the totals before deciding.

3) How to treat the home office
- Only for your self-employed marketing income. You must use it regularly and exclusively for business.
- You can choose:
  - Regular method (Form 8829): allocates portion of rent/utilities to business. Using your numbers, rent share ≈ $4,330 (plus allocable utilities). This reduces Schedule C net income and therefore reduces both income and SE tax base.
  - Simplified method: claim $650 (130 sq ft × $5). Easier, no Form 8829, but smaller deduction here.
- Limits: Home office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business (after other business expenses). Any excess may be carried forward (special rules apply). Because your self-employment income is $22,100, the home office deduction should be usable unless other large business expenses push the business into a loss.
- If you are an employee (W-2) and not self-employed, you cannot take the home office deduction for 2018–2025.

4) Forms you’ll need for federal and IL
Federal (IRS)
- Form 1040 (individual tax return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for the student loan interest deduction (and half of SE tax, etc.)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report the $22,100 and business expenses (including home office if using simplified you report there; if regular method use Form 8829 to compute the deduction to carry to Schedule C)
- Schedule SE — calculate self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you choose the regular method for home office (computes allowable home office expenses)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC: include any 1099 income you received; report all self-employment income even if no 1099
- 1099-INT, 1099-DIV, 1099-B — for interest and investment income reporting
- Form 1040-ES — if you expect to owe self-employment tax / income tax and didn’t make sufficient withholding, you may need to make estimated quarterly tax payments (or pay when you file to avoid penalties)

State (Illinois)
