I can help — here’s a clear breakdown of how your 2023 federal tax picture looks based on what you gave, what you can and can’t deduct, which filing method likely benefits you, how the home-office deduction works, and the forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag a few clarifying questions so I can give exact numbers if you want.

Key assumptions and clarifying questions
- You’re filing married filing jointly for 2023 in Illinois.
- Clarify whether the $4,800 student-loan-interest amount was paid in tax year 2023 (you wrote “Sallie Mae, 2016–2020” — if those payments were earlier years, they don’t affect your 2023 return). The student loan interest deduction is limited to interest paid in the tax year being filed.
- Are you renting (the $1,720/mo is rent) or do you own and pay mortgage + property tax? (That changes available home and other deductions.)
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business (self-employed) expenses? I’ll assume yes (business expenses for your marketing work).

Income summary (based on the amounts you gave)
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self-employment (marketing, 6 clients): $22,100
- Interest: Chase savings $920 + TD Ameritrade $290 = $1,210
- Total reported gross income ≈ $102,710

What you can deduct (federal)
- Student loan interest:
  - The deduction is an “above-the-line” adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1). The maximum annual student-loan interest deduction is $2,500 (not $4,800). If you paid $4,800 in 2023, you can only deduct $2,500. If the $4,800 refers to earlier years, then none of that affects 2023.
  - Phase-outs apply at higher MAGI, but your gross income as reported (~$103k) is below typical MFJ phaseout thresholds, so you’re likely eligible for the full $2,500 (confirm exact MAGI once we compute AGI).
- Self-employment business expenses:
  - Your unreimbursed business expenses ($1,350) are deductible on Schedule C (they’re NOT deductible as itemized employee expenses).
  - Home-office deduction (see below).
- Home-office deduction:
  - You said 130 sq ft exclusive use out of 620 sq ft total → business-use percentage ≈ 21.0% (130/620).
  - Two methods:
    1. Simplified: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction (simple, no Form 8829).
    2. Actual-expenses: allocate business % of qualifying home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Example using only rent: annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640 → business portion ≈ 21% × $20,640 ≈ $4,334. If you have utilities and other deductible home expenses, the amount could be higher.
  - Because you have self-employment income, you are eligible for the home-office deduction. If you rent, you cannot take mortgage interest or property tax depreciation, but you can allocate rent and utilities.
  - If you use actual-expense method you must file Form 8829 (business use of home) with your return.
- Self-employment tax:
  - You must file Schedule SE to compute self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on your net self-employment income. You may deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Charitable contribution:
  - Your $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized deduction (Schedule A). It only helps if your total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction.
- Other items:
  - Interest income ($1,210) is taxable and reported on Form 1040. Because it’s under $1,500, you generally do not need Schedule B.

Standard deduction vs. itemizing
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your provided itemizable amounts (so far) are small: charitable $1,350. You did not list mortgage interest, property tax, large medical expenses, or large state/local taxes (SALT is capped at $10,000), so it’s very likely the standard deduction ($27,700) will be larger than your itemized deductions.
- Conclusion: Most likely take the standard deduction for 2023 unless you have additional itemizable deductions (large mortgage interest, property taxes, big state taxes, substantial other charitable gifts) that would push itemized > $27,700.

Rough numeric examples (to illustrate effect of home office choice)
(Assumes unreimbursed business expenses are deductible on Schedule C and student loan interest paid in 2023 is fully deductible up to $2,500. These are approximate illustrative calculations.)

Scenario A — simplified home-office method
- Self-employment gross: $22,100
- Business expenses: $1,350 (other unreimbursed)
- Home office simplified deduction: $650
- Net self-employment profit ≈ 22,100 − 1,350 − 650 = $20,100
- Net earnings subject to SE tax ≈ 20,100 × 0.9235 ≈ $18,571
- Self-employment tax ≈ 18,571 × 15.3% ≈ $2,840 (approx)
- Deductible half of SE tax (above-the-line) ≈ $1,420

Scenario B — actual-expense method using rent allocation only
- Business home allocation of rent ≈ $4,334 + business expenses $1,350 = $5,684 total expenses
- Net self-employment profit ≈ 22,100 − 5,684 = $16,416
- Net earnings for SE ≈ 16,416 × 0.9235 ≈ $15,167
- Self-employment tax ≈ 15,167 × 15.3% ≈ $2,321
- Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $1,160

Takeaway from the examples: Using actual allocation of rent (if you have rent and can substantiate it) generally produces a larger home-office deduction and reduces both income tax and self-employment tax versus the simplified method — but you must keep good records and file Form 8829 for actual expenses.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main tax return.
- Schedule 1 — Additional Income and Adjustments to Income (student loan interest deduction, the half of SE tax deduction, and other adjustments).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report your marketing self-employment income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — compute self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if you use the actual-expenses method for the home office).
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you choose to itemize instead of taking standard deduction).
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income depending on your net business income and whether you meet thresholds — I can check this if you want).
- Any 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC that you received from clients should be included on Schedule C; include W-2 (from Riverside Hospital) on Form 1040. Interest 1099-INT/1099-B info from TD Ameritrade goes on your return as taxable interest/dividends and capital gains info if relevant.

Illinois state return
- File IL-1040 for Illinois state income tax. Illinois starts with your federal AGI as the baseline and then has state-specific additions/subtractions. There is no separate Chicago local income tax.

Other practical notes
- Student loan interest: confirm the amount paid in 2023 and whether Sallie Mae provided a Form 1098-E for 2023. The deductible maximum is $2,500 and it’s an above-the-line deduction.
- Keep receipts and documentation for charitable deductions, all business expenses, and home-office calculations (square footage floorplan or measurements, rent receipts, utility bills).
- If you expect similar self-employment income in future years, consider making quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties.
- If your self-employment net income is substantial, you may be eligible for the QBI 20% deduction — we can check that after finalizing net business income and total taxable income.

Recommendations / next steps
1. Clarify: Did you actually pay $4,800 in student loan interest in 2023 or is that a historical total from 2016–2020? Are you renting or owning? Confirm the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business-related.
2. If you want, I can:
   - Recalculate more precisely after you confirm the above (including computing estimated AGI, taxable income, expected tax, and QBI effect).
   - Prepare a checklist of documents and 1099s/W‑2s you’ll need to file.
   - Draft a message/email to your tax preparer summarizing these numbers and asking for professional review.

I’m not a CPA or tax preparer — this is general guidance based on 2023 rules. If your situation is complex (ownership with mortgage and property tax, large itemized deductions, or uncertainty about student-loan interest year), I recommend a quick review with a tax professional.

Tell me the answers to the clarifying questions and I’ll run the exact numbers and list the forms/lines you’ll fill in.

