Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk you through how these items are treated for 2023 and give a practical recommendation. A few quick clarifying questions first (they change the answer):
- Were the $4,800 in student‑loan interest payments made in 2023 (or are those payments from earlier years 2016–2020)? Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible on your 2023 return.
- Are the $1,350 labeled “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to your marketing/self‑employment work (so you paid them as part of doing that work), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a job where you received a W‑2? (Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible 2018–2025.)
- Do you have any property tax or state income tax amounts for 2023 (for SALT comparison)? That affects whether itemizing could exceed the standard deduction.

Assuming you are married filing jointly, live in IL, and interpreting the items as follows:
- W‑2 wages: $79,400
- Self‑employment (marketing) gross: $22,100 (six clients)
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210
- Home rent: $1,720/month → $20,640/year; home 620 sq ft, office 130 sq ft (exclusive use)
- Business (self‑employment) expenses: $1,350 (unreimbursed business expenses)
- Student loan interest: see question above
- Charitable cash gift: $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity

1) Where each item goes / how it’s reported
- W‑2 wages: Form 1040, wages box (from your W‑2).
- Self‑employment income (marketing): Report gross and expenses on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Net flows to Form 1040.
- Self‑employment tax: Use Schedule SE to compute SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment income. One half of SE tax is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) and reduces AGI.
- Interest income: Report on Form 1040. Use Schedule B only if total taxable interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 or other special circumstances.
- Home‑office deduction: If this is for your self‑employed marketing business, you can claim it on Schedule C:
  - Two methods: simplified (safe-harbor) or regular.
  - Simplified = $5 × home office square footage (max 300 sq ft). For 130 sq ft that would be $650.
  - Regular method = allocate actual home expenses by percentage of home used for business. Office % = 130 / 620 ≈ 21.0%. Annual rent $20,640 × 21.0% ≈ $4,329. Under the regular method you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Regular method often gives a larger deduction here because $4,329 > $650.
  - To use the regular method, you’ll generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the allowable amount to Schedule C. Note the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business (after other business expenses).
- Charitable contribution ($1,350): Report on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, cash gifts are not separately deductible.
- Student loan interest: Above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500 max) reported on Schedule 1 (Adjustment to Income) if the interest was paid in tax year 2023 and you meet income limits. If the $4,800 you gave is interest paid over multiple years (2016–2020), only interest paid in 2023 is deductible on 2023 return.

2) Quick calculation example (using reasonable assumptions)
- Gross income: 79,400 (W‑2) + 22,100 (SE) + 1,210 (interest) = 102,710
- Assume business expenses (other) = $1,350.
- Home‑office regular allocation (rent portion) ≈ $4,329 (130/620 × $20,640).
- Net self‑employment income before SE tax = 22,100 − (1,350 + 4,329) = 16,421.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × (92.35% × 16,421) ≈ $2,322; half ($1,161) is an above‑the‑line deduction.
- If you paid student loan interest in 2023 and qualify, you can deduct up to $2,500 (subject to AGI phase‑outs).
- Interest income ($1,210) is taxable (reported on 1040).
- If you take the standard deduction for MFJ in 2023, it is $27,700.

Putting that together (approximate):
- AGI roughly = 79,400 + 16,421 + 1,210 = 97,031 (then subtract half SE tax $1,161 = 95,870; minus up to $2,500 student interest if eligible → ~93,370).
- Taxable income = AGI − standard deduction $27,700 ≈ ~65,670 (numbers rounded). This is just to show scale — I can compute a more precise estimate if you want.

3) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- For 2023 the standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700. You will generally choose itemizing only if your Schedule A deductions (charity + state & local taxes + mortgage interest + other allowable items) exceed $27,700.
- From the items you listed, the only Schedule A item is $1,350 charitable contribution. Unless you also have large property taxes/state taxes (SALT up to $10,000) or mortgage interest or other big itemized amounts, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- So likely: take standard deduction. You might itemize only if you paid significant Illinois state income tax or property tax in 2023 such that charity + SALT + other itemized amounts exceed $27,700.

4) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income / adjustments: includes student loan interest adjustment if applicable)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses including the home office deduction
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the regular method for home‑office; if you use simplified method, you just report the simplified amount on Schedule C and do not file Form 8829
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you decide to itemize (e.g., if SALT + charity etc. exceed the standard deduction)
- Schedule B only if interest + dividends > $1,500 or other reasons
- Keep and attach/retain any 1099s (1099‑MISC/1099‑NEC for contractor income if you received them, 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1099‑B etc.). You don’t attach most 1099s to the return but you use them to fill it out.

State (Illinois)
- IL‑1040 for Illinois income tax. Illinois uses federal AGI (with limited additions/subtractions) as starting point. Illinois’s rate in 2023 is a flat 4.95% (confirm your tax forms).

5) Other practical notes and recommendations
- If the $22,100 was reported on 1099‑NEC (or you received no 1099s), still report it on Schedule C.
- Keep receipts for business expenses and the Habitat donation (bank record, receipt). For the charitable cash gift, retain the charity receipt showing date and amount.
- The home‑office deduction requires exclusive, regular use for business. You said the 130 sq ft is exclusive — keep documentation (floor plan, photos, records of rent, utilities).
- If you expect to owe additional tax because of self‑employment income, consider making estimated tax payments for future years (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid penalties.
- The student loan interest deduction phases out for MFJ at higher incomes (phaseout range for 2023 is $145,000–$175,000 MAGI). If your MAGI is inside or above that range, the allowable deduction may be reduced or eliminated.

6) Next steps I can help with
- Recompute these numbers precisely using exact receipts and confirm whether the $4,800 interest was paid in 2023.
- Prepare a draft of the Schedule C and Form 8829 (or compute whether simplified vs. regular home‑office method yields a larger deduction).
- Estimate your likely federal and Illinois tax liability, including self‑employment tax and whether you should have paid estimated taxes.

If you want, answer my clarifying questions above (student‑loan interest timing; whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses; any state/property tax amounts), and I’ll run the precise numbers and list exactly which lines/forms to fill. Also, if you want professional tax‑return preparation or binding advice, I recommend consulting a CPA or enrolled agent — I can help prepare calculations and the paperwork for your tax pro.

