Thanks — I can walk through this and tell you what’s generally allowed, how the home-office works, whether itemizing will likely help, and which federal/state forms you’ll need. I’m not a CPA or enrolled agent, so consider this guidance only and consult a tax professional for anything binding or complex.

Summary up front
- You almost certainly need to file Form 1040 with Schedule C (for the marketing income) and Schedule SE (self‑employment tax) because the $22,100 from clients is business income.
- Home office deductions are allowed only if that space is used regularly and exclusively for the self‑employment work — and only for the self‑employment income (not for W‑2 work).
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (up to $2,500 in most cases) but only for interest you actually paid in the tax year and subject to MAGI phaseouts.
- Charitable cash contributions ($1,350) are deductible only if you itemize; otherwise they don’t reduce your federal tax if you take the standard deduction.
- For 2023 the married filing jointly standard deduction is $27,700. Based on the items you listed, you’re likely better off taking the standard deduction unless you have other itemizable deductions (large state/local tax or mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.).

What you can (potentially) deduct and how
1. Self‑employment income and related expenses
- Report the $22,100 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” relate to the marketing clients (supplies, advertising, travel for that work, etc.), you can deduct them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for federal returns (suspended through 2025 by TCJA).
- The net profit from Schedule C is subject to income tax and self‑employment tax. You’ll also be able to deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment on your Form 1040.

2. Home office
- Allowed only if the 130 sq ft space is used regularly and exclusively for your self‑employment marketing work.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft, up to 300 sq ft. For 130 sq ft that’s $650.
  b) Regular (actual expense) method: allocate a share of allowable home expenses to the business. Your business-use percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97% (≈21%).
     - If you rent and pay $1,720/month ($20,640/year), the deductible portion of rent would be about 21% × $20,640 ≈ $4,327.50 (plus a share of utilities if applicable).
     - Use Form 8829 to compute the deduction if you use the regular method (the amount is reported on Schedule C).
  - Note: The home office deduction cannot exceed your net self‑employment income from that business (loss‑limitation rules). If your Schedule C net profit is small, the full home‑office deduction may be limited and could create a suspended loss to carry forward.

3. Student loan interest ($4,800)
- Only interest actually paid in 2023 qualifies for the student loan interest deduction for 2023 returns. Interest paid in earlier years (2016–2020) is not deductible on your 2023 return.
- Maximum deduction is generally $2,500 (above‑the‑line), but it phases out by MAGI. For 2023 the phaseout for married filing jointly begins at $145,000 and is eliminated at $175,000 MAGI. If your joint MAGI is in that range, the deduction may be reduced or eliminated.
- If eligible, report it on Schedule 1 (adjustments to income).

4. Charitable contribution ($1,350)
- Deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this on the federal return.
- Keep your receipt (you said you have it) — good practice.

5. Interest from banks / brokerage ($920 + $290)
- Report interest and dividends on Form 1040. If total taxable interest (or ordinary dividends) exceeds $1,500 you must also file Schedule B. With $1,210 combined, Schedule B likely not required.
- If the $290 from TD Ameritrade is capital gains or brokerage proceeds, that’s reported differently (Schedule D/Form 8949). Clarify whether it’s interest/dividend or realized capital gains.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $27,700.
- Your itemizable items you noted: $1,350 charitable. Unless you have additional sizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to $10k SALT cap, large unreimbursed medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI, etc.), your itemized total looks far below $27,700. So most likely you should take the standard deduction.
- One exception: if you have large state income taxes or property taxes or mortgage interest you didn’t mention, re‑calculate with those included before deciding.

Other important tax points
- Self‑employment tax: expect to pay Social Security and Medicare on net self‑employment earnings (~15.3% on net earnings subject to the usual calculation), with one‑half deductible.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from the Schedule C activity. This is computed on Form 8995 (simplified) or 8995‑A. The deduction depends on taxable income and the nature/amount of business income.
- 1099 forms: if clients issued you 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC, include them on Schedule C even if you did not receive a 1099 for some clients (you must still report all income).
- State return: file Illinois Form IL‑1040. IL taxes start from federal AGI with some state-specific adjustments; check IL rules for deductions/credits.

Forms you’ll generally need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — for student loan interest adjustment and certain other items
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 marketing income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual expense method for home office; OR use the simplified method (report the simplified home office deduction on Schedule C)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize
- Schedule B if interest/dividends > $1,500 (probably not needed with the amounts you listed)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for Qualified Business Income deduction if eligible
- Illinois Form IL‑1040 for the state return

What I need from you to be precise
Please confirm or provide:
1. Did you actually pay $4,800 in student loan interest during 2023, or is that total interest paid across 2016–2020? (Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible on your 2023 return.)
2. Do the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” relate to your marketing/self‑employment work (Schedule C) or to your W‑2 job? If to the W‑2 job, they are not deductible federally.
3. Is the $290 from TD Ameritrade interest, dividends, or capital gains (sale proceeds)? Also, were there any other bank/brokerage interest/dividend/ capital gains in 2023?
4. Do you or your spouse have any other itemizable items (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state income tax payments, major medical expenses, etc.)?
5. Is your spouse working and did they have other income or deductions that affect MAGI (needed to determine student loan interest phaseout)?
6. Do you want me to run a rough tax estimate (calculate tentative taxable income, self‑employment tax, and whether standard deduction is better)?

Recommended next steps
- If you want a quick check: confirm the clarifying items above and I’ll run a rough calculation showing taxable income both ways (itemize vs standard) and estimate self‑employment tax and any QBI impact.
- If you prefer, engage a tax preparer/CPA who can examine your documents and make definitive choices (especially if you want to use the actual home‑office method or have carryovers/suspended losses).

