Quick summary up front
- You can (likely) deduct: business expenses for your self-employment (including a home-office share of rent), the charitable gift if you itemize, and student-loan interest only if it was paid in 2023 (and subject to limits). Interest from Chase/TD and your W‑2 wages are taxable income.
- You will almost certainly take the standard deduction (2023 MFJ = $27,700) instead of itemizing based on the numbers you gave.
- Use Schedule C + Schedule SE to report the $22,100 self-employment income. For the home office: the actual-cost method (Form 8829) appears to give a larger deduction than the simplified method in your case.
- Forms you will likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (for adjustments), Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use actual home‑office expenses), Schedule A (only if you decide to itemize), plus any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC/1099‑INT or 1098‑E you received.

Before I go into detail, two quick clarifying questions
1) The $4,800 student‑loan interest: was that interest paid in 2023, or are those totals from earlier years (2016–2020)? Only 2023 interest payments are deductible on your 2023 return.  
2) The $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses”: are those expenses for your self‑employment marketing work (i.e., business expenses), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job? (Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for 2018–2025.)

Assuming (A) the $1,350 are business expenses for your self‑employment, and (B) the $4,800 student interest was NOT paid in 2023 (I’ll cover the alternate cases below)

Income (taxable)
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self‑employment (marketing): $22,100 (report on Schedule C)
- Interest: $920 + $290 = $1,210 (taxable interest)

Total gross income = $79,400 + $22,100 + $1,210 = $102,710

Home office: two methods and which is better here
- Your home office: 130 sq ft exclusively out of 620 sq ft total = 130/620 ≈ 21.0% of home.
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
- Actual‑cost allocation of rent to office = 21.0% × $20,640 ≈ $4,328 (you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. the same way if you pay them).
- Simplified method = $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650.

If those $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses, you can deduct them on Schedule C. Compare the two approaches for your Schedule C:

1) Actual method (rent allocation + business expenses)
- Home‑office share (rent portion): ≈ $4,328
- Other business expenses: $1,350
- Total Schedule C expenses = $5,678
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $22,100 − $5,678 = $16,422

2) Simplified method
- Home‑office deduction: $650
- Other business expenses: $1,350
- Total Schedule C expenses = $2,000
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $22,100 − $2,000 = $20,100

Which is better? The actual‑cost method gives a much larger deduction here (≈ $4,328 for the home office vs $650). That lowers your self‑employment net income and reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax. So actual method (using Form 8829) appears preferable based on the numbers you provided. Keep detailed records (floor plan/measurements, rent receipts, utility bills, how you calculated the allocation).

Self‑employment tax and the “above‑the‑line” deduction
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax on net earnings from self‑employment (Schedule SE). Roughly, SE tax is 15.3% on about 92.35% of net earnings.
- Half of your SE tax is an adjustment to income (deductible on Schedule 1), which lowers AGI.

Illustrative rough comparison (estimates)
- Using actual method (net SE ≈ $16,422):
  - Net earnings for SE ≈ $16,422 × 0.9235 ≈ $15,170
  - SE tax ≈ $15,170 × 15.3% ≈ $2,321 → you deduct half (≈ $1,160) on Schedule 1
- Using simplified (net SE ≈ $20,100):
  - SE tax ≈ roughly $2,840 → half deductible ≈ $1,420
- Result: actual method lowers your taxable income more (difference in taxable income after standard deduction ≈ a few thousand dollars in your favor). These are approximate; file Schedule SE to compute exactly.

Student‑loan interest
- Only interest you actually paid in 2023 is deductible on 2023 return (up to $2,500 maximum), subject to income phaseouts. Your MAGI from the numbers you gave (~$96–100k range after the Schedule C/half‑SE adjustments) would be well under usual phaseout thresholds for MFJ, so you would likely be eligible for the full amount of interest paid in 2023 (up to $2,500). If the $4,800 figure is from 2016–2020, it is not deductible for 2023.

Charitable gift
- The $1,350 gift to Habitat for Humanity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly is $27,700 and your itemizable amounts shown are small, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction instead of itemizing.

Itemize vs standard deduction (conclusion)
- Standard deduction (MFJ 2023) = $27,700. Given only $1,350 in charitable gifts and no other large itemizable deductions listed, take the standard deduction.
- Note: the home‑office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it’s claimed on Schedule C (so using the standard deduction doesn’t prevent you from taking the home‑office deduction).

Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (federal return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax adjustment if applicable
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $22,100 and business expenses
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — business use of home (if you use the actual‑cost method)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably not)
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (if you are deducting 2023 interest)
- Form(s) 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if you received them from clients) and 1099‑INT (for the Chase & TD interest)

Other practical points and records to keep
- Keep receipts for your $1,350 charitable gift (you said you have one) and for all business expenses, rent receipts, utility bills, and the measurement of the office space. Keep client invoices and bank/1099 records to support the $22,100.
- If any clients didn’t issue 1099s, you still must report the income.
- Because you have self‑employment income, consider estimated tax payments (quarterly) for the next year if you expect similar income.
- State return: you’ll also file Illinois Form IL‑1040; rules differ by state — IL will generally tax your federal taxable income with state adjustments.

If you want, I can:
- Recompute exact AGI, SE tax, and taxable income for both the actual and simplified home‑office methods using exact rounding and show the tax impact; or
- Draft a checklist or an email to a CPA with these numbers and the questions above (e.g., clarify student‑loan interest year and whether the $1,350 is business expense); or
- Prepare a brief summary you can print and keep with your records.

Tell me whether the $4,800 was paid in 2023 and whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your marketing work, and I’ll run exact numbers and recommend the best steps to file.

