I can walk through this and give concrete next steps. I’m not a CPA or tax attorney, so consider this guidance (and the calculations below) as an informed estimate — you may want to confirm with a tax pro or use tax-prep software to prepare the return.

Quick summary / bottom line
- Based on the amounts you gave, you will almost certainly take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) rather than itemizing (your itemizable charitable gift of $1,350 is far below the standard deduction).
- You have self-employment income (the $22,100) so you must file Schedule C and Schedule SE (self-employment tax). That also lets you claim a home-office deduction and ordinary business expenses.
- The home-office deduction (actual-expense method) looks significantly more beneficial than the simplified method for your numbers.
- Student-loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (up to $2,500 subject to income limits) — whether you can take the full amount depends on when the interest was paid and your MAGI; I’ll ask a clarifying question about that below.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W-2 job) are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. If the $1,350 is a business expense for your self-employment work, it is deductible on Schedule C.

Details, calculations, and how to treat each item

1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: $79,400 — reported on Form 1040 (with withholding shown on W-2).
- Self-employment (marketing) gross receipts: $22,100 — report on Schedule C. You must also pay self-employment tax on net profit (Schedule SE).
- Interest (Chase savings): $920 and brokerage interest/dividends $290 — report on Form 1040 (supported by 1099-INT / 1099-B or 1099-DIV as applicable).

Important: always report all income even if you do not receive an information form (1099).

2) Business (self-employment) expenses
- If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses related to your self-employment work, include them on Schedule C. That reduces your net self-employment income.
- If those $1,350 are unreimbursed employee expenses for your Riverside Hospital W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on your federal return (suspended through 2025).

3) Home-office deduction (self-employed only)
Requirements (you appear to meet them):
- Exclusive, regular use of the area for your business — you said 130 sq ft exclusive, so that is satisfied.
- Principal place of business or used to meet clients — you indicated self-employment, so this is typically allowed.

Two methods — compare both:

A. Simplified method
- $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft = 130 * $5 = $650.

B. Actual-expense method (seems better here)
- Rent: $1,720/month × 12 = $20,640/year.
- Business-use percentage = 130 / 620 = 0.209677 ≈ 20.97%.
- Home-office portion of rent = $20,640 × 0.209677 ≈ $4,328.

If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, or other shared household expenses, those too can be apportioned and added under the actual method (increasing the deduction). The actual method requires Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) when you file as a sole proprietor; the simplified method does not.

Practical result: Actual method likely yields ~ $4,328 vs $650 with the simplified method — so actual saves more and reduces both income tax and self-employment tax (because it reduces net profit).

4) Self-employment tax and its deduction
- Net SE income (example): assume you deduct the $1,350 business expense first (if it is a business expense) and then deduct home office:
   Example with actual home-office:
   - Gross SE receipts: $22,100
   - Business expenses (non-home-office): $1,350
   - Home-office deduction (actual): ~$4,328
   - Net SE income ≈ 22,100 − 1,350 − 4,328 = ≈ $16,422
- Self-employment tax is computed on 92.35% of net SE income; total SE tax rate ≈ 15.3%. For the example above, SE tax ≈ $2,319 and half of that (~$1,160) is deductible above the line on Form 1040 (reduces AGI).

(If you choose the simplified home-office method, net SE income and SE tax will be larger because the home deduction is only $650.)

5) Student loan interest ($4,800 stated)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (reported on Schedule 1 of Form 1040) with a maximum deduction of $2,500 for most taxpayers, subject to MAGI phaseouts.
- Key facts / questions:
  - Only interest paid in tax year 2023 is deductible for the 2023 return. You mentioned “2016–2020” — do you mean you paid $4,800 total during those earlier years, or did you pay $4,800 in 2023? If the $4,800 was not paid in 2023, it generally cannot be deducted on your 2023 return.
  - Income limits: the deduction phases out at higher MAGI. Based on the income numbers you gave, your MAGI (after half SE tax but before the student loan interest deduction) will likely be under standard phaseout limits and you would probably be eligible for the full $2,500 — but I can compute a more precise MAGI estimate if you confirm whether $4,800 was paid in 2023.

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Your known itemizable deduction is charitable contributions $1,350. You did not list mortgage interest, large state & local taxes (SALT), or other big itemizable items. Therefore, you will very likely take the standard deduction — it’s far larger than the $1,350 charitable gift.
- Home-office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it reduces business income on Schedule C and therefore reduces AGI/taxable income whether you itemize or not.

7) Forms you will likely need (for 2023 return)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) — for student loan interest (and other adjustments)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report self-employment revenue and expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if using the actual-expense home-office method
- Form(s) 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC / 1099-K if you received them for self-employment income (report even if you did not receive)
- Form 1099-INT (interest), Form 1099-DIV / 1099-B from TD Ameritrade (report dividends/interest/stock sales)
- Form 1098-E (student loan interest) if lender provided it; you can still deduct allowable interest even if you do not receive a 1098-E, but keep your records/loan statements.

8) Other items and tax obligations
- Because you are self-employed, you may owe quarterly estimated tax payments. If you did not make sufficient estimated payments in 2023 and owe tax at filing, you could owe penalties. Consider planning estimated payments for 2024.
- Keep receipts and documentation for the home office, charitable gift (you said you have the receipt), business expenses, and student loan interest statements.

Illustrative taxable-income comparison (rough)
- Using the numbers you provided and treating the $1,350 as a business expense:
  - Gross total income ≈ $79,400 (W-2) + $22,100 (SE) + $1,210 (interest) = $102,710
  - If you use the actual home-office deduction (~$4,328) and include half SE tax and student loan interest $2,500 (assuming allowed), your AGI and taxable income are materially lower than if you don’t take the home-office deduction. The actual home-office method looks beneficial for your situation.

What I need from you (quick clarifying questions)
1. Did you pay the $4,800 in student loan interest in 2023, or was that amount paid during 2016–2020? (Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible on the 2023 return.)
2. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” for your self-employment business (marketing) or for your W‑2 job at Riverside Hospital?
3. Do you have any other potential itemizable deductions not listed (mortgage interest, large state income or property taxes paid, large medical expenses, etc.)?
4. Do you have 1099s for the $22,100 and for the brokerage/interest? (If not, that’s okay — you still report the income.)

If you’d like, I can:
- Run the numbers exactly with your answers above and show a side-by-side comparison (standard deduction vs itemizing; simplified vs actual home-office) with estimated tax and SE tax amounts.
- Draft an email to your accountant summarizing these items and asking for filing help.
