What you can deduct / how to treat each item

1) Self‑employment income and business expenses
- Report the $22,100 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).  
- If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are related to your marketing business, include them as business expenses on Schedule C. If those were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on your federal return (suspended under TCJA). Clarify this.

2) Home office
- Only available if you use the 130 sq ft space exclusively and regularly for your self‑employment business. You cannot take a home‑office deduction for a W‑2 job.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 (easy, no allocation of rent/utilities needed).
  - Actual method: prorate actual home expenses by square footage. Percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97% (≈21%). Annual rent $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,328. (You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., and deduct the business portion.)
- You likely get a larger deduction using the actual method (≈$4,328) vs simplified ($650). Keep documentation (lease, rent checks, utility bills, showing exclusive business use).

3) Self‑employment tax
