6) Interest income
- Report the $1,210 of interest on Form 1040. You generally do not need Schedule B unless total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500.

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $27,700.
- Your known potential itemized amounts are currently small: charitable $1,350 plus any state/local taxes (SALT), mortgage interest, property taxes, or other deductible amounts you didn’t list. Because you rent (no mortgage interest or property tax), and your charitable amount is only $1,350, it is very likely the standard deduction ($27,700) is larger than your itemized total.
- Conclusion: Most likely take the standard deduction and claim the student‑loan interest above the line. If you have additional itemizable items (significant state income tax withholding, large property tax, larger charitable gifts, etc.), re‑check the total; itemize only if itemized > $27,700.

Other items / credits to consider
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A): you may qualify for a 20% deduction on qualified business income from your Schedule C. With a small net business profit (example ~ $16k), you’d likely be eligible for a QBI deduction ≈ 20% × QBI (roughly $3,200 in the example). That reduces taxable income (claimed using Form 8995). Exact amount depends on final net profit and any wage/property limitations (likely not an issue at this income level).
