1) What you can (and can’t) deduct — high level
- Self‑employment business expenses: If the $22,100 is from self‑employment (you said “marketing, 6 clients”), you report that on Schedule C and can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work (supplies, advertising, phone, the business portion of home expenses, etc.).
- Home office: Deductible for self‑employed business use (see section 2). NOT deductible for employees (W‑2) under current law.
- Unreimbursed expenses: If those $1,350 are business expenses for your self‑employment, they go on Schedule C and are deductible. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible (TCJA suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions through 2025).
- Student loan interest: Only interest paid in the tax year (2023) can be deducted, up to $2,500, as an adjustment to income — and subject to MAGI phaseouts. You mentioned $4,800 paid 2016–2020 — that prior interest is NOT deductible on your 2023 return. You need Form 1098‑E (or lender statement) for interest paid in 2023.
- Charitable cash: Your $1,350 gift is deductible only if you itemize (and you have the receipt — good). Cash gifts to qualified charities are reported on Schedule A.
- Investment interest/dividends/sales (TD Ameritrade): Tax treatment depends on whether the $290 is interest, qualified dividends, or sale proceeds — each has different reporting and tax implications (1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1099‑B).
- Self‑employment tax: You will owe self‑employment tax (Social Security and Medicare) on net self‑employment income and may deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.

2) Home‑office deduction (details & quick calc)
- Your home office qualifies only if it is used regularly and exclusively for your self‑employment business (marketing) and is your principal place of business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 130 sq ft -> 130 × $5 = $650.
  b) Regular method: business % of home expenses. Your business percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97% (about 21%).
     - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640
     - Home‑office portion of rent ≈ $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,329
     - Under the regular method you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, real estate taxes (if applicable), repairs, etc., pro rata to the office.
- Which is better? Regular method looks bigger given your numbers (~$4,329 > $650), but the deduction cannot exceed the net profit from that self‑employment activity (loss limitations apply). Use Form 8829 to compute the regular‑method deduction. If you use the simplified method you don’t file Form 8829.

3) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly in 2023 is $27,700.
- Your only listed potential Schedule A items are $1,350 charity + possibly state/local taxes and mortgage interest (you didn’t list those). Even with SALT up to $10,000, you’d need more deductible items (mortgage interest, larger charitable giving, high medical expenses, etc.) to beat $27,700.
- Conclusion: Based on the information provided, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction. Note: home‑office and Schedule C business deductions reduce your AGI regardless of whether you itemize or take the standard deduction — they’re separate from Schedule A.

4) Forms you’ll need (typical set for your situation)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) — for e.g., half of SE tax, student loan interest (if eligible).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the marketing income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method to calculate business use of home
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) — you’ll likely use the simpler Form 8995 if your taxable income is well under threshold.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (you’ll likely not)
- Forms 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC — any you received for the marketing income
- Form 1099‑INT (Chase $920) and 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B from TD Ameritrade (confirm type)
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement for 2023 (if you paid interest in 2023)

5) Other items to consider
- QBI (20% pass‑through deduction): If your marketing work is a qualified trade or business, you may be eligible for up to 20% deduction of QBI (calculated from net Schedule C income after allowable deductions). With your income level it probably applies, but the exact amount depends on net profit.
- Estimated taxes / withholding: Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes if not enough tax is withheld from your W‑2 to cover total tax liability.
- Retirement/health insurance: If you contributed to a SEP IRA/SIMPLE/solo 401(k) or paid self‑employed health insurance, those reduce taxable income — consider these if applicable.
- Recordkeeping: Keep receipts for charity, 1098‑E, business expenses, and documentation proving exclusive use of the home office.

6) Quick example (illustrative only)
