What you can deduct (federal)
- Student loan interest: maximum allowed deduction is $2,500 (above‑the‑line adjustment to income). There is a MAGI phaseout for MFJ ($155k–$185k in 2023); based on the income you gave, you should be below the phaseout and eligible for the full $2,500, but the deduction cannot exceed the actual interest you paid in 2023.
- Charitable gift ($1,350 to Habitat): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You must have the receipt.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 if they are employee/unreimbursed expenses for a W‑2 job. If those $1,350 are business expenses related to your self‑employment work, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home office: deductible if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the self‑employed marketing work. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × square feet (max 300 sq ft) → 130 × $5 = $650 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Regular (actual) method: allocate a percentage of allowable home expenses to the office. Your office percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 21.0%. If you use rent, that’s 21% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330 deductible on Schedule C (plus proportional utilities if you pay them separately and can document). To use the regular method you generally file Form 8829. The actual method usually gives a larger deduction here (≈$4.3k vs $650), but it must be documented and is limited to net profit from that business (excess may carry forward).
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll pay self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Sec. 199A): you may qualify for up to 20% of qualified business income from your marketing business (reported on Form 8995 or 8995‑A) if thresholds and other rules are satisfied. This is separate from itemized vs standard deduction decisions.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your single known itemized item (charitable $1,350) is well below the standard deduction. Unless you have substantial other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to $10k SALT limit, large medical expenses above threshold, etc.), you should take the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: Most likely take the standard deduction; only itemize if your combined Schedule A items exceed $27,700.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
