Quick overview up front — then what I need from you to finish exact numbers.

Short answer
- You’ll file Form 1040 (federal) and IL-1040 (Illinois). For the self‑employment work you’ll use Schedule C (profit/loss) and Schedule SE (self‑employment tax). If you use the regular home‑office method you’ll prepare Form 8829. You may also prepare Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for the 20% Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction.
- The standard deduction for married filing jointly (2023) is $27,700. Based on the amounts you gave, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than itemized deductions (only $1,350 charitable shown), so you will very likely take the standard deduction.
- The home office deduction is available only for the self‑employment income (the $22,100 marketing income), not for W‑2 wages. Because your exclusive home office is 130 sq ft of a 620 sq ft home, the business‑use percent is 130/620 ≈ 20.97%. Using the regular method that portion of your rent (and utilities, insurance, depreciation, etc.) becomes business expense; the simplified method would be 130 sq ft × $5 = $650. With your rent of $1,720/month the regular method will almost certainly give a larger deduction (about $4,331 of rent allocated to the business for the year).
- “Unreimbursed employee expenses” for W‑2 jobs are not deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA) except in very limited categories. If your $1,350 is a self‑employment/business expense (expenses related to the marketing work) then yes — it’s deductible on Schedule C.
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in 2023 is potentially deductible (up to $2,500, subject to income limits). You’ll need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing interest paid in 2023. (You mentioned $4,800 for 2016–2020 — that does not create a current 2023 deduction.)

Details and calculations (illustrative — I made minimal assumptions; I can recalc if you confirm the points below)
Inputs you gave: W‑2 wages $79,400; self‑employment/contract marketing $22,100; interest income $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD) = $1,210 total interest.

Home office
- Business percent = 130 / 620 = 0.209677 ≈ 20.97%.
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Business share of rent ≈ $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,331.
- Regular method: you allocate business share of rent, utilities, insurance, repairs and possibly depreciation. Form 8829 computes limits if business losses exceed allowable home office deduction.
- Simplified method: $5 × 130 = $650.
Recommendation: regular method looks better given your rent, but if you don’t want to gather utility/insurance/other records simplified is easier.

Self‑employment tax and QBI
- After business expenses you’ll have a Schedule C net profit; 92.35% of that is subject to self‑employment tax (15.3% for Social Security + Medicare up to thresholds). You may deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- You’re likely eligible for the QBI deduction (20% of qualified business income) because your total taxable income is well below the QBI phaseout thresholds for 2023. QBI reduces taxable income (Form 8995) but does not reduce SE tax.

Student loan interest
- Only interest paid in 2023 qualifies. Maximum deductible amount is $2,500 (phased out at higher incomes). For MFJ the 2023 phaseout begins at MAGI ~$145,000 and ends ~$175,000. With the income numbers you gave you’d be eligible for the full $2,500 only if you actually paid at least that in 2023 and get a Form 1098‑E from the lender.

Charitable contributions
- $1,350 charitable cash gift is deductible only if you itemize. Because the standard deduction ($27,700) is much larger than the charitable amount, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have large other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state taxes up to $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, etc.).

Unreimbursed expenses
- If those $1,350 are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job they’re not deductible for 2018–2025. If they are ordinary and necessary business expenses for your marketing self‑employment, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible.

Forms you will generally need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (for certain adjustments if applicable)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $22,100, your business expenses (including home office deduction amount), and calculate net profit
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — calculates SE tax and half‑SE tax deduction
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for the home office deduction
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction) — if you want the 20% deduction for qualified business income
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) — needed to claim the student loan interest above‑the‑line deduction (you should receive it from Sallie Mae if you paid interest in 2023)
- IL‑1040 (Illinois state return) and any IL supporting schedules

What I need from you to give precise numbers and a recommended filing strategy
1. Did you actually pay $4,800 of student loan interest in 2023, or is that a total paid in 2016–2020? (Only 2023 payments are relevant now.) If you paid interest in 2023, do you have Form 1098‑E and the exact 2023 interest amount?
2. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the marketing/self‑employment work (i.e., ordinary & necessary expenses for that business), or unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job?
3. Do you have any other deductible items for 2023 you haven’t mentioned (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state/local taxes, other charitable contributions, large medical expenses)?
4. How much tax was withheld from your W‑2(s)? (This matters for whether estimated tax payments are necessary or whether you owe a balance.)
5. Do you own the home or rent? (You stated rent $1,720/month — sounds like renter; confirm whether utilities/insurance are in your name and whether you have other home expenses to allocate.)

Notes and recommendations
- Keep Form 1099‑NEC/MISC records and receipts for the marketing work and for the charitable gift. Keep rent/utility receipts for the home office allocation if you use the regular method.
- If you expect similar self‑employment income this year, consider making quarterly estimated tax payments to cover income and self‑employment taxes (Form 1040‑ES).
- If you want, I can:
  a) Recalculate estimated federal tax and self‑employment tax exactly if you confirm the points above (I’ll show AGI, taxable income, federal tax, SE tax, and estimated refund/amount due).
  b) Draft a checklist of documents to collect for filing.
  c) Prepare suggested entries to enter into tax software (or a CPA) for 2023.

Which of the clarifying items above can you confirm now? If you confirm them I’ll run the numbers and show federal tax, SE tax, and a recommendation (itemize vs standard) with exact figures.

