Short bottom line
- Most likely: take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) — it will be larger than the itemized deductions you listed.
- You can deduct (on your federal return): allowable student loan interest (subject to limits), your self-employment business expenses (including a home-office deduction if those expenses are for your marketing business), interest/dividend income, and pay self-employment tax (Schedule SE). Charitable donations are only deductible if you itemize — they don’t beat the standard deduction here.
- You should report your self-employment income on Schedule C and pay self-employment tax; that also allows the home-office deduction and business expense deductions.

A. Which items are deductible and how
1. W-2 wages ($79,400)
- Report on Form 1040 via your W-2. No special deduction here beyond any itemized or standard deduction and payroll tax withholdings.

2. Self-employment income ($22,100 — marketing, 6 clients)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (examples: advertising, supplies, software, part of phone/internet used for business, and the home-office deduction if that space is used exclusively and regularly for the business).
- The $1,350 you listed as “unreimbursed expenses”: if these are expenses related to your self-employment (marketing) they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed expenses as a W-2 employee, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 on your federal return.
- Self-employment tax (Schedule SE) applies to net self-employment income; you get an above-the-line deduction for half of the self‑employment tax.

3. Interest / investment income (Chase $920; TD Ameritrade $290)
- Report interest on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if your total taxable interest and dividends exceed $1,500). Combined here (920 + 290 = $1,210) is below the Schedule B threshold, so just report on Form 1040.
- If the $290 is actually dividends or capital gains, that affects reporting (1099-DIV or 1099-B). Tell me what the TD Ameritrade form shows if you want exact handling.

4. Student loan interest ($4,800 listed)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction limited to $2,500 per return for 2023 (and it phases out at higher MAGI).
- You will need Form 1098-E from Sallie Mae showing interest paid during 2023. Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible for 2023. So if your 1098-E shows $4,800 paid in 2023, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts).
- Phaseout for Married Filing Jointly in 2023 begins at MAGI $145,000 and fully phases out at $175,000. Based on the income numbers you gave, you’re likely under the phaseout and can take the full $2,500, but I can check more precisely with final numbers.

5. Charitable contribution ($1,350 to Habitat for Humanity)
- Charitable gifts are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you cannot separately deduct this.
- Keep the receipt you mentioned (required for cash gifts of $250+).

B. Home office (130 sq ft exclusive use; home = 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/mo)
You can take a home-office deduction only if you are self-employed (Schedule C) and the space is used regularly and exclusively for business.

Two methods:
1) Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
- Your deduction = 130 sq ft * $5 = $650 (no need to allocate rent/utility bills; easier, fewer forms).

2) Regular (actual) method: allocate a share of actual home expenses to the business.
- Business percentage = 130 / 620 = 0.20968 ≈ 20.97% (approx. 21.0%).
- Annual rent = $1,720 * 12 = $20,640.
- Rent portion allocable to home-office = $20,640 * 0.20968 ≈ $4,330.
- You could also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, and other allowable home expenses; those would increase the deduction. You must use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C for the actual method.

Which to pick?
- The actual method will often be larger when rent + utilities * business percentage exceed the simplified amount. Based on rent alone, actual method rent share (~$4,330) is much larger than the simplified $650 — so the actual method seems favorable.
- However, the actual method requires more recordkeeping and Form 8829; it will reduce your Schedule C net profit (and thus self-employment tax and taxable income).
- Note: home-office deduction is not a Schedule A (itemized) deduction — it lowers your business income on Schedule C.

C. Itemize vs. standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your only listed potential Schedule A itemized amount is the $1,350 charitable gift (you didn’t list mortgage interest, large state/property taxes, or significant medical expenses). So your likely total itemized deductions are much less than $27,700.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction. (Even with the home-office deduction, that reduces your Schedule C net profit but does not increase Schedule A itemized deductions, so it does not change the comparison for itemize vs standard.)

D. Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like the student loan interest deduction and half of self-employment tax (depending on software/layout).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses (including home office).
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deduction for half of that tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for home-office deduction. If you use simplified, you don’t file Form 8829.
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20%) if your business qualifies. For your income level this is likely applicable; the simple form is Form 8995.
- Form(s) 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if you received any) and W-2(s).
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement from Sallie Mae (to support the student-loan interest deduction).
- Form(s) 1099-INT and 1099-DIV / 1099-B from TD Ameritrade and Chase.

State: Illinois
- You’ll also file Illinois Form IL-1040 (state return). Illinois has a flat income tax; deductions and treatment differ from federal — but everything you report on your federal 1040 serves as the starting point for IL. If you want, I can outline the IL specifics once federal numbers are set.

E. Other items and possible additional tax considerations
- Self-employment tax (Social Security and Medicare) will apply to net self-employment earnings.
- You may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income) — that further reduces taxable income. Form 8995 is used for most filers with incomes under the phase-out.
- Keep records: receipts for business expenses, receipt for charity, Form 1098-E, rent/utility bills for home-office allocation.
- Estimated tax / underpayment: if you haven’t had enough withholding to cover self-employment taxes + income tax, you may owe tax/penalties or need to pay estimated tax for the current year. Check for underpayment.

F. What I need from you to finalize a precise calculation / filing plan
1. Confirm whether the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses related to the $22,100 self-employment income (Schedule C) or unreimbursed W-2 employee expenses (not deductible).
2. Confirm whether the $290 from TD Ameritrade is interest, dividends, or proceeds from sales (i.e., what 1099 you received).
3. Confirm how much student loan interest was actually paid in 2023 and whether you have a 2023 Form 1098-E (the $4,800 amount: is that interest paid in 2023 or something else?).
4. Any other possible itemizable deductions you didn’t list (mortgage interest, state income taxes or property tax > $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses, casualty losses, etc.)
5. Do you want me to run a quick rough tax estimate (federal tax, self-employment tax, and net refund/amount due) using the actual-home-office rent allocation and using the simplified method so you can see which net result is better?

