Important preliminary question (please confirm)
- The $4,800 student‑loan interest — was that interest paid in 2023 (on a Form 1098‑E you received for 2023)? Only interest paid in the tax year you’re filing (2023) is potentially deductible for your 2023 return. If those payments were in prior years, they aren’t deductible on the 2023 return.

Summary of your income (from your message)
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self‑employment / freelance marketing income: $22,100
- Interest from Chase savings: $920
- Interest (or other brokerage income) from TD Ameritrade: $290
- Total interest reported here: $1,210

What you can (generally) deduct and how
1) Student‑loan interest
- Maximum deduction: $2,500 (above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1) per return for 2023.
- If your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) is within the phase‑out range you might lose some or all of it. Based on the income numbers you provided (see estimates below), you’re well below typical phaseout thresholds, so you would likely be able to take the full $2,500 — provided the $4,800 was paid in 2023. If you only paid part of that in 2023, only the 2023 payments count (and the max allowed remains $2,500).

2) Charitable donation ($1,350 to Habitat for Humanity)
- Charitable cash gifts are deductible only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you do not separately deduct charitable gifts (except for any special 2020/2021 pandemic rules that no longer apply).
- You have a receipt, which is good — needed when itemizing.

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are business expenses related to your freelance/marketing work (e.g., supplies, advertising, travel for clients), they belong on Schedule C as ordinary business expenses and reduce your self‑employment income.
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 due to TCJA.

4) Home‑office (130 sq ft exclusive; home is 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/month)
- You are self‑employed, so you can deduct home‑office expenses on Schedule C either by:
  a) Actual‑expense method (you deduct your business percentage of rent, utilities, etc.) — computed on Form 8829 and carried to Schedule C; or
  b) Simplified method ($5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft).
- Your business percentage = 130 / 620 = 20.9677% ≈ 21%.

Two quick comparisons (to help choose method)
- Actual method (rent only shown):
  - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
  - Home portion of rent = 20.9677% × $20,640 ≈ $4,328.
  - If you have utility/internet amounts to share, add the business percentage of those as well — that will increase the actual method benefit.
- Simplified method:
  - 130 sq ft × $5 = $650.

Because $4,328 (actual‑method rent portion) > $650 (simplified), the actual method is likely better for you — especially if you have any additional allocable utilities or renter’s insurance to include. Use Form 8829 when using the actual method.

Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Your freelance income is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of net SE income). You compute SE tax on Schedule SE.
- You can deduct half of the SE tax as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1 (this reduces AGI).
- Business expenses (home office actual portion, unreimbursed business expenses) reduce net Schedule C profit and thereby reduce both income tax and SE tax.

Estimate (based on the numbers you provided; rounded)
I’ll show two scenarios: actual home‑office method (using only rent) vs simplified.

A) Using actual home office method (rent portion $4,328) and unreimbursed business expenses $1,350:
- Business expenses total = $4,328 + $1,350 = $5,678
- Net self‑employment profit = $22,100 − $5,678 = $16,422
- Net SE earnings (for SE tax) = 16,422 × 0.9235 ≈ 15,168
- SE tax ≈ 15,168 × 0.153 ≈ $2,321 → half SE tax deductible ≈ $1,160
- Gross income before adjustments = 79,400 (W‑2) + 16,422 (net SE) + 1,210 (interest) = $97,032
- Subtract half SE tax ($1,160) and student loan interest (max allowed $2,500) = AGI ≈ $93,372
- Standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly, 2023) = $27,700
- Taxable income ≈ $93,372 − $27,700 = $65,672

B) Using simplified home office ($650) and same unreimbursed $1,350:
- Business expenses = $650 + $1,350 = $2,000
- Net SE profit = $22,100 − $2,000 = $20,100
- Net SE earnings = 20,100 × 0.9235 ≈ 18,568
- SE tax ≈ 18,568 × 0.153 ≈ $2,841 → half SE tax ≈ $1,421
- Gross income before adjustments = 79,400 + 20,100 + 1,210 = $100,710
- Subtract half SE tax ($1,421) and student loan interest $2,500 = AGI ≈ $96,789
- Taxable income ≈ $96,789 − $27,700 = $69,089

Conclusion from these estimates
- The actual method (with the rent allocation) gives you a lower taxable income in this example (by about $3,400), so the actual method is likely preferable.
- Your charitable gift ($1,350) is smaller than the gap between the standard deduction and what you might itemize, so you’ll probably take the standard deduction ($27,700 for MFJ) unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state/local taxes up to $10K SALT cap, large medical expenses, etc.). Based only on numbers you gave, standard deduction is almost certainly better.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for reporting student loan interest deduction (adjustment) and possibly other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses (home office, unreimbursed business costs)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to calculate SE tax on the Schedule C net profit
