Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether you should itemize, how the home-office works, and which forms you’ll need. I’ll also call out a few things I need to know to give more precise numbers.

Quick summary / bottom line
- Most likely you should take the standard deduction for 2023 (Married Filing Jointly = $27,700) unless you have other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, bigger charitable gifts, large medical expenses) you haven’t listed.
- Your $22,100 marketing income looks like self‑employment income — that is reported on Schedule C and is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE). If those “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for that income, you can deduct them on Schedule C.
- Home office: If the marketing income is self‑employment income you can claim a home‑office deduction. Using the actual method (pro rata of rent/utilities) looks more favorable than the simplified method for the numbers you gave, but the deduction can’t exceed the profit from the business.
- Student loan interest: only up to $2,500 of student loan interest is deductible as an adjustment to income (above-the-line), subject to income phaseouts. You cannot deduct more than $2,500 even if you paid $4,800 in 2023.
- Charitable gift of $1,350 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because that’s relatively small compared with the standard deduction, itemizing probably won’t beat $27,700.

Detailed guidance and calculations

1) Income you must report
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 — reported on Form 1040 via W-2.
- Self‑employment/marketing: $22,100 — report on Schedule C (profit or loss) and Schedule SE (self‑employment tax).
- Interest: $920 (Chase) and $290 (TD Ameritrade) — report on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INT / 1099‑B from the broker).

2) Business expenses and “unreimbursed expenses”
- If the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your marketing work (supplies, advertising, mileage, etc.), include them on Schedule C — they will reduce your net business income and reduce SE tax.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (i.e., you were an employee and your employer didn’t reimburse job expenses), those are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 under current law.

3) Home‑office deduction (you said: office 130 sq ft exclusive; total 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/month)
- Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction.
- Actual method: business-use percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97%. Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Pro rata of rent = $20,640 × 0.2097 ≈ $4,328. You could also include a pro rata share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if they’re paid separately. If you use the actual method and have other direct home office expenses, complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if self‑employed; the result is transferred to Schedule C.
- The home‑office deduction can’t exceed net profit from the business. If your Schedule C shows a loss, special excess deduction rules apply (and passive loss limitations do not apply — business use of home is not treated as a rental).
- For a sole proprietor, the home‑office deduction reduces Schedule C net income and therefore reduces both income and SE tax.

4) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- SE tax is roughly 15.3% on ~92.35% of net self‑employment income. You get to deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), which lowers AGI.
- Example rough estimate (illustrative only): if you have $22,100 gross and $1,350 business expenses, then before home office net = $20,750. If you take the actual home office ≈ $4,328, net ≈ $16,422. SE tax on that would be ~ $2,300 (approx.) and half (~$1,150) is an adjustment.

5) Student loan interest ($4,800 you reported)
- The student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 per return for 2023 (and phases out at higher MAGI). You can only deduct interest actually paid in 2023 and only up to the $2,500 cap (and subject to MAGI/phaseout). The lender should issue Form 1098‑E showing interest paid; keep that and your statements.
- Because the deduction is an adjustment (on Schedule 1), it reduces your AGI whether or not you itemize.

6) Charitable contribution ($1,350)
- Cash gift to Habitat for Humanity is deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. For any single contribution of $250 or more you must have a written acknowledgment from the charity showing the amount and date and whether you received goods or services. Keep your receipt.

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
- For MFJ in 2023 the standard deduction is $27,700. Based on the items you listed:
  - Charitable: $1,350
  - No mortgage interest listed
  - No (or unknown) large SALT (state & local taxes) beyond the $10k cap
  => It’s very likely standard deduction is larger than your itemized total, so you should take the standard deduction.
- Note: student loan interest and half of SE tax are adjustments to income and do not affect whether you itemize.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (to report adjustments to income: student loan interest deduction, deductible part of self‑employment tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses including the $1,350 if they are business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual method for the home office; if you use the simplified method, do not file Form 8829
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — used to support your student loan interest deduction (the lender provides it)
- 1099‑INT / 1099‑B or broker statements for the bank/broker interest ($920 and $290)
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital

9) Other items and recommendations
- Get Form 1098‑E from Sallie Mae for interest paid in 2023 and 1099s from any payers (1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC if you received them).
- Keep the Habitat for Humanity written acknowledgment (required for gifts ≥ $250).
- If you have not been making estimated tax payments for your self‑employment income, consider using Form 1040‑ES to make estimated tax payments to avoid penalties (income tax + SE tax).
- Consider retirement contributions (SEP‑IRA or Solo 401(k)) if you are self‑employed — these can lower taxable/self‑employment income and reduce tax.
- If you want precise dollar amounts (final AGI, taxable income, tax liability, estimated SE tax), I can run through a detailed worksheet — but I’ll need:
  - Confirmation that the $22,100 is self‑employment (1099) income
  - Whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business-related (and what they are)
  - Whether the $4,800 student loan interest was paid in 2023 (or is a total of prior years)
  - Any other deductions you have (property tax, state tax paid, mortgage interest, large medical expenses)
  - Whether the $1,720 rent includes utilities or you pay utilities separately

Caveat
- This is general guidance (not a substitute for personalized tax advice). For complex situations, large amounts, or to prepare and file returns, consider consulting a CPA or tax preparer.

Would you like me to:
- run an illustrative calculation (with the assumptions above) showing estimated AGI, taxable income and approximate federal tax and SE tax; or
- draft a checklist of documents to gather so you (or your preparer) can file; or
- help you locate and organize the 1099 / 1098 forms in your email?

