Quick conclusion (based on the facts you gave)
- You’re almost certainly better off taking the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) because the itemizable deduction amounts you listed are small.
- You can deduct the student loan interest (subject to limits and income phaseouts) and claim business-related deductions (home office and unreimbursed business expenses) on your Schedule C if that “additional income” is self‑employment income.
- Home office: you can use either the simplified method (130 sq ft × $5 = $650) or the actual-expense method (about $4,330 of your annual rent, see calculation below) — the actual method gives a larger deduction in your numbers.
- Required tax forms include: Form 1040, Schedule C (business income/expenses), Schedule SE (self-employment tax), Schedule 1 (adjustments, e.g., student loan interest), Form 8829 if you use the actual home‑office method, and Schedule A only if you itemize (and Schedule B only if interest > $1,500). Also keep W-2, 1099s, and Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) / 1099‑INT (interest).

Details and calculations

1) Income summary (you reported)
- W-2 wages: $79,400
- Self‑employment/marketing income: $22,100
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210
- Total gross (before expenses/adjustments): $102,710

2) Home-office deduction options
- You said the home office is 130 sq ft exclusive and the whole unit is 620 sq ft.
  - Business-use percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 0.2097 (about 21.0%).
- You pay $1,720/month rent → annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
  - Actual-expense home-office portion of rent ≈ 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330.
- Simplified method: 130 sq ft × $5 = $650.
- Which to choose: actual-expense method (≈ $4,330) yields a larger business expense in your situation, so it’s likely better. Use Form 8829 (or follow Schedule C instructions) for actual; simplified needs no Form 8829 (you still report the deduction on Schedule C).

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are business expenses related to your self‑employment work (marketing, supplies, travel for clients, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (you were an employee and employer didn’t reimburse), those are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800 noted)
- The student loan interest deduction is an “above-the-line” deduction (you claim it on Schedule 1) up to $2,500 per return in most cases, subject to income phaseouts.
- It only covers interest paid (not principal) in the tax year. Check whether the $4,800 is interest paid in 2023; if so you may be able to deduct up to $2,500 (reduced if your MAGI is above the phaseout thresholds).
- You should get Form 1098‑E from Sallie Mae if you paid $600+ interest in 2023; keep records if not.

5) Self-employment tax
- Net self‑employment income (after business deductions including home office and the $1,350 business expenses) will be subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) via Schedule SE (approx. 15.3% on net earnings, with 92.35% of net used to compute the tax).
- You can deduct half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

6) Rough example (using actual home-office method and treating $1,350 as business expense)
- Self‑employment gross: $22,100
- Less home-office (actual): ≈ $4,330
- Less other business expenses: $1,350
- Net self‑employment income ≈ $22,100 − $4,330 − $1,350 = $16,420
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 0.9235 × $16,420 ≈ $2,320; half deductible ≈ $1,160
- Adjusted gross income (rough):
  - W-2 + interest + net self‑employment = $79,400 + $1,210 + $16,420 = $97,030
  - Less half SE tax (~$1,160) and student loan interest (up to $2,500) = $97,030 − $1,160 − $2,500 = ≈ $93,370
- Standard deduction (MFJ) = $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $65,670 (very approximate)
- If you instead use the simplified home-office ($650), your net business income would be higher by ~$3,680 and SE tax would increase somewhat — but you’d still almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for itemizing.

7) Itemize vs standard
- Itemized deductions you listed: charitable $1,350. (Student loan interest is an above-the-line adjustment, not an itemized deduction; home-office and business expenses are Schedule C deductions that reduce self-employment income.)
- With only $1,350 of charitable gifts and no mortgage interest, state/local taxes, or large medical expenses listed, the standard deduction ($27,700) will be larger than your likely total itemized deductions. So take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, high state income/property taxes, big medical expenses, larger charitable giving, etc.).

8) Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments: student loan interest deduction, etc.)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your $22,100 and business expenses (including home office)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual‑expense method for the home office
- Schedule A only if you itemize (probably not in your case)
- Schedule B only if your interest/dividend income exceeds $1,500 (your interest is $1,210, so not required)
- Keep these informational forms: W-2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients, 1099‑INTs, 1098‑E for student loan interest

9) Practical next steps / questions for you (so I can be more precise)
- Was the $4,800 student loan interest paid during 2023? Did you receive a 1098‑E for 2023?
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your marketing/self‑employment work? (If so, they go on Schedule C.)
- Does your spouse have income to report? Any other deductions (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state taxes, large medical expenses, more charitable gifts)?
- Are you renting (I assumed yes from the $1,720 monthly) or do you own the home? (That changes what actual expenses look like — depreciation is available for owners.)

