User: I want to file my 2023 taxes correctly. I'm married filing jointly in Chicago IL. W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400. Additional income: $22,100 (marketing, 6 clients), $920 from Chase savings, $290 from TD Ameritrade. Home office: 130 sq ft exclusive; I pay $1,720/month for 620 sq ft. Deductions: $4,800 student loan interest (Sallie Mae, 2016–2020); $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity I have the receipt; $1,350 unreimbursed expenses. Can you tell me what I can deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and what forms I need?
Assistant: I can walk through this and give concrete, practical guidance — but I’m not a CPA or enrolled agent, so consider this general tax guidance and, if your situation is important/complex, confirm with a tax pro.

Summary answers up front
- Likely best to take the standard deduction (married filing jointly = $27,700 for 2023) unless you have substantial other itemizable deductions (state/local tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses). Based on the items you listed, itemizing probably won’t beat the standard deduction.
- Student loan interest: you can deduct up to $2,500 (above-the-line) for 2023 if you actually paid that interest in 2023 and your MAGI is below the phaseout. Based on the income numbers you gave, you should be eligible for the full $2,500 — but I need one clarification (see questions below).
- Charitable gift: your $1,350 donation to Habitat for Humanity is deductible if you itemize and have the receipt. It does not affect AGI (it’s an itemized deduction).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $1,350) are NOT deductible on Schedule A for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension) — unless those expenses are business expenses of a self-employed trade (see below).
- Home office: deductible only if you are self-employed (or a partner / certain business owners). Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft → 130 sq ft × $5 = $650.
  - Regular: pro rata share of allowable home expenses. Based on your numbers: business % = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97%. Rent $1,720/mo × 12 = $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,330 (plus allocable utilities if you pay them and can document them). For a renter with the numbers you gave the regular method is likely larger than the simplified method.
  - Home-office deduction for self-employed is reported on Schedule C (or Form 8829 for the regular method). It cannot be taken if you are an employee (W-2) with unreimbursed expenses (unless very rare exceptions apply).
- Forms you’ll likely need:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - W-2 from Riverside Hospital
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $22,100 marketing income and business expenses (including home office, if self-employed)
  - Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax on your net self-employment income
  - Form 8829 — if you use the regular method to compute business use of home (otherwise use the simplified method and just enter amount on Schedule C)
  - Form 8995 (qualified business income deduction) or 8995-A — if you want to claim the QBI 20% deduction from your net self-employment income (you will likely qualify for a simple 20% QBI deduction at your income level)
  - Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income) — student loan interest deduction and half of self-employment tax are reported here
  - 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if you received them for the self-employment income)
  - 1099-INT (Chase) and 1099 (TD Ameritrade) for interest/dividends/capital gains (report that income on 1040)
  - Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement (if available) to support the student loan interest deduction
  - Keep receipts for charitable contributions and business expenses

Detailed walk-through and example calculation (approximate)
1) Income
- W-2 wages: $79,400
- Self-employment/marketing income: $22,100
- Interest/dividends: $920 + $290 = $1,210
- Total gross income ≈ $102,710

2) Self-employment items (if marketing income is from self-employment)
