- Example net profit (regular method): $22,100 − $5,680 ≈ $16,420

3) Self-employment tax and related adjustment
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (92.35% × net profit) → roughly $2,300 (ballpark)
- Half of SE tax (~$1,150) is an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1

4) Student loan interest
- If you actually paid interest in 2023 and are eligible, you can deduct up to $2,500 (above-the-line). You said $4,800 — if that is interest paid in 2023 the deduction still caps at $2,500 for the year. If $4,800 was paid in earlier years, only 2023 payments matter.

5) QBI deduction
- If you have QBI from the Schedule C, you likely can take a 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995). For example, 20% × net profit (~$16,420) ≈ $3,284 (subject to limits, but at your income level the simple 20% generally applies).

6) Standard vs itemize
- Standard deduction for MFJ (2023) = $27,700.
- Itemized items you listed: charitable $1,350. Student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (not an itemized deduction). Without other sizable itemizable items (SALT up to $10,000, mortgage interest, large medical expenses), itemizing will be far less than $27,700. So you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction.

Key limitations and rules to watch
- Student loan interest deduction: limited to $2,500; phaseout for MFJ if MAGI is high (phaseout starts well above your reported income).
- Home office: must be regular and exclusive use for business; available only to self-employed or certain other business owners; employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers in 2018–2025.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: NOT deductible on Schedule A for 2018–2025 (unless you are self-employed and report them on Schedule C).
- Home office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business (excess may carry forward for depreciation cases).
- Keep receipts, a log of business use (dates/hours if relevant), and documentation for donations.

