What you can deduct (general rules)
- Self-employment business expenses: deductible on Schedule C. That includes ordinary and necessary expenses for your marketing work (e.g., the $1,350 if they are business expenses).  
- Home office (self-employed): deductible on Schedule C if you meet the exclusive-and-regular-use rule. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft (130 sq ft → $650).  
  - Regular: proportionate share of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) — you provided rent only, so the rent-based share would be (130 / 620) × annual rent.
- Self-employment tax: you pay self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment income; half of SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: maximum deduction is $2,500 (above-the-line) for qualified interest paid in the tax year; phased out at higher MAGI ranges. If your 2023 MAGI is ~ $100k, you should be under the phase-out for MFJ and eligible for the full $2,500 (if you actually paid that much in 2023).
- Charitable donation ($1,350): deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. Under current law (through 2025) the standard deduction often exceeds typical itemized totals for many taxpayers.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally not deductible for employees (suspended through 2025) — again, only deductible if they are business expenses for self-employment.

Quick comparison: itemize vs. standard
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.  
