I can walk you through how your 2023 return will generally work, what is (and is not) deductible, whether itemizing makes sense, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag a few things I need you to confirm so I can run more precise numbers.

Important high-level points first
- I’m not a tax preparer — this is general guidance. For a final return or complicated situations, consider a CPA or tax preparer.
- Self-employment income and employee (W-2) income are treated differently for deductions. That matters for your “unreimbursed expenses” and home-office deduction.
- Student-loan interest and charitable deductions have limits and timing rules — the year the interest was paid matters.

Summary of your numbers (as you provided)
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self-employment/marketing gross: $22,100 (assumed 1099/self-employed)
- Interest from Chase: $920
- TD Ameritrade: $290
Total gross income = $79,400 + $22,100 + $920 + $290 = $102,710

What you can (probably) deduct and how to report it
1) Self-employment income (Schedule C)
- Report the $22,100 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the marketing work (not unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job), they belong on Schedule C and reduce your self-employment net income.
- If the $1,350 were unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job, they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act).

2) Home office (only if you are self-employed and the space is exclusive and regular business use)
You have two methods:
- Simplified method: $5 × square feet used (max 300 sq ft). For 130 sq ft that’s 130 × $5 = $650. No Form 8829 required.
- Regular (actual) method: use the business percentage of actual home expenses. You said the apartment is 620 sq ft and home-office is 130 sq ft → business-use percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97% (~21%).
  - Rent: $1,720/month = $20,640/year. Business portion of rent = 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,328.
  - You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., by that percentage.
  - If using the actual method for a sole proprietor, you normally use Form 8829 to compute the allowable deduction.
Which to choose: if you only have rent and no large other allocable home expenses, actual method here (~$4,328) looks larger than simplified ($650), so actual likely gives a bigger deduction — but confirm you want to allocate utilities, internet portion, etc. Note: depreciation is applicable if you own; since you rent, depreciation is not relevant.

3) Self-Employment Tax and deduction for half of it
- After expenses, net profit from self-employment is subject to self-employment (SE) tax (Social Security + Medicare ~15.3% on 92.35% of net).
- You can deduct one-half of the SE tax as an “above-the-line” adjustment on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

Example estimate (useful to show magnitude)
Assume:
- Business gross = $22,100
- Business expenses = home-office (actual) ~$4,328 + $1,350 business expenses = $5,678
Net business income ≈ $22,100 − $5,678 = $16,422

SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 16,422 ≈ $2,322
Half of SE tax deduction ≈ $1,161 (above-the-line)

4) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (Form 1040 Schedule 1), max $2,500 for the tax year, subject to income phaseouts and only for interest paid in the tax year (2023). The years you listed (2016–2020) suggest that interest paid in earlier years is not deductible on your 2023 return.
- If you actually paid $4,800 of student loan interest in 2023, you could still only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to phaseouts).
- Phaseouts apply by MAGI; I’ll need to confirm whether your income exceeds the phaseout range for MFJ in 2023 before assuming full deduction.

5) Charitable contributions
- $1,350 charitable gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you don’t deduct these on 2023 returns (there was no non-itemizer above-the-line special deduction in 2023 except certain years had one-off rules for 2020).
- You have a receipt — good, keep it for substantiation.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- If they are related to your W-2 job → not deductible for 2018–2025.
- If they are business expenses for your self-employed marketing work → deductible on Schedule C (as discussed above).

Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (MFJ): $27,700.
- Likely itemizable amounts you’ve listed: charitable $1,350 + state/local taxes (SALT) up to $10,000 + mortgage interest or property taxes if any (you didn’t list mortgage interest or property taxes).
- Illinois state income tax (flat 4.95%) may generate a SALT deduction, but unless you paid a large amount of state and local taxes (or have substantial mortgage interest/property tax), your itemized total is likely far less than $27,700.
- Based on the amounts you gave (charity $1,350 + likely IL tax < ~$5,000 depending on withholdings), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction. So standard deduction is probably the right choice, unless you have additional itemizable items (large mortgage interest, big property taxes, large unreimbursed medical expenses exceeding threshold, or substantially more charitable donations).

What forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for the student loan interest adjustment (if eligible) and the deduction for half of SE tax
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses (including home-office expense if self-employed)
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — only if you use the actual (regular) method to claim home-office expenses for a self-employed business; not required for the simplified method
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (I expect you will not)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if you received them), W-2 (Riverside Hospital), 1099-INT (Chase), 1099-DIV or 1099-INT or 1099-B from TD Ameritrade as applicable — collect all these.
- Keep receipts for charitable contributions and documentation for student loan interest (Form 1098-E for student loan interest paid in 2023) — the lender typically issues Form 1098-E.

Other items to consider / action items for you
- Clarify whether the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for your marketing work (Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses from your hospital job (not deductible).
- Confirm whether the $4,800 student loan interest was paid in 2023 or if that amount is the total paid across 2016–2020. Only interest paid in 2023 can be deducted on your 2023 return, and it is capped at $2,500 (subject to income phaseout).
- Tell me whether you own or rent (you said you pay $1,720/month for 620 sq ft — that sounds like rent; confirm).
- Provide how much state tax (Illinois) was withheld or paid in 2023 (from W-2 or estimated payments). That affects itemizing and state tax credit/deduction.
- If you want, I can run a more detailed estimate of:
  - AGI, taxable income, federal tax + self-employment tax
  - Whether you’ll owe federal estimated taxes (or need to increase withholding)
  - A comparison of standard deduction vs itemizing given any additional items you might have

Quick next-step recommendation
- If the unreimbursed $1,350 are business expenses and the home office is used exclusively for your marketing business, plan to file Schedule C and Schedule SE, claim the home-office deduction (actual method seems larger than simplified), take the standard deduction on Schedule A (i.e., do not itemize unless you have more itemizable amounts), and claim up to $2,500 student loan interest on Schedule 1 only if that interest was paid in 2023 and your MAGI isn’t high enough to phase it out.
- Gather: W-2, all 1099s, Form 1098-E (student loan interest paid in 2023), receipts for charitable contributions, documentation of business expenses and rent/utilities for the home office.

If you want, I can:
- Use your clarifications to produce a line-by-line sample calculation (AGI, taxable income, estimated federal tax and self-employment tax) and show the standard vs itemized comparison.
- Or list exactly what to upload to a preparer or tax software.

Which would you like me to do next? (If you want estimates, please confirm: whether the $1,350 expenses are business-related, whether you paid $4,800 of student loan interest in 2023 or earlier years, and whether you rent — and please provide state tax withheld if you know it.)

