Thanks — I can walk you through this and tell you what’s normally deductible, what forms you’ll need, and whether itemizing likely helps. I’m not a CPA, so treat this as general guidance and consider confirming with a tax preparer for anything you’ll file.

Quick summary of the numbers you gave
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self-employment (marketing, 6 clients): $22,100
- Interest: Chase savings $920; TD Ameritrade $290
- Total gross income (before adjustments): $102,710

You told me:
- Home office: 130 sq ft exclusive use; residence is 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/month ($20,640/yr)
- Student loan interest: $4,800 (you noted “Sallie Mae, 2016–2020”)
- Charitable cash gift to Habitat: $1,350 (you have a receipt)
- Unreimbursed expenses: $1,350 (unclear whether these are business expenses for your marketing work or unreimbursed employee expenses for the W-2 job)

Key points and what’s deductible for 2023

1) Self-employment income (your $22,100)
- Report on Schedule C (Form 1040). Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (advertising, supplies, mileage, unreimbursed business expenses if they relate to this business, etc.).
- Self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) is calculated on Schedule SE. You’ll owe SE tax on net self-employment earnings (after Schedule C expenses). You can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

2) Home-office deduction
- Only available if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your trade or business (self-employed marketing qualifies if you meet the rules; it does NOT apply if the space is for your W-2 job unless you are a qualifying statutory employee — most employees cannot claim it).
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 130 sq ft that’s 130 × $5 = $650.
  - Regular (actual) method: allocate actual home expenses by the business-use percentage (business square footage ÷ total home square footage). Your percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97%. Annual rent $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,328 would be the rent portion attributable to the office; you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., proportionally. You would report this on Form 8829 (if using actual method).
- The home-office deduction reduces your Schedule C net income. If your Schedule C net profit is small, the deduction cannot create or increase a business loss beyond rules that limit it.

3) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- For 2023, the maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (above-the-line adjustment on Schedule 1), and it only applies to interest you actually paid in 2023. If your $4,800 was paid during 2016–2020, that’s not deductible on your 2023 return.
- There is an income phaseout for married filing jointly in 2023: the deduction phases out for MAGI between $145,000 and $175,000; above $175,000 you cannot take it. So whether you can take up to $2,500 also depends on your 2023 MAGI.

4) Charitable contribution ($1,350)
- Cash gift to a qualified charity like Habitat for Humanity is deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). You must itemize to take this.
- If you take the standard deduction, this donation won’t separately reduce your federal tax (unless special provisions apply for a specific year; for 2023 there is no universal above-the-line charitable deduction for individuals).

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these expenses are business expenses for your self-employment work, deduct them on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job (e.g., travel, uniforms, tools) they are not deductible on your federal return for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA) unless you are in a narrow category (e.g., certain performing artists, armed forces reservists, etc.). Clarify whether these are self-employment expenses.

6) Interest income ($920 + $290)
- These are taxable interest and reported on Form 1040. You should receive 1099-INTs from the banks/broker; include them even if you don’t get a 1099.

Standard deduction vs itemizing
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your likely itemizable deductions (from what you provided): charitable $1,350 + possibly state/local taxes (SALT) up to $10,000 if you paid state/local income or property taxes — you didn’t list SALT amounts. Student loan interest is an adjustment to income (not an itemized deduction).
- Given the $1,350 charitable amount and no big mortgage interest or large SALT, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction ($27,700) rather than itemizing. Exception: if you have large state taxes, medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, mortgage interest, or other significant itemized items, itemizing could be better. Based on the numbers you gave, plan to take the standard deduction.

Forms you’ll likely need for 2023 (Married filing jointly)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (e.g., half of SE tax, possibly student loan interest if eligible)
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — Profit or Loss from Business (for your $22,100 marketing work)
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (to compute SE tax)
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if you use the regular/actual method for home-office deduction). If you use the simplified method ($5/sqft), you do NOT file Form 8829; you simply claim the allowable amount on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you choose to itemize; likely you won’t need this)
- 1099-INTs and 1099-NEC/1099-MISC (if you received) and W-2 from Riverside Hospital (attach when filing or retain for records)
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement (if issued for 2023 interest); use to support student loan interest deduction if you paid interest in 2023

Other practical notes
- Estimated taxes: Because you have self-employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (both income tax and SE tax). If you didn’t make estimated payments during 2023, you may owe tax and possibly penalties unless withholding from W-2 covered it.
- Keep records: receipts, bank statements, 1098-E (student loan interest), 1099s, home utility bills, receipts for charitable donation, and records showing exclusive use of the home office.
- If you use the home-office actual method and your home is rented, you cannot claim depreciation of the home structure if you are a renter. (Depreciation applies to homeowners who own the residence used for business.)
- Student loan interest paid in prior years (2016–2020) is not deductible on your 2023 return — only interest actually paid in the tax year is deductible.

Example comparison (home office simplified vs actual)
- Simplified home-office deduction: $650 (130 sq ft × $5)
- Actual (rent portion only): ~$4,328 (20,640 × 130/620). If you also allocate utilities and other eligible expenses, actual may be larger. The actual method requires Form 8829 and more recordkeeping and reduces your Schedule C profit by the allocable amount.

What I need from you to be more specific / give estimates
- Did you actually pay any student loan interest in 2023? If yes, how much in 2023?
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to your self-employment work or unreimbursed employee expenses for the W-2 job?
- Do you have significant state or local taxes paid in 2023, mortgage interest, or other itemizable amounts beyond what you listed?
- Do you (or your spouse) have any other income, credits, or deductions (retirement contributions, tuition, childcare, etc.)?

