I’m not a CPA or tax attorney, but here’s a clear, practical summary based on the facts you gave and typical 2023 rules. If you want, I can run a more exact estimate after you give withholding and any other deductions/credits. Key assumptions I use below: the $22,100 is self‑employment (marketing for clients), the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for that self‑employment, you rent (not own) your home, and no other major deductions/credits exist.

Quick highlights / bottom line
- You will report the $22,100 as self‑employment income on Schedule C. Related business expenses (home‑office portion of rent, the $1,350 business expenses, any other business costs) reduce that income.
- The home‑office deduction is allowed for self‑employed use. Using the actual allocation of your rent (130/620 ≈ 20.97%) will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified method ($5 × 130 ft2 = $650). With your rent, the rent portion alone would be about $4,326/year (1,720 × 12 × 130/620).
- Student loan interest: the maximum deduction is $2,500 (adjustment to income). You reported $4,800 interest paid — you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to the MAGI phaseout rules). With the income levels you reported you are likely eligible for at least part (and probably the full $2,500), but verify against the exact MAGI phaseout if you want certainty.
- Charitable gift $1,350 is only deductible if you itemize. With the likely totals here, the standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700 for 2023) will almost certainly be larger, so you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction and not deduct the $1,350 charity on Schedule A.
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). You get to deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income.

Estimated numbers (rounded) — so you can see impact
- Total income used here: W‑2 $79,400 + self‑employment $22,100 + interest $920 + TD $290 = $102,710.
- Estimate business deductions (if the $1,350 unreimbursed items are business-related and you use the actual home allocation):
  - Home (rent portion): ≈ $4,326/year (1,720 × 12 × 130/620)
  - Other business expenses: $1,350
  - Total business deductions ≈ $5,676
- Estimated net self‑employment income ≈ $22,100 − $5,676 = $16,424.
- Self‑employment tax (approx): 15.3% on ~92.35% of net earnings → SE tax ≈ $2,320; half of that (~$1,160) is an above‑the‑line deduction.
- Student loan interest deduction: limited to $2,500 (assuming you qualify; see note above).
- Estimated AGI ≈ (79,400 + 1,210 + 16,424) − 1,160 (half SE tax) − 2,500 (student loan interest) ≈ $93,374.
- Standard deduction (MFJ 2023): $27,700 → estimated taxable income ≈ $65,674.
- Income tax rough estimate (2023 MFJ brackets): about $7,440; adding SE tax (~$2,320) gives total tax ≈ $9,760 before applying withholding/credits. This is a rough estimate to show scale; exact tax will vary with precise numbers, withholding, other credits, etc.

What you can (and cannot) deduct
- Deductible (as reported here):
  - Self‑employment business expenses: the $1,350 (if they were business expenses), any supplies, advertising, etc. — reported on Schedule C.
  - Home office: allowed for self‑employment. Either:
    - Actual method (Form 8829): prorate rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs by business square footage (130/620 ≈ 20.97%); or
    - Simplified method: $5 × 130 ft2 = $650 (easy but usually smaller in your case).
  - Half of self‑employment tax: above‑the‑line deduction.
  - Student loan interest: up to $2,500 (adjustment to income), subject to MAGI phaseouts.
  - Interest income and brokerage income: taxable on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if interest/dividend total > $1,500 or other triggers).
- Not deductible (under current law for 2018–2025 unless you’re self‑employed):
  - Unreimbursed employee business expenses for W‑2 employees are not deductible (suspended by TCJA). If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are employee expenses related to a W‑2 job, they are not deductible on your 2023 return. If they were self‑employed business expenses, they are deductible on Schedule C.
  - Charitable contributions only reduce tax if you itemize (which you likely will not do given your standard deduction is larger).

Forms you will probably need (federal)
- Form 1040.
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — e.g., for student loan interest and other adjustments.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) for the $22,100 self‑employment income and related expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) to compute SE tax and the deductible half.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual method for the home office. If you use the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829.
- Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (unlikely here).
- Schedule B only if interest/dividend total > $1,500 or other conditions apply (your interest totals were $1,210, so probably not needed).
- Illinois state return (IL‑1040), since you’re in Chicago — include the same income items; state rules differ for deductions and credits.

Other practical notes and recommendations
- If your self‑employment net income results in tax owed (after withholding) greater than $1,000, you should make estimated quarterly tax payments to avoid penalties.
- Keep all receipts and documentation for business expenses and charitable donations. For home office actual method keep records of rent, utilities, insurance, repairs allocated to business percent.
- If any of the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are employee expenses (W‑2 job), they’re not deductible on your federal 2023 return.
- If you’d like, I can:
  - Recompute the numbers exactly if you provide W‑2 federal withholding, any other deductions (state tax paid, mortgage interest if any, other credits), and whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses were for the self‑employment work vs for a W‑2 job.
  - Prepare a checklist of documents to gather before you file.
  - Draft questions to ask a CPA if you want professional review.

Would you like me to:
- run a more exact tax estimate if you give me your W‑2 withholding and confirm whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are self‑employment related, or
- prepare a checklist of documents/forms to gather for filing and/or for a CPA?

