What must be reported
- Report the W-2 wages on Form 1040.
- Report the $22,100 self‑employment income on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Report the bank/broker interest/dividends on Form 1040 (usually via Forms 1099-INT and 1099-DIV).

How to treat the self‑employment income
- Schedule C: report the $22,100 gross receipts and reduce by ordinary and necessary business expenses (marketing costs, client expenses, etc.). If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” were actually business expenses for the marketing work (not employee expenses), they belong on Schedule C and reduce your net profit.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe Social Security/Medicare on your net self‑employment income (calculated on Schedule SE). Rough estimate using the numbers you provided:
  - If you deduct $1,350 business expenses and take the home‑office rent share (see next), an approximate net SE income might be ~ $16,400 (your actual will vary depending on exact business expenses).
  - SE tax (~15.3% on 92.35% of net): you’ll owe SE tax and you can deduct half of that SE tax as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Form 1040.

Home office treatment
- Because you are self‑employed, you can use either:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650 (no Form 8829). Simple but often smaller.
  - Regular (actual) method: allocate the business percentage of home expenses. Business percentage = 130 / 620 = 20.97% (~21%).
    - Rent portion deductible on Schedule C = 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,327 (plus you could allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have them). Because your rent share (just the rent alone) produces a larger deduction than the simplified method, the regular method is likely more beneficial here.
- If you use the regular method, you’ll generally file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) attached to Schedule C (this helps compute the allowable home‑office deduction; depreciation rules differ if you own vs rent).

Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction (if you paid interest in 2023) is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1 of Form 1040), not an itemized deduction.
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 per year (subject to MAGI phase‑outs). You must have actually paid interest in 2023 and received Form 1098-E (or other documentation) from the lender.
- Note: your note “$4,800 student loan interest (Sallie Mae, 2016–2020)” suggests those interest amounts might be for prior years — only interest paid in 2023 can be deducted on the 2023 return. Please confirm the year(s) the interest was paid and whether you have a 2023 1098-E.

Charitable contribution
- $1,350 to Habitat qualifies as an itemized deduction on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you do not separately deduct it on the federal return.

Unreimbursed employee expenses
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (for W-2 employees) are not deductible for tax years 2018 through 2025 (TCJA). If the $1,350 is actually business expense for your self‑employment, put it on Schedule C. If it is unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job, it is not deductible on your federal 2023 return.

Standard deduction vs itemize
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $27,700.
- Your known itemizable amounts (based on what you listed): charitable $1,350. You didn’t list mortgage interest, property taxes, or state/local taxes — those could raise itemized deductions, but SALT is capped at $10,000. With only $1,350 in charitable contributions shown, it’s almost certain the standard deduction ($27,700) is higher than your likely itemized total.
- Important: the home office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it reduces your Schedule C net income and thus your AGI/taxable income even if you take the standard deduction.
- Preliminary conclusion: probably take the standard deduction for 2023, but claim business deductions (Schedule C) including the home office, pay SE tax and take the allowable SE tax deduction and (if eligible) student loan interest adjustment.

Possible Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- If you have net qualified business income from your self-employment, you may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995). Given your numbers and filing jointly well under phaseout thresholds, you’ll likely be eligible for a QBI deduction of roughly 20% of your qualified net business income (subject to special rules). This further reduces taxable income. A tax preparer or software will compute it precisely.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — e.g., student loan interest, half SE tax is technically reported elsewhere but adjustments show on Schedule 1)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $22,100 self‑employment income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the regular (actual) home‑office method
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) for the QBI deduction (if applicable)
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (unlikely based on the numbers provided)
- State return: Illinois Form IL‑1040 — IL starts from your federal AGI and has its own forms and rules (IL has a flat tax; charitable deduction and other items differ); check IL instructions or a tax pro for exact state impacts.

Estimated approximate calculation (rough illustrative example)
- Total income (wages + SE + interest) ≈ $102,710
- Example Schedule C deductions: $1,350 business expenses + home office rent share ≈ $4,327 → net SE income ≈ $16,423
- Self-employment tax (estimate) ≈ $2,320 → half deductible ≈ $1,160 (above-the-line)
- If eligible for full student loan interest deduction (max) = $2,500 (only if you actually paid that in 2023 and don’t exceed MAGI phase‑outs)
- Adjusted Gross Income estimate ≈ $93,400 (very rough)
- Subtract standard deduction $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $65,700 (very rough)
- Then compute tax, subtract QBI (if applicable), credits, etc.

Recommended next steps / questions for you
