I can walk through this and give practical next steps. I’m not a CPA or enrolled agent, so consider this general guidance and confirm with a tax professional for your final return.

Summary of your 2023 items
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (you stated).
- Income you reported:
  - W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
  - Self‑employment/contract marketing: $22,100 (6 clients)
  - Interest from Chase savings: $920
  - Income from TD Ameritrade: $290
  - Total gross income = $79,400 + $22,100 + $920 + $290 = $102,710

What you can/don’t usually be able to deduct
- Student loan interest: Limited to a maximum deduction of $2,500 for 2023 (this is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1). The deduction phases out with MAGI for MFJ (roughly $145k–$175k for 2023). With the income numbers you gave, you’re under the phaseout, so you can take the $2,500 maximum (not the full $4,800).
- Charitable contribution: $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity — deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep your receipt.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: Not deductible for 2018–2025 if they are nondeductible “employee” expenses (suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions). If those $1,350 are expenses related to your self‑employment work (the 6 clients), they should instead be reported as business expenses on Schedule C and reduce your self‑employment income.
- Home office: Deductible only if you qualify (regular and exclusive use for business and you’re self‑employed). You have 130 sq ft exclusive, home is 620 sq ft → business-use percentage = 130/620 ≈ 21.0%. Two options:
  - Regular method: deduct your share of actual home expenses. With rent $1,720/month = $20,640/year, the home‑office portion would be ≈ $20,640 × 21.0% ≈ $4,328. You’d report this on Schedule C and (if using the regular method) Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home).
  - Simplified method: $5 × square feet, up to 300 sq ft → 130 × $5 = $650 (no Form 8829). Usually the regular method gives a larger deduction here because of the high rent.
  - Note: Home office deduction cannot create a loss from that business beyond the other income limits — it’s limited by your net self‑employment income from that business. Also only available if your home office is for self‑employment; employees generally cannot claim it.
- Self‑employment expenses & taxes: You’ll report the marketing income and related business expenses (and the home office) on Schedule C. Self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) will be computed on Schedule SE; you pay the employer+employee share (15.3%) but you can deduct half of that as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

Quick illustrative calculation (approximate)
(Assumes the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses; uses regular home‑office method.)
- Self‑employment gross: $22,100
  - Less business expenses: $1,350 → $20,750
  - Less home‑office portion of rent (~21%): ≈ $4,328 → net self‑employment profit ≈ $16,422
- Self‑employment tax (approx): 16,422 × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $2,320
  - Half of SE tax deductible = ≈ $1,160 (adjustment)
- Total income to the return before adjustments: W‑2 79,400 + net SE profit 16,422 + interest 1,210 = ≈ $97,032
- Adjustments: student loan interest $2,500 + half SE tax $1,160 = $3,660
- Approx AGI = $97,032 − $3,660 = ≈ $93,372

Itemize vs. standard deduction
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2023) = $27,700.
- Your known potential itemized deductions: charitable $1,350 (plus any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses, etc., that you did not list).
- With only $1,350 of known itemizable items, standard deduction ($27,700) will almost certainly be larger. So recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (large mortgage interest, large property taxes, state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, or big unreimbursed medical bills above 7.5% of AGI).
- If you itemize to get the $1,350 charity, you must use Schedule A.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main individual return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for student loan interest deduction and adjustment for half of SE tax
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report $22,100 and all self‑employment business expenses (including home office deduction if using Schedule C entries)
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method for the business use of your home (otherwise use the simplified method)
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (for the $1,350 charity or other itemized deductions)
- Possibly Schedule B — only required if taxable interest/dividends exceed $1,500 or certain other conditions. Your interest totals $1,210 so Schedule B is not required based on that threshold.
- State return: Illinois Form IL‑1040 (you’ll need to file state taxes for Illinois unless you have an exception).

Other practical points
- Keep detailed receipts for the charitable donation and all business expenses (including the unreimbursed expenses if they are business‑related). Keep a floor plan or contemporaneous log that supports the exclusive use of the 130 sq ft office.
- If you are self‑employed, you may owe estimated taxes (Form 1040‑ES) for 2024 quarters to cover income and self‑employment tax — check whether you had sufficient withholding from your W‑2 spouse or need to make estimated payments.
- Make sure you receive 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for contract income and 1099‑INT or 1099‑DIV from banks/brokerages; reconcile amounts.
- If your spouse has income or other deductions not listed, that will change AGI and phaseouts (especially student loan interest).

Questions for you (so I can refine the guidance)
1. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” employee expenses (i.e., for the W‑2 job) or business expenses for the marketing/contract work? (If business, they belong on Schedule C; if employee, they’re not deductible in 2023.)
2. Do you (or your spouse) have any other deductible items not listed — mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax withheld/paid, large medical expenses, retirement contributions, or other charitable gifts?
3. Is the TD Ameritrade amount interest, dividend, or a capital gain? (You’ll get 1099s that clarify.)
4. Do you want me to draft a short checklist of documents to collect and the exact lines/pages to fill on each form, or estimate your federal tax liability roughly based on the assumptions above?

If you answer the clarifying questions I’ll refine the numbers and give a short checklist showing exactly where each item goes on the forms.

