I can walk through this and give a clear, practical recommendation — but first a few clarifying questions. I’ll also state assumptions and show approximate calculations so you can see the effect of each choice.

Quick summary / likely outcome (based on what you gave)
- You most likely should take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) because the itemizable amounts you listed (charitable $1,350 plus any other itemizable things you didn’t list) are well below $27,700.
- Many of your listed amounts affect different parts of the return:
  - Student loan interest is an “above-the-line” adjustment (but limited and subject to phaseout) — it’s not an itemized deduction.
  - Home office and unreimbursed expenses may be business deductions if the $22,100 is self-employment income; if they are employee unreimbursed expenses (W-2 job), they generally are NOT deductible for 2018–2025.
  - If the $22,100 is self-employment income, you’ll need Schedule C and Schedule SE (self-employment tax). Home office is reported on Schedule C (and Form 8829 if you use actual expenses).

Important clarifying questions (please answer so I can be precise)
1. Is the $22,100 “marketing, 6 clients” self-employment/contractor income (1099-NEC/1099-MISC) that you reported as business income? Or is it additional W-2 wages from an employer?  
2. The $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” — were those expenses for that self-employment work (you have receipts and paid them as a contractor), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a W-2 job?  
3. The $4,800 student loan interest — was that interest you paid in tax year 2023, or is that total interest paid across 2016–2020? (Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible on your 2023 return.)  
4. Is the $1,720/month a rent payment (not mortgage), and do utilities/insurance/other housing costs get paid separately? (That matters for actual home-office calculations.)

What you can (potentially) deduct — rules & limits
- Standard deduction vs itemize:
  - 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700. You should itemize only if your Schedule A itemized deductions exceed that.
  - Your listed charitable contribution $1,350 would be on Schedule A if you itemize. With the amounts you listed, itemizing is unlikely to exceed $27,700 unless you also have large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other itemizable amounts you didn’t list.

- Student loan interest:
  - Only interest paid in 2023 qualifies. The deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1, not Schedule A), limited to $2,500 per year (subject to MAGI phaseouts). If your MAGI for MFJ is too high you may be ineligible or partially phased out — I can estimate phaseout if you want to provide expected MAGI.

- Self-employment income / unreimbursed expenses:
  - If the $22,100 is self-employment income, ordinary and necessary business expenses (receipts) reduce Schedule C net profit. Those include supplies, business travel, advertising, etc.
  - If the $1,350 expenses are business expenses for that self-employment, they’re deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W-2 job, they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025.

- Home office:
  - Allowed only if used regularly/exclusively for business and you’re self-employed (or meet narrow employee-agent exceptions). If allowed, two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5 × square feet used for business (max 300 sq ft). For 130 sq ft → 130 × $5 = $650 deductible.
    - Actual-expense method: take the business-use percentage of allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs allocated to the business portion). You gave 130 sq ft exclusive out of 620 sq ft → business % = 130/620 ≈ 20.97% (≈21%).
      - If rent = $1,720/month → annual rent = $20,640. Business share of rent ≈ $20,640 × 0.2097 ≈ $4,330. Add business share of utilities/insurance/etc. for larger deduction.
  - Home-office deduction is reported on Schedule C (Form 8829 if using actual expenses). Note: the deductible home-office expenses generally cannot create a net business loss that is disallowed (there are limits); excess may be carried forward.

- Interest/dividends:
  - Bank interest $920 + TD Ameritrade $290 = $1,210 interest income — report on Form 1040. Schedule B is required if interest/dividend > $1,500, so you’re under that threshold.

Forms you’ll likely need for 2023
- Federal:
  - Form 1040
  - W-2(s) and report W-2 wages on Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (for adjustments such as student loan interest deduction if eligible)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — if $22,100 is self-employment income
  - Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to calculate Social Security/Medicare self-employment tax
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use actual method for home-office deduction (or use simplified method and only claim it on Schedule C)
  - Schedule A — only if you itemize
  - Schedule B — only if interest/dividend > $1,500 (you’re below that)
  - 1099 forms and 1098-E (student loan interest statement) and 1099-INT / 1099-B as applicable — keep with your records

- Illinois state:
  - IL-1040 — Illinois taxes are computed starting from certain federal amounts; Illinois tax rate is a flat rate (for 2023 it was 4.95%). You’ll file IL-1040 and include federal AGI and additions/subtractions required by Illinois rules.

Approximate numeric example (if $22,100 is self-employment and $1,350 expenses are business expenses; rent = $1,720/mo)
