Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- You almost certainly should take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly (2023 standard = $27,700) unless you have large mortgage interest, large state & local taxes, or other itemizable amounts you haven’t listed.  
- Because you have $22,100 of marketing/self-employment income, you can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (including a home-office deduction if the space is used exclusively and regularly for the business). W‑2 unreimbursed employee expenses are NOT deductible under current law.
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C (business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and either Form 8829 (regular home‑office method) or use the simplified home‑office method. Student loan interest is claimed as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) if you qualify; charitable gifts are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).

Details, calculations, and assumptions
I used the figures you gave: W‑2 wages $79,400; self‑employment/marketing income $22,100; interest $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210; rent $1,720/mo for a 620 sq ft place; home office 130 sq ft exclusive; student loan interest $4,800 (see question below); charitable $1,350; “unreimbursed expenses” $1,350 (I assume these are business expenses — see questions).

Home office
- Square footage fraction = 130 / 620 = 20.97% (approx).
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
- Regular-method home-office share of rent = 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,328. (You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., with the same percentage if you have them.)
- Simplified method = $5 × 130 sq ft = $650.
Notes:
- The home-office deduction is available only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business and it is your principal place of business (or you use it to meet clients, etc.).
- Self-employed taxpayers use Schedule C to claim business expenses and either Form 8829 (regular method) or the simplified method. W‑2 employees generally cannot claim a home-office deduction.

Self-employment tax and effect on AGI (estimate)
Assuming the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses and you take the regular home-office deduction above:
- Business expenses = $1,350 + $4,328 = $5,678.
- Net self‑employment income = $22,100 − $5,678 = $16,422.
- Net earnings subject to SE tax = 92.35% × $16,422 ≈ $15,166.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × $15,166 ≈ $2,320. Half of that (~$1,160) is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI).
Estimated AGI (rough):
- W‑2 79,400 + net self‑employment 16,422 + interest 1,210 = 97,032
- Less half SE tax ≈ 1,160 → AGI ≈ $95,872

Student loan interest
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (if you paid that much in 2023 and your MAGI is under the phaseout). You reported $4,800 — you can deduct at most $2,500 and only the interest paid in the tax year (the $4,800 number may refer to total interest paid across prior years — confirm).  
- For 2023, the phaseout for married filing jointly generally begins at MAGI ~$145,000 and phases out by ~$175,000. Your estimated AGI (~$95,900) is well below that, so you would be eligible for the full deduction up to $2,500 (assuming the $4,800 is interest paid in 2023 and is qualified interest). You will need Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from your servicer to substantiate the deduction.

Charitable contributions
- $1,350 cash gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction ($27,700 for MFJ), you cannot separately deduct that charitable amount.

Unreimbursed employee expenses
- If these $1,350 are unreimbursed expenses for a W‑2 job (Riverside Hospital), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law. If they are business expenses for your marketing/self‑employment work, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Itemize vs standard
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2023) = $27,700. From the items you listed, your only likely itemizable amounts are $1,350 charitable + any state & local taxes (SALT), mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses, etc. You would need total itemized deductions to exceed $27,700 to make itemizing beneficial. Based on what you listed, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return). Status: Married Filing Jointly.
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income — e.g., student loan interest deduction, deductible part of self‑employment tax is reported here via calculations).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses (including the home-office deduction if you use the simplified method or if you allocate expenses directly on Schedule C).
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the regular method for the home-office deduction; alternatively use the simplified method on Schedule C (no Form 8829 required).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize.
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividends exceed $1,500 or other special cases.
- Keep 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for self‑employment income if issued, W‑2 from Riverside Hospital, 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B statements from banks/brokers, and Form 1098‑E for student loan interest.

Practical next steps & documentation to gather
- Gather W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for clients, 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV from banks/brokers, and Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer (to substantiate student loan interest).
- Collect receipts / records for the $1,350 business expenses and the $1,350 charitable gift.
- Record home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance) and keep notes showing the exclusive business use of the 130 sq ft area.
- If you didn’t make estimated tax payments during the year, be prepared that you may owe income tax + self‑employment tax when you file.
- If you want the maximum home‑office deduction, use the regular method but be sure your records support the allocation. The simplified method ($5/sq ft) is easier and requires less recordkeeping.

Questions I need to confirm to refine this advice
1. Is the $4,800 student‑loan interest amount the interest you paid in 2023, or is it total interest across prior years? (You should have a 2023 Form 1098‑E that shows interest paid in 2023.)  
2. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the marketing/self‑employment work, or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from the W‑2 job?  
3. Do you have any other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax withheld/paid, big medical expenses) not listed?  
4. Did you receive any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC from your six clients, or were you paid without 1099s?

If you confirm those, I can:
- Recalculate more exactly and show the numbers line-by-line for your 2023 return (estimated AGI, taxable income under standard vs itemized, estimate of self‑employment tax).  
- Draft a checklist of documents to give to your preparer or to use in tax software.

Disclaimer
This is general tax guidance based on the information you provided. I’m not a CPA/tax attorney. For definitive answers specific to your situation, please consult a tax professional.

