Short summary / bottom line
- You will report the Riverside W-2 ($79,400) on Form 1040 and your marketing income ($22,100) on Schedule C (self-employment). Interest income ($920 + $290 = $1,210) is taxable and reported on 1040 (Schedule B if required).
- For the self-employment income you should use Schedule C and Schedule SE. A home-office deduction is allowed for self-employed work used exclusively and regularly — the actual-expense method (using a portion of your rent) looks substantially more beneficial than the simplified $5/sq ft method in your numbers.
- Student loan interest is only deductible for interest paid in the tax year (not prior years). The maximum federal student-loan interest deduction is $2,500 (phaseouts apply by MAGI). Confirm whether the $4,800 you listed was paid in 2023; if not, it’s not deductible on your 2023 return.
- With the info you gave, you’ll almost certainly take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) rather than itemize (your listed charitable gift $1,350 is too small to beat the standard deduction).
- Important forms you’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self-employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use the actual-home-office method), and Form 8995 (or 8995-A) for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction. Also IL Form IL-1040 for state tax.

Key details, calculations, and reasoning

1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Form 1040)
- Self-employment (marketing): $22,100 → Schedule C
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210 (Form 1040; Schedule B only if required)

2) Business expenses and the “unreimbursed expenses”
- Unreimbursed expenses: If these $1,350 are business expenses for your self-employment (supplies, travel, ads, etc.), report them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job, those are not deductible for 2018–2025 on Schedule A (suspended). Clarify which they are.
- Home office: qualifies for self-employed taxpayers if used regularly and exclusively for business. You gave an exclusive 130 sq ft.

3) Home office — simplified vs actual method (quick numeric comparison)
- Home size: 620 sq ft; office: 130 sq ft → business-use percentage = 130 / 620 = 20.97% (≈21%).
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.

A. Actual-expense method (allocate rent proportion):
- Home-office share of rent = 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330 (plus any allocated utilities/insurance you can document).
- Business expenses (other) = $1,350 (assuming these are business expenses).
- Net Schedule C profit = $22,100 − $4,330 − $1,350 ≈ $16,420.
- Self-employment tax (approx) = 15.3% × (92.35% of net SE income)
  ≈ 0.153 × (0.9235 × 16,420) ≈ $2,320. Half of that (~$1,160) is an above-the-line deduction.
- Potential QBI (20% of qualified business income) ≈ 20% × 16,420 ≈ $3,284 (subject to limitations — likely available at your income level).

B. Simplified method ($5 per sq ft):
- Deduction = 130 × $5 = $650.
- Business expenses (other) = $1,350.
- Net Schedule C profit = $22,100 − $650 − $1,350 = $20,100.
- Self-employment tax ≈ 0.153 × 0.9235 × 20,100 ≈ $2,842 (half ≈ $1,421).
- QBI ≈ 20% × 20,100 ≈ $4,020 (again subject to limits).

Which is better?
- The actual-expense method reduces your net SE income more in this case (≈$16.4K vs $20.1K), reduces SE tax, and lowers taxable income. Rough estimate from the two scenarios shows the actual method yields lower overall federal tax (difference on the order of a few hundred dollars, using the numbers above). So actual-expense method (using a portion of rent and utilities) is likely more beneficial.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- Important: the student-loan interest deduction is only for interest actually paid in the tax year. You listed “Sallie Mae, 2016–2020” and $4,800 — that sounds like interest paid in earlier years. If those payments occurred in 2016–2020 they cannot be deducted on your 2023 return. If you actually paid $4,800 in 2023, the maximum deductible in 2023 is $2,500, and the deduction phases out at higher MAGI (for MFJ 2023 phaseout range begins around $145k and fully phased out near $175k — verify exact numbers for your filing). Based on the income totals you provided, you would likely be under the phaseout and eligible for the full $2,500 if the payments were made in 2023.

5) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- Standard deduction for married filing jointly, 2023 = $27,700.
- Your itemizable items you reported: charitable gift $1,350. Employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible. Unless you have much larger additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses exceeding threshold, etc.), you will take the standard deduction.

6) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — to report student loan interest deduction and the deduction for half of self-employment tax if applicable
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — marketing income and business expenses (including home office if actual method)
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use actual expenses for the home office (business use of home)
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — to claim the QBI deduction (Form 8995 is the simpler form if you’re under the taxable-income thresholds)
- Schedule B — if interest income requires it (generally if total taxable interest/dividends > $1,500 or certain other conditions)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely here)
- Illinois state return: IL Form IL-1040 (IL taxes virtually all income at the flat state rate; state rules differ for some deductions)

7) Records to gather / documents to keep
