Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can walk you through this and tell you what’s deductible, whether you should likely itemize, how to treat the home office, and which tax forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions first so I give accurate answers:

- Is the $22,100 “additional income” self-employment/contractor income (1099-NEC/1099-MISC), or was it wages reported on a W-2?  
- Is the $4,800 student-loan interest the amount you actually paid in 2023 (and is Sallie Mae the lender who can provide Form 1098-E)?  
- Do you rent your home? (Your $1,720/month suggests rent, but please confirm.)  
- Do you have any other deductible items (mortgage interest, property taxes, state income tax withheld or paid, other charitable donations, medical expenses, etc.)?

Below I’ll assume: the $22,100 is self-employment income, the $4,800 is interest paid in 2023 (but see limits below), and you rent (so we’ll use rent to compute home-office portion). If any of those assumptions are wrong tell me and I’ll re-calc.

1) What you can deduct (short answers)
- Self-employment income (Schedule C): business income $22,100. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (you mentioned $1,350 unreimbursed expenses — those belong on Schedule C).  
- Home office: deductible as a business expense on Schedule C. You can use simplified ($5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft) or the regular/actual method (your pro-rated share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.).  
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 (adjustment to income on Schedule 1) if you meet the income limits (see below). If the $4,800 is interest paid in 2023, you cannot deduct the full $4,800 — the maximum is $2,500 (and it phases out by MAGI).  
- Charitable cash donation: $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity — deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt.  
- Interest from Chase ($920) and TD Ameritrade ($290) are taxable interest income; you should receive 1099-INT/1099 forms and report them on Form 1040.

2) Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (MFJ) = $27,700.  
- Known itemizable amounts you gave: charitable $1,350. Student loan interest is an above-the-line adjustment (Schedule 1), not an itemized deduction, so it does not count toward Schedule A. Unless you have substantial state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other large itemized deductions, your total itemized deductions are very likely below $27,700. Therefore, in most cases the standard deduction will be better.

Example rough comparison (with only the items you listed):
- Itemized (known): charitable $1,350 (+ any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes you haven't reported). That’s far below $27,700, so standard deduction normally wins.
Recommendation: Take the standard deduction unless your combined itemizable expenses (SALT up to $10,000 + mortgage interest + substantial medical >7.5% of AGI + other charitable gifts) exceed $27,700.

3) Home office — how to treat it (with numbers)
You said the office is 130 sq ft exclusively and home is 620 sq ft. Office % = 130 / 620 = 20.97% (≈21%).

Two methods:

a) Simplified method:
- $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction.
- Simpler to claim (no Form 8829). Good if your home-office share of rent/expenses is small.

b) Actual method (likely larger in your case):
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Home-office portion of rent = 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,328.
- You can also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., by the same percentage and deduct the business portion.
- To use the actual method you generally use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute the deductible amount and transfer it to Schedule C.
- If you’re renting (not owning), you cannot claim depreciation on the home; depreciation rules mostly apply to owners. So for a renter the principal actual deduction will be the proportion of rent and utilities.

Given your rent, the actual method (≈$4,328 just for rent) is considerably larger than the simplified $650, so the actual method is likely better. Note: the home-office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and thus both income tax and self-employment tax).

Important limits: the home office deduction cannot create or increase a net loss from the business in certain cases; if your business expenses (including home office) exceed your business income, there are limitations and possibly carryforwards — typical for most sole proprietors this is manageable, but I can check once you confirm full expenses.

4) Self-employment tax and related adjustments
- Self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) applies to net self-employment earnings. The rate is 15.3% on net earnings (subject to the usual rules). Net earnings are 92.35% of your self-employment profit for SE tax calculation.  
- You may deduct one-half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (this reduces AGI).  
- The home office deduction and your $1,350 business expenses reduce Schedule C profit and therefore reduce both income tax and SE tax.

Quick illustrative estimate using your numbers (if $22,100 is gross self-employment, $1,350 business expenses, and actual home-office rent deduction ≈ $4,328):
- Gross self-employment: $22,100  
- Less business expenses $1,350 → 20,750  
- Less home-office portion (rent) ≈ 4,328 → net profit ≈ 16,422  
- Net for SE tax = 16,422 × 0.9235 ≈ 15,171 → SE tax ≈ $2,322. Half (≈$1,161) is deductible as an adjustment to income.

(These are illustrative; actual numbers vary with other deductible utility items, etc.)

5) Student loan interest
- Maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (adjustment to income on Schedule 1). If you paid $4,800 in 2023, you can still only deduct up to $2,500 (assuming you meet income phase-out rules).  
- Phaseout for MFJ in 2023: starts at MAGI $145,000 and phases out to $175,000; above $175,000 you cannot take this deduction. Based on the rough AGI estimate from your numbers, you should be under the phaseout threshold and eligible for the full $2,500 (but I’ll confirm after we finalize numbers).

6) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).  
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments): to claim the deductible part of self-employment tax and the student loan interest adjustment.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business): report $22,100 and the $1,350 business expenses, and include the home office deduction result.  
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax): calculate SE tax.  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual method for the home office. If you use the simplified method you do not use Form 8829.  
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you decide to itemize (unlikely based on the figures you gave).  
- 1099s / forms to expect: W-2 from Riverside (you have it), 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC for contractor income (if issued), 1099-INT from Chase and 1099-INT/1099-DIV/1099-B from TD Ameritrade as applicable. Keep Form 1098-E (student loan interest statement) from Sallie Mae for the amount of interest paid in 2023.  
- Illinois state return: IL Form IL-1040 (Illinois uses your federal AGI as a starting point and has its own rules; expect to file IL-1040). Illinois income tax is a flat rate (see current rate) and you will report your federal AGI there.

7) Records to keep / receipts
- 1099s and W-2.  
- Sallie Mae Form 1098-E (student loan interest).  
- Charitable donation receipt from Habitat for Humanity.  
- Documentation for the home office: floor plan or measurement showing 130 sq ft, lease showing rent amounts, utility bills, renter’s insurance premium receipts.  
- Business receipts for the $1,350 expenses and any other business costs.  
- Brokerage/interest statements from TD Ameritrade and Chase.

8) Other practical notes
- If you had no quarterly estimated tax payments for your self-employment income, you may owe tax and possibly underpayment penalties. You can estimate final tax owed once we compute total tax.  
- If you prefer simplicity and record-keeping is minimal, the simplified home-office method is easier; but given your rent the actual method is likely much more beneficial.  
- If you want, I can run a more precise estimate of Federal tax owed (including SE tax and likely refund/amount due) — but I’ll need confirmation on the clarifying questions above and any additional deductible items (state taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes, other charitable gifts, other business expenses, estimated payments made).

Next step
- Confirm the clarifying details I asked (self-employed vs W-2 for the $22,100, whether the $4,800 is interest paid in 2023, and whether you rent). Once you confirm, I’ll produce a more exact calculation (AGI, taxable income under standard vs itemized, estimated federal tax and SE tax, and recommended method for home office including exact form lines to fill).

