- You can deduct student loan interest only for interest actually paid in 2023 (and the deduction is capped at $2,500 and phases out at higher MAGI). The $4,800 you noted for 2016–2020 is not deductible on your 2023 return.
- Forms you’ll commonly need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (for adjustments such as student‑loan interest), Form 8829 (if using the actual home office method), and Schedule A only if you decide to itemize. Also bring any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B, and your W‑2.

Details and quick numbers (using the amounts you provided)
1) Income summary you provided
- W‑2 wages: $79,400
- Self‑employment gross (marketing): $22,100
- Interest (Chase savings): $920
- TD Ameritrade: $290
Gross total = $102,710

2) Treatment of the self‑employment income and business expenses
- Business expenses: you listed $1,350 unreimbursed business expenses — those are deductible on Schedule C (they are NOT deductible as employee unreimbursed expenses if you were an employee).
- Home office: you said 130 sq ft exclusive, home = 620 sq ft ⇒ business % = 130/620 ≈ 20.97%.
  - Rent = $1,720/mo ⇒ $20,640/year. Using the actual method, home‑office portion of rent = 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330. (You can also use the simplified method at $5/sq ft ⇒ 130 × $5 = $650 — in your case the actual method is larger.)
  - Home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and therefore reduces self‑employment tax and taxable income).
- Example (illustrative): If you use the actual home office amount above:
  - Net business income = $22,100 − $1,350 − $4,330 ≈ $16,420
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × $16,420) ≈ $2,324
  - Deductible half of SE tax (adjustment to income) ≈ $1,162

3) Student loan interest
- Only interest paid in 2023 is relevant. The deduction is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), limited to $2,500 (maximum) and is phased out for higher MAGI. Based on the income totals you gave, you should be well under the phaseout and could take the full $2,500 — but I need to confirm the actual 2023 interest you paid to be sure. The $4,800 you mentioned for 2016–2020 cannot be applied to 2023.

4) Charitable contribution
- $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized deduction (Schedule A) if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction you do not separately deduct it.

5) Standard deduction vs itemize
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (2023) = $27,700.
- With the items you listed (only $1,350 charitable plus unknown state/local taxes or mortgage interest), itemizing is unlikely to exceed $27,700. So the standard deduction is almost certainly better unless you have sizable state income taxes, property tax, mortgage interest, or other big itemizable amounts you didn’t list.

6) Forms you’ll need (checklist)
- Form 1040
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report gross receipts ($22,100), business expenses ($1,350), and home office expense (either carry from Form 8829 or report simplified)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments: half of SE tax and student loan interest deduction)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if using the actual‑expense method for the home office. If you use the simplified method ($5/sq ft), you generally don’t file Form 8829.
- Schedule A only if you decide to itemize
- Attach/keep: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1099‑B, receipts for the Habitat donation, proof of student loan interest paid in 2023 (Form 1098‑E or lender statement)

7) Other practical items and cautions
- Confirm the $4,800 student interest: was that paid in 2023 or earlier years? If earlier, it’s not deductible for 2023.
- Confirm the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses related to your marketing/self‑employed work. If they are employee unreimbursed expenses (for an employer job), they’re not deductible for 2018–2025.
- Home‑office rules: the space must be used regularly and exclusively for business, and it must be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients). You said the space is exclusive, so that sounds okay.
- Home‑office deduction cannot create a business loss in some situations (limitations can apply). If your business expenses exceed your business income, some deductions may be limited — software or a tax preparer can check limits.
- You may owe estimated taxes (quarterly) for self‑employment income if withholding doesn’t cover tax + SE tax.

