Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- You will almost certainly take the standard deduction ($27,700 for married filing jointly in 2023). Your $1,350 charitable gift alone is far below that.
- Report the $22,100 marketing income on Schedule C (self‑employment). That makes you subject to self‑employment tax and lets you claim business deductions (including a home‑office deduction).
- Home office: you can use the actual‑expense method or the simplified method. With 130 sq ft of a 620 sq ft home and rent $1,720/mo, the actual method will give a substantially larger deduction than the simplified method.
- You can deduct student loan interest as an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500 for 2023) if the payments you reference were made in 2023 and you meet the MAGI limits.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for W‑2 work) are not deductible for 2018–2025. If the $1,350 are business expenses for your marketing work, include them on Schedule C instead.

What to report (income)
- W‑2 wages: $79,400 (report on Form 1040).
- Self‑employment/contract income: $22,100 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business).
- Interest from Chase ($920) and TD Ameritrade ($290): report as interest/dividend income on your federal return. (Schedule B is only required if interest/dividends > $1,500.)
- Keep all 1099s/records you received from clients and the banks/broker.

Home‑office treatment (self‑employment)
- Eligibility: must be used regularly and exclusively for your trade/business.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction.
  - Actual expense method: pro rata share of actual home expenses. Your business use percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97% (≈21%).
    - If you only have rent as the home cost: annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Business portion ≈ $20,640 × 21% ≈ $4,326.
    - The actual method also allows a share of utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc. (If you own, depreciation can also apply; if you rent, depreciation of the dwelling is not applicable.)
- You generally should choose the actual method here because ~$4,300 > $650. To claim actual-method home office deduction for a sole proprietor you compute it on Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the result to Schedule C.

Self‑employment tax and deductions
- Schedule C net profit flows to Schedule SE to compute self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare ~15.3% on 92.35% of net SE earnings).
- You may deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- You may also deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (supplies, client expenses, the $1,350 if those are business expenses, advertising, part of phone/internet, etc.) on Schedule C.

Student loan interest and charitable gift
- Student loan interest: maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (an “above‑the‑line” adjustment on Schedule 1) if you paid that interest in the tax year and your MAGI is below the phase‑out range. If you actually paid $4,800 in 2023, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to phaseouts). Note: if the $4,800 was paid in earlier years (2016–2020), it cannot be deducted on the 2023 return — you only deduct interest paid in 2023.
- Charitable contribution $1,350: deductible only if you itemize. Because the standard deduction ($27,700 MFJ) is much larger than your reported itemizable amounts, you will likely not itemize, so this gift would not reduce your federal tax if you take the standard deduction. (Keep the receipt — for any cash gift of $250+ you need a contemporaneous written acknowledgment.)

Qualified Business Income (QBI)
- You may qualify for the 20% QBI deduction (Section 199A) on up to 20% of your qualified business income from Schedule C. For most taxpayers well under the income thresholds, the deduction is roughly 20% of net SE income after business deductions. If your taxable income is low enough, use Form 8995 (simplified QBI).

Which forms you’ll typically need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments (student loan interest, half of SE tax flows here)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — if claiming QBI deduction
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (seems unlikely here)
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividend income > $1,500
- Keep any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT, 1099‑B, and your W‑2s to attach/store as records (1099s are not “attached” but used to prepare return).

Illinois (state) filing
- File Illinois IL‑1040. Illinois taxable income starts with federal AGI with some state modifications; you’ll report federal AGI and follow IL instructions. (Illinois does not use the federal standard deduction amount as your IL personal exemption; check IL instructions or ask your preparer for exact IL additions/subtractions.)

Quick example (very rough estimate) — helps show impact
- Total income: 79,400 + 22,100 + 920 + 290 = 102,710.
- If home office actual deduction ≈ $4,326 and business expenses $1,350 (assuming those are business), Schedule C net ≈ 22,100 − 4,326 − 1,350 = 16,424.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 0.9235 × 16,424 ≈ $2,320 (rough). Half (~$1,160) is deductible.
- Student loan interest deduction (if paid in 2023 and you’re under the phaseout) = up to $2,500 (not the full $4,800).
- Adjustments (half SE tax + student loan interest) ≈ $3,660 → estimated AGI ≈ $99,050.
- Standard deduction $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $71,350 (very approximate).
This is to illustrate flow; these are estimates — exact values require all records and exact amounts.

Practical next steps I recommend
1. Clarify a few items (below) so I can give more precise guidance or calculate estimated tax due:
   - Did you pay the $4,800 in student loan interest during 2023, or is that an aggregate for 2016–2020? Only 2023 payments matter on your 2023 return.
   - Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” employee expenses for your W‑2 job or business expenses for the marketing work? (If employee, they are not deductible; if business, include on Schedule C.)
   - Do you have any mortgage interest, property taxes, large state/local taxes, or other itemizable amounts beyond the items you listed?
   - Do you receive any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for the $22,100, and do you have other business expenses (phone/internet portion, supplies, advertising, mileage)?
   - Do you want me to compute an estimated federal tax liability (or refund) using the numbers you provided?
2. Gather records: W‑2, all 1099s, bank/broker 1099s for interest/dividends, receipts for business expenses, home rent/utility bills, receipt from Habitat for Humanity, student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E if available).
3. Decide home‑office method (actual vs simplified). Given your inputs, actual method looks better; you’ll need to prepare Form 8829.
4. Consider whether you owe estimated taxes for self‑employment income (you may need to pay quarterly or expect tax due when filing).

If you want, I can:
- Calculate more exact estimated federal tax and self‑employment tax given confirmation of the points above.
- Prepare a checklist of all forms and line items for your tax preparer or for use with a tax software package.
- Draft a summary you can hand to a CPA.

Which of those would you like next? And please confirm the timing of the $4,800 student‑loan interest and whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses.

