I can walk you through how these items are treated for 2023, what forms you’ll need, and whether itemizing will likely help. A few quick clarifying questions first (answers will let me run numbers more precisely):

- Is the $4,800 in student loan interest paid in tax year 2023, or is that total interest paid in earlier years (2016–2020)? Only interest paid in 2023 is eligible for the deduction on your 2023 return.
- The $1,350 labeled “unreimbursed expenses” — were those expenses for your self-employment/marketing work (i.e., business expenses), or were they unreimbursed expenses related to your W-2 hospital job? (Employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for 2018–2025.)
- Do you have any other potential itemizable amounts for 2023 (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax paid, large medical expenses, etc.)?

Summary of tax treatment and recommended forms (based on what you provided)

1) Income to report
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 — reported on Form 1040 via the W-2.
- Self-employment/marketing income: $22,100 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, include those; regardless of receiving 1099s, you must report the income.
- Interest: $920 (Chase savings) and $290 (TD Ameritrade) — reported on Form 1040 (1099-INT / 1099-DIV / 1099-B as applicable).

2) Self-employment tax and related deduction
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self-employment (SE) tax. Use Schedule SE to calculate SE tax (Social Security + Medicare ~15.3% on ~92.35% of net SE earnings).
- You can deduct one-half of your SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).

3) Home office (only if the marketing income is self-employment)
- You appear to meet the “exclusive and regular use” requirement (130 sq ft used only for business).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per square foot (2023 rate) up to 300 sq ft. For 130 sq ft = $650 deduction. You do NOT file Form 8829 if you use simplified method; just report the deduction on Schedule C.
  b) Regular/actual-expense method: allocate actual home expenses by business-use percentage. Your business-use % = 130 / 620 = ~20.97% (~21%). If you pay $1,720/month rent = $20,640/year, the deductible portion of rent = 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330. You can also allocate a share of utilities, renters insurance, etc. If you use actual-expense method you generally complete Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C. (Form 8829 is required to calculate allowable deduction and any carryover.)
- If you are an employee (W-2) performing some marketing as part of employment and those expenses are “unreimbursed employee expenses,” those are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. Home office deduction for employees is effectively unavailable.

4) Charitable contribution
- $1,350 cash gift to Habitat for Humanity — deductible only if you itemize (on Schedule A). Keep the receipt. There is no 2023 above-the-line charitable deduction for non-itemizers (the one-year special rules passed earlier have ended).

5) Student loan interest
- Up to $2,500 of student loan interest paid in 2023 can be deducted as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1 / Form 1040), subject to income phaseouts. For MFJ the phaseout begins at MAGI around $145,000 and is fully phased out around $175,000 for 2023. You must have actually paid the interest in 2023 and receive Form 1098-E from the lender. If the $4,800 you mentioned is not 2023 payments, it isn’t deductible on 2023 return.

6) Itemize vs standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly for 2023)
- Standard deduction (2023) for Married Filing Jointly: $27,700.
- Based on the items you gave: charitable $1,350 and (possibly) student loan interest is not an itemized deduction anyway (it’s an adjustment), so your known itemizable amount is far below $27,700. Unless you also have sizable mortgage interest, property taxes, or state & local taxes (SALT) that push total itemized deductions above $27,700, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction. In short: likely take the standard deduction unless you have substantial mortgage interest, large property taxes, state income tax payments, or other large deductible expenses.

7) Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for adjustments (half SE tax shown on Schedule 1)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for your marketing/self-employment income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual-expense method for home office (not required with simplified method)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize deductions
- Supporting forms/1099s/1098s:
  - W-2 from Riverside Hospital
  - 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC or records of 1099s for self-employment income
  - 1099-INT from Chase (and any 1099s from TD Ameritrade — 1099-INT / 1099-DIV / 1099-B)
  - 1098-E (student loan interest statement) if you paid interest in 2023
  - Receipts for charitable contributions
  - Documentation for home office (floor plans, square footage calculations, lease/rent receipts, utility bills, etc.) and for any business expenses

8) Recordkeeping & compliance notes
- Keep documentation proving exclusive/regular business use of the 130 sq ft (floor plan, photos, log).
- Keep receipts for the $1,350 charitable contribution and any business expenses.
- If you choose the actual-expense home-office method, keep copies of rent payments and utility bills (the IRS expects records).
- If any of your clients didn’t issue 1099s, you still must report the income.

9) Estimated tax / payments
- If you owe self-employment tax and additional income tax (because of the self-employment income), check whether any estimated tax payments were required or made during 2023 to avoid underpayment penalties. If no estimated payments were made and tax is owed, you may owe both income tax and SE tax when you file.

10) Rough illustrative calculation (approximate — using assumptions)
If your self-employment net before business deductions is $22,100 and:
- You treat the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses as business expenses (i.e., deductible on Schedule C),
- You use the actual home-office allocation of rent ($4,330),
Then:
- Schedule C net profit ≈ 22,100 − 4,330 − 1,350 = 16,420.
- SE tax approx = 15.3% × (0.9235 × 16,420) ≈ $2,320; half SE tax ≈ $1,160 (deduction).
- AGI roughly = W-2 + interest + net SE (79,400 + 1,210 + 16,420) − half SE tax − student loan interest (if eligible & assuming up to $2,500) … etc.
But these are rough numbers. If instead you use the simplified home-office deduction ($650), net profit is higher and SE tax increases accordingly. I can compute more precise tax estimates if you want — but I’ll need the clarifications asked at the top and any other deductible items.

