Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- You will almost certainly take the 2023 standard deduction (married filing jointly = $27,700) unless you have large additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, high SALT withheld/paid, large medical expenses).  
- Your self-employment (marketing) income needs a Schedule C and Schedule SE (self-employment tax). If the 130 sq ft space is used regularly and exclusively for that business, you can take a home-office deduction — either the simplified method ($5 × 130 = $650) or the actual-expense method (your portion of rent/utilities, calculated on Form 8829).  
- Student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (max $2,500) only for interest you actually paid in the tax year and subject to AGI phaseouts. Interest paid in prior years is not deductible now.  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. If the $1,350 is a self‑employment/business expense, deduct it on Schedule C.

What you gave me (summary)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly, Chicago, IL.  
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Riverside Hospital)  
- Self-employment (marketing) gross: $22,100 (6 clients)  
- Interest: $920 (Chase savings) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210  
- Home: rent $1,720/month for 620 sq ft; home office 130 sq ft exclusive  
- Student loan interest: $4,800 (Sallie Mae; you said 2016–2020 — see note below)  
- Charitable: $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity (you have receipt)  
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $1,350 (need to know whether business or employee)

How each item is treated (details)
- W-2 wages: reported on Form 1040 from your W-2 — straightforward.  
- Self-employment income (marketing): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Subtract business expenses on Schedule C to get net profit (this net profit flows to Form 1040 and is subject to income tax). You also compute self-employment tax on Schedule SE (Social Security & Medicare); you may deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income. You should have 1099-NEC/1099-MISC if clients issued them — include even if you didn’t receive a 1099.  
- Home office: valid only for self-employed/business use that is regular & exclusive. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × square footage (max 300 sq ft) => 130 × $5 = $650. Simpler recordkeeping.  
  - Actual-expense method: you take the business percentage of actual household expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Business percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97%. Example: annual rent = $1,720×12 = $20,640 → home-office portion ≈ $4,330 (20.97% × $20,640). To use actual expenses you normally complete Form 8829. The actual method usually gives a larger deduction here, but requires records. Note: home-office reduces your Schedule C net profit.  
- Interest income ($1,210): report on Form 1040. Schedule B is only required if taxable interest + dividends > $1,500 or other conditions apply. With $1,210 you likely do not need Schedule B. Expect 1099-INT / 1099-DIV from banks/brokers.  
- Student loan interest ($4,800): only interest actually paid in 2023 is potentially deductible (up to $2,500). Interest paid in prior years (2016–2020) is not deductible on your 2023 return. The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line adjustment on Schedule 1 and phases out for MFJ with MAGI roughly between $145,000 and $175,000 for 2023. From the income you reported, you’re likely under the phaseout and eligible for the deduction up to $2,500 if you actually paid that much in 2023. You should receive Form 1098-E from your loan servicer showing interest paid in 2023.  
- Charitable gift ($1,350): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). No universal above-the-line charitable deduction in 2023 for most taxpayers.  
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $1,350: if these are business expenses for your self-employment work, report them on Schedule C and they reduce self-employment income. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses as a W-2 employee, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 on federal returns.  
- State tax (Illinois): you must file IL-1040 and report the income. Illinois has a flat tax rate (report wages and business income on IL-1040). SALT (state and local taxes) paid can be itemized on federal Schedule A but the deduction is capped at $10,000.

Quick estimate (illustrative) — with reasonable assumptions
(You can skip if you want; I’m showing an example to illustrate itemize vs standard)
- Gross income: 79,400 (W-2) + 22,100 (self-employment) + 1,210 (interest) = 102,710.  
- Suppose business expenses = home-office actual portion (~4,330) + business expenses $1,350 = $5,680. Net self-employment = 22,100 − 5,680 = 16,420.  
- Total income then ≈ 79,400 + 16,420 + 1,210 = 97,030.  
- Self-employment tax roughly: 92.35% × 16,420 × 15.3% ≈ $2,320; half deductible ≈ $1,160.  
- Student loan interest deduction (if you actually paid $2,500 in 2023) = $2,500.  
- AGI estimate ≈ 97,030 − 1,160 − 2,500 = about $93,370.  
- Standard deduction (MFJ) = $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $65,670.  
- Itemized deductions (from what you listed) likely = charitable $1,350 + SALT up to $10,000 (unknown) = at most about $11,350 unless you have mortgage interest or big medical expenses — still well under $27,700. So standard deduction is almost certainly better.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the marketing income and business expenses  
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and deduction for half that tax  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual-expense method for the home office (not needed for simplified method)  
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and some other adjustments flow here  
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (probably you will not)  
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividends > $1,500 or other conditions apply (likely not needed here)  
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income; most taxpayers under the taxable-income thresholds use Form 8995)  
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), W-2, 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, 1099-INT / 1099-DIV, and receipts for charitable gifts and business expenses.

Illinois state
- IL-1040 (Illinois Individual Income Tax Return). Report your federal AGI and adjust per Illinois rules; Illinois taxes business income as well.

Recordkeeping and practical points
- You must have documentation: receipts for charity, records/receipts for business expenses, 1098‑E for student loan interest, and 1099s. Keep records for at least three years.  
