- Is the $1,720/month a rent payment (you’re a renter) or a mortgage + housing costs (you own)?  
- Was the $4,800 student‑loan interest paid in tax year 2023 (interest paid in prior years is not deductible on your 2023 return)?

If you want, answer those and I’ll re-run the numbers. Below I’ll assume (A) the $1,350 is self‑employment/business expenses, (B) the $1,720/mo is rent, and (C) the $4,800 is interest paid in 2023 — and I’ll show the impact under those assumptions plus alternatives.

Summary / quick answer
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) instead of itemizing, because the itemizable amounts you listed (charitable $1,350) are far below the standard deduction.  
- You must report the $22,100 marketing income on Schedule C (Form 1040) and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). Business expenses (the $1,350 if it’s business-related) reduce Schedule C net profit.  
- You can claim a home‑office deduction only if the space is used exclusively & regularly for the business. You can choose the simplified method (130 sq ft × $5 = $650) or the actual method (a share of rent/expenses). The actual method here gives a larger deduction (see numbers below), and you’d compute it on Form 8829 (or report the simplified amount directly on Schedule C).  
- Student‑loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (Schedule 1) up to $2,500 in most cases; it phases out at higher incomes — based on the numbers below, you’d likely be eligible for up to the $2,500 maximum (but confirm your exact MAGI).  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (unless you’re in a specific category—military, certain educators, etc.). If they were business expenses for your self‑employment, they reduce Schedule C net profit.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)  
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — student loan interest, half of SE tax adjustment)  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute Social Security/Medicare on self‑employment net earnings  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office (not needed for the simplified $5/sq‑ft method)  
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (likely you will not)  
- Schedule B — only if taxable interest > $1,500 (your $920 + $290 = $1,210, so probably not required)  
- State return: Illinois IL‑1040 (IL has a flat rate; IL taxation starts from Federal AGI with modifications)

Estimated numbers (with the assumptions above)
- W‑2 wages: $79,400  
- Self‑employment gross receipts: $22,100  
- Taxable interest: $920 + $290 = $1,210  
- Total gross income = $102,710

Home office calculation details
- Home size = 620 sq ft, office = 130 sq ft → office percentage = 130/620 ≈ 20.97% (≈21%).  
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Actual-method share of rent = $20,640 × 20.9677% ≈ $4,328. (Actual method could include utilities, renter insurance, repairs apportioned the same way; those increase the deduction further.)  
- Simplified method = 130 sq ft × $5 = $650.

Two example cases (both assume the $1,350 is a deductible business expense):

1) Using the actual home‑office method
- Business gross: $22,100  
- Less business expenses (other): $1,350  
- Less home‑office (actual share of rent as above): $4,328  
- Schedule C net profit ≈ $16,422  
- Self‑employment tax roughly = 15.3% × (0.9235 × net profit) ≈ $2,320  
- Deductible half of SE tax (adjustment to income) ≈ $1,160

2) Using the simplified home‑office method
- Business gross: $22,100  
- Less business expenses: $1,350  
- Less simplified home office: $650  
- Schedule C net profit ≈ $20,100  
- Self‑employment tax roughly ≈ $2,841  
- Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $1,420.50

Student loan interest
- The student‑loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction up to $2,500 per year (if you paid that much in 2023 and don’t exceed the MAGI phaseout). You mentioned $4,800 — only up to $2,500 can be deducted for 2023 (and only the interest you actually paid in 2023 counts).

Standard vs itemize
- Your listed itemizable amounts (charitable $1,350) are much less than the MFJ standard deduction ($27,700). Unless you have large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other itemizable amounts not listed, standard deduction will be better.

Other important notes
- If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are generally NOT deductible for 2018–2025. If they were business expenses related to the marketing/self‑employment work, they belong on Schedule C.  
- Report any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC you received for the $22,100 (or report the income even if you didn’t receive 1099s).  
- Keep receipts for the Habitat donation (you have that) and all business/home‑office documentation (dates, square footage, receipts for rent/utilities/repairs).  
- If you owe significant tax because of self‑employment (underwithholding), you may owe estimated tax penalties — check whether additional tax is owed when you file.

Next steps I can help with
- Recalculate using your answers to the three clarifying questions above.  
- Produce a line‑by‑line draft of how to enter these items on the federal return (Form numbers/where to put each figure).  
- Draft an email to a CPA with these details and ask for review/preparation.  
- Provide an estimated federal + IL tax bill using either home‑office method.

Disclaimer: I’m not a licensed tax professional. This is general guidance based on the facts you gave. For final filing and complex situations (e.g., special phases, depreciation, carryovers, or if you’re uncertain about whether expenses are employee vs business), I recommend consulting a CPA or tax preparer.

