I can help — here’s a clear, practical breakdown for your 2023 federal filing (married filing jointly) based on what you gave. I’m not a CPA; consider using this to prepare your return or bring to a tax professional for final filing.

Summary of your income (you provided)
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self-employment (marketing, 6 clients): $22,100 — treat as business income (Schedule C)
- Interest: $920 (Chase savings) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210 (report as interest income)
Total gross income to report on federal return: include all items above.

How each item is treated and what you can deduct
1. Self-employment income ($22,100)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., marketing costs, supplies, unreimbursed expenses that relate to this self-employment).
- Self-employment tax applies (Schedule SE) — ~15.3% on ~92.35% of net SE income. Half of your SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040.

2. Home office (130 sq ft exclusive; home = 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/mo)
- Because you’re self-employed for the marketing income, you can take a home office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business.
- Two methods:

  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 130 sq ft = $650 (you enter this directly on Schedule C). No Form 8829 needed.

  b) Actual expense method: allocate a percentage of allowable home expenses to the home office.
     - Business-use percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97% (≈21%).
     - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Rent portion for office ≈ 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330.
     - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, or other qualifying expenses, include the office portion of those as well.
     - Use Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) to compute and carry to Schedule C.
  - Note: actual method will likely give a larger deduction here (≈$4,330 just for rent) vs simplified $650. However, the home office deduction cannot create or increase a self-employment loss; it’s limited to net profit from the business. Any disallowed portion generally carries forward.

3. Unreimbursed expenses: $1,350
- If these are employee unreimbursed expenses (related to your W-2 job), they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 for most taxpayers (suspended by TCJA).
- If they are business expenses for your self-employment work, report them on Schedule C (deductible).

4. Student loan interest: $4,800 (Sallie Mae, 2016–2020)
- Important: only interest you paid in 2023 can be deducted on your 2023 return. Payments made in prior years (2016–2020) do not get deducted on 2023 returns.
- The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (up to $2,500 in most cases) and is subject to MAGI phaseouts. You’ll need Form 1098-E from your loan servicer for interest paid in 2023.
- If you actually paid $4,800 in 2023, you can only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). If you paid none in 2023, you get no deduction this year.

5. Charitable donation: $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity (you have receipt)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt.
- If you take the standard deduction (likely), you cannot also deduct this.

6. Interest income ($1,210)
- Report on Form 1040. Schedule B is not required unless total interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 or you have certain special situations.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly: $27,700.
- Likely outcome with the info provided:
  - Your itemizable deductions appear small (you listed $1,350 charity; you didn’t list mortgage interest or large property taxes). Even with state and local taxes (SALT), remember the SALT cap of $10,000 (state income tax + property tax + local taxes combined).
  - Unless you have substantial mortgage interest, large property taxes, or other sizable itemized deductions, the standard deduction ($27,700) will very likely be larger than Schedule A itemized deductions.
- Important: home office deductions are NOT on Schedule A — they reduce your self-employment income on Schedule C regardless of whether you itemize or take the standard deduction.

Other items to consider
- QBI (Qualified Business Income) deduction: you may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income (from Schedule C). Eligibility and amount depend on taxable income and the type of business; you may be able to claim Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — consider this when preparing the return.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self-employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated tax (Form 1040-ES) for 2024 if withholding isn’t sufficient for 2023-level taxes. For 2023 return you’ll compute any tax due and see if you owed estimated payments or had underpayment penalties.
- Illinois state return: file IL Form IL-1040. Illinois uses federal AGI as a starting point; state tax is a flat rate (check the 2023 IL rate; confirm current rate and rules).

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — for adjustments such as deductible part of self-employment tax or student loan interest (if eligible)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report self-employment income and expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) — only if you choose the actual expense method for home office
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — for QBI deduction if eligible
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable gifts, SALT up to $10k, etc.)
- Schedule B — only if interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 (not required here based on numbers you gave)
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement (verify interest paid in 2023)
- IL-1040 — Illinois state return

Practical next steps / checklist
1. Confirm 2023 payments:
   - Did you pay any student loan interest in 2023? If yes, how much? Request Form 1098-E from Sallie Mae for 2023.
   - Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business-related (marketing self-employment) or expenses tied to your W-2 job?
   - Do you have other deductible expenses (mortgage interest, property taxes, state income tax withheld, major medical expenses, etc.)?
2. Gather documents:
   - W-2 from Riverside Hospital
   - 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC (if any) and records of the $22,100 self-employment receipts/expenses
   - 1099-INT / 1099-DIV statements
   - Receipts for Habitat donation and business receipts
   - Lease/rent statements, utilities bills (if using actual home office method)
3. Decide home office method:
   - If rent is your only allocable item and numbers you gave are accurate, actual method ( Form 8829 ) likely yields the larger deduction; confirm no limitation due to net business profit.
4. Consider tax payments:
   - Estimate your self-employment tax and income tax liability. If you expect ongoing self-employment income, consider making estimated tax payments (Form 1040-ES).
5. If you want, I can:
   - Run a rough estimated tax computation using the numbers you provided (estimate federal income tax + self-employment tax and whether you’ll owe).
   - Draft a summary of what to give a CPA or tax preparer.

If you want, tell me:
- Did you pay any student loan interest in 2023 (and how much)?
- Are the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses self-employment related or employee related?
- Do you have mortgage interest, property taxes, or other large deductions?
I will then calculate an estimated tax outcome and show step-by-step where to enter each item on the return.

