  - Subtotal = 97,034
  - Less half SE tax adjustment ≈ −1,160
  - Estimated AGI ≈ 95,874
- Standard deduction −27,700 → taxable income before QBI ≈ 68,174
- Less estimated QBI (≈3,285) → taxable income ≈ 64,889
- Income tax would then be computed on that taxable income. (I’m not calculating tax liability here unless you want me to.)

9) Forms you’ll likely need for 2023
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — e.g., self‑employment tax deduction, student loan interest if applicable)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) for the $22,100 self‑employment income and business expenses (including home office)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the regular method for the home office deduction (computes allowable portion of rent/insurance/utilities, etc.). Use Form 8829 only for regular method (not for simplified method).
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for the Qualified Business Income deduction
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize
- Attach copies of any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients, 1099‑INT (Chase), 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B (TD Ameritrade if applicable), and 1098‑E for student loan interest (if any paid in 2023).

10) Other practical items and filing considerations
- Estimated taxes and withholding: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe estimated quarterly taxes in addition to what’s withheld on your W‑2. Check whether you made estimated payments in 2023 or had sufficient withholding to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Keep documentation: receipts for business expenses, the Habitat donation receipt, 1098‑E (student loan interest for 2023 if any), and any 1099s.
- If you rent, you cannot deduct property taxes or mortgage interest, but you still can deduct your share of rent allocable to the home office as a business expense.
- If any of the TD Ameritrade activity includes sales of securities (capital gains or losses), you’ll need Form 8949 and Schedule D; if only dividends/interest, report them on 1040 lines for interest/dividends.

11) Clarifying questions I need to finalize exact numbers
- Did you pay any student loan interest in calendar year 2023? If yes, how much and do you have Form 1098‑E for 2023?
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to your marketing work (self‑employment)? Or were those employee expenses for your W‑2 job?
- Is the $290 from TD Ameritrade interest, dividends, or did you sell investments (capital gains/losses)? Do you have 1099‑DIV/1099‑B?
- Do you have other deductible items (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax or state/local taxes withheld, larger charitable gifts) not yet listed?
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC for the $22,100 or were you paid via other means?

