If you confirm those, I can run exact estimates. Below is how each item is treated and what to expect.

1) Income you must report
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 — report on Form 1040.
- Self‑employment/marketing income: $22,100 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Interest from Chase savings ($920) and TD Ameritrade ($290) — report on Form 1040 (you’ll typically receive 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B).

2) Home office (you said 130 sq ft exclusive; home = 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/mo)
- You qualify for a home‑office deduction only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your business and it is your principal place of business (self‑employment). It does not apply to a W‑2 job unless you’re an independent contractor/owner.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 130 sq ft → 130 × $5 = $650.
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate actual home expenses by business-use percentage.
     - Business-use percentage = 130 / 620 = 0.2097 (≈21.0%).
     - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Rent portion for office ≈ 21% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330.
     - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., you can allocate the business share as well; that increases the deduction.
- Which to choose: the actual method usually gives a larger deduction here (≈$4,330 vs $650) if you can substantiate rent/utility amounts. Use Form 8829 for actual expenses (or the Schedule C worksheet for the simplified method). Home-office deduction reduces your business net income on Schedule C (and therefore reduces self‑employment tax).

3) Self‑employment expenses and “unreimbursed expenses”
- If the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your marketing work, they belong on Schedule C (office supplies, advertising, mileage, etc.). They reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, those are generally NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions). So only deductible if they’re business expenses from self‑employment.

4) Student‑loan interest ($4,800)
- Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible on the 2023 return, up to $2,500 (above‑the‑line adjustment to income) and subject to income limits (phaseout based on MAGI).
- If your $4,800 were paid in prior years (2016–2020), they are not deductible on the 2023 return — you can only deduct interest paid in the year you’re filing for.
- If you qualify, the deduction is reported as an adjustment on Schedule 1 (not on Schedule A).

5) Charitable gift ($1,350 to Habitat for Humanity)
- Deductible if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct this on 2023 federal return (the temporary universal charitable deduction that existed in 2020 does not apply in 2023).
- Keep the receipt.

6) Itemize vs. Standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Compare your total itemizable deductions (Schedule A): charitable gifts ($1,350), state & local taxes paid (SALT; limited to $10,000), mortgage interest, property taxes, medical expenses above threshold, etc.
- With only $1,350 charitable and no large mortgage interest/property taxes listed, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction ($27,700). Home office and business expenses are not Schedule A items — they reduce business income on Schedule C (or are an above‑the‑line adjustment), so they don’t change the choice to itemize vs standard in most cases.

7) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (Social Security/Medicare) computed on Schedule SE; the SE tax is ~15.3% of net SE income (with the usual computation using 92.35% of net profit).
- You may deduct one‑half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — additional income and adjustments (student-loan interest deduction, half SE tax, etc).
- Schedule C — report self‑employment income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — to compute actual home‑office deduction (if you use actual expenses). If you use the simplified method, you don’t use Form 8829.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (unlikely given your numbers unless you have other large itemized items).
- 1099‑NEC or 1099‑Misc / 1099‑K (you should have any 1099s you received from clients/platforms), 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV/1099‑B from broker.
- Illinois state return (IL‑1040) — you’ll file state income tax in Illinois. State rules differ; check IL instructions for deductions/credits.

9) Recordkeeping and other tips
- Keep receipts for the Habitat donation, business expenses, rent/utility bills, and student‑loan interest statements (Form 1098‑E if available).
- If you’re self‑employed, consider quarterly estimated tax payments for 2024 if you expect similar income (to avoid underpayment penalties).
- For the home-office actual method you’ll need documentation for all allocated expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs).

10) Example quick estimate (if the student‑loan interest paid in 2023 is up to $2,500 and unreimbursed $1,350 are business expenses)
- Business income: $22,100. Subtract home‑office actual rent portion ≈ $4,330 and $1,350 other business expenses → business net ≈ $16,420.
- That $16,420 is then subject to SE tax and income tax; half of SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1.
- Even with these business deductions, standard deduction $27,700 will likely be larger than any itemized Schedule A amount you showed.

