Thanks — I can walk you through how these items are treated for your 2023 federal return and what forms you’ll likely need. A few quick clarifying questions first (they change the answers):
- Is the $22,100 “marketing” income self‑employment income you received as an independent contractor (1099‑NEC / you ran the business), or was it reported on W‑2/other?  
- The $4,800 student‑loan interest — is that interest paid in 2023, or is that a total paid over 2016–2020? (Only interest paid in 2023 is eligible for the 2023 student‑loan interest deduction.)  
- The $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” — are those expenses from your self‑employment/marketing work (business expenses you paid), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job?

Below is guidance based on the usual situations and the information you gave.

1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages: report $79,400 on Form 1040 (from Riverside Hospital W‑2).  
- Self‑employment/marketing income: report $22,100 on Schedule C if it’s self‑employment. (If it was reported on a W‑2 or you were an employee, treat differently — clarify above.)  
- Interest: include $920 (Chase) and $290 (TD Ameritrade) on Form 1040. (Total $1,210 < $1,500, so you normally do not need Schedule B unless other triggers apply.)

2) Home office
- Because you said the office is used exclusively (130 sq ft) and your home is 620 sq ft, you meet the exclusive/regular use test for a home office. That fraction is 130/620 ≈ 20.97%.  
- If the marketing income is from self‑employment, you can take a home office deduction on your Schedule C. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 (easy, no Form 8829).  
  - Actual expense method: allocate a share of rent and other home expenses to the business. Example using rent only: rent $1,720 × 12 = $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,330. You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc. (If you use the actual method you’ll complete Form 8829.)  
- Note: If the marketing income is NOT self‑employment (i.e., it’s employee pay with a W‑2), you generally cannot claim a home office deduction on your federal return.

3) Self‑employment tax and business expenses
- If the $22,100 is self‑employment income, you’ll file Schedule C for that business. Business income minus business expenses = net profit. Net profit is subject to income tax and self‑employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE).  
- Business expenses you paid for that work (advertising, supplies, the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses if they were business expenses, phone portion, etc.) belong on Schedule C and reduce net self‑employment income.  
- You can deduct half of your SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (so it reduces AGI).

4) Student‑loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (Schedule 1), not an itemized deduction. For 2023 the maximum deduction is $2,500 (not $4,800). There is an income phaseout for married filing jointly (the deduction phases out at higher MAGI — if your MAGI is under the lower threshold you can take up to $2,500). You must have paid student loan interest in 2023 and should receive Form 1098‑E from Sallie Mae showing the interest you paid in 2023. If the $4,800 is not interest paid in 2023, you cannot claim it on the 2023 return.

5) Charitable contribution ($1,350)
- Charitable gifts are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You said you have a receipt — keep it. If you take the standard deduction, this $1,350 won’t reduce your federal tax.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($1,350)
- If these are unreimbursed expenses as a W‑2 employee, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law (except for a few specific categories like certain Armed Forces reservists, qualified performing artists, etc.).  
- If they are business expenses for your self‑employment work, report them on Schedule C.

7) Standard deduction vs. itemize (quick assessment)
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.  
- Your itemizable items you reported: charitable $1,350 plus any state/local income tax (SALT) and property tax. You didn’t list state tax withheld or mortgage interest. Illinois income tax could be roughly ~4.95% of your income paid or withheld; even with SALT, your itemized total based on the amounts you provided will likely be far less than $27,700. So in most cases you will take the standard deduction.  
- Takeaway: Unless you have large mortgage interest, high medical expenses, or substantially more charitable/SALT/property taxes, standard deduction is almost certainly better.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040.  
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments to income (e.g., student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax is reflected on Schedule 1).  
- Schedule C — to report self‑employment income and business expenses (if the $22,100 is self‑employment).  
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8829 — only if you use actual expense method for home office (otherwise use simplified method on Schedule C).  
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (probably you will not).  
- Keep 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if any) and 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / Form W‑2 / Form 1098‑E (student loan interest).

9) State tax (Illinois)
- File IL‑1040 for Illinois. Illinois does not allow many of the same credits/deductions as federal; check IL requirements. Rent is not deductible on IL returns. Report all income per IL rules; taxes withheld on W‑2 will be on your W‑2.

10) Next steps I recommend
- Confirm the three clarifying points I asked above (2023 student‑loan interest, whether the $22,100 is self‑employment, and what the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are for). I can then run a quick worksheet estimating:
  - Schedule C net profit, home office deduction (actual vs simplified), self‑employment tax, taxable income, and which deduction (standard vs itemize) is best.  
- Gather Forms: W‑2, any 1099s, 1098‑E for 2023, receipts for charitable gift, records of rent/utilities for home office, and receipts for business expenses.  
- Consider whether you owe estimated tax payments for self‑employment — if you had no/insufficient estimated payments in 2023, you may owe underpayment penalties.

11) A couple of cautions
- Student‑loan interest: the $2,500 cap and income phaseouts apply; you must have paid interest in 2023 and have the 1098‑E.  
- Home office: must be exclusive and principal place of business or for clients. If audited, keep documentation (floor plan, square footage, utility bills, photos).  
- If you’re unsure about any of the above or your situation is more complex, consider consulting a CPA or tax preparer—especially to handle self‑employment tax planning and to ensure state filing is correct.

If you’d like, tell me the answers to the three clarifying questions and I’ll build a sample worksheet with numbers (net Schedule C profit, home office deduction (actual vs simplified), estimated self‑employment tax, and a recommendation on standard vs itemize).

