I can walk you through how these items are treated and what to file — and give a worked example so you can see likely results — but first a couple quick clarifying questions (they change the calculations and what’s allowed):

Clarifying questions
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses you paid for your self‑employed work (the marketing clients)? Or are they employee unreimbursed job expenses (for a W‑2 job)?
- Was the $4,800 of student‑loan interest paid in tax year 2023 (and reported on a 1098‑E for 2023)? Or is that an aggregate from earlier years?
- The $290 from TD Ameritrade — was that interest, dividends, or proceeds from sales (you should have a 1099 from them)?
- The $1,720/month is rent you pay for your home, correct (not a mortgage payment)?

I’ll assume for the example below that:
• The $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your self‑employment (so deductible on Schedule C).
• The $4,800 is total interest paid in 2023 (I’ll show the limitation that applies).
• The $290 is investment interest/interest/dividend reported on a 1099 (treated as investment income).
If any of those assumptions are different, tell me and I’ll recalc.

Which items are deductible and how
- W‑2 wages ($79,400): taxable as reported on Form W‑2. Employee unreimbursed expenses are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA). So if those $1,350 are employee expenses, you cannot deduct them on your 2023 return.
- Self‑employment income ($22,100): report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., business supplies, the portion of home rent & utilities allocable to a home office if used for the business, etc.). That reduces self‑employment net profit.
- Home office: you have two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft: 130 sq ft × $5 = $650 deduction.
  - Regular (actual) method: take the business percentage of home expenses. Your business percentage = 130 / 620 = 20.9677% ≈ 21%. If your rent is $1,720/mo → annual rent $20,640. Business portion ≈ $20,640 × 21% ≈ $4,330. (If you pay utilities or renter’s insurance, a business portion of those can also be deducted.)
  - Note: home‑office deduction for a self‑employed person is taken on Schedule C and cannot exceed net profit from that business (there are carryover rules for depreciation in some cases). Because the actual method gives a much larger deduction here, it will usually be preferable if you have enough net business income to absorb it.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings; you pay Schedule SE and get to deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest: the max deduction is $2,500 (above‑the‑line on Schedule 1) and it phases out for Married Filing Jointly at MAGI roughly $145k–$175k in 2023. You cannot deduct more than $2,500 even if you paid $4,800 in 2023. Also you can only deduct interest paid in the tax year and typically need Form 1098‑E from the lender.
- Charitable contribution ($1,350 to Habitat for Humanity): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you generally cannot deduct charitable gifts (no universal above‑the‑line charity deduction for 2023).
- Interest from Chase ($920) and TD Ameritrade ($290): report on Schedule B (if required) and include as ordinary income. If TD Ameritrade payments are dividends or sales proceeds, they may be reported on 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B and have their own treatment.
- State and local taxes (SALT): deductible on Schedule A up to $10,000 if you itemize. You didn’t list property tax or state tax withheld — that affects whether itemizing makes sense.

Itemize vs. Standard (quick verdict)
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your listed potential itemized deductions (as given): charitable $1,350 and student loan interest does not go on Schedule A (it’s an adjustment), so itemized total looks small unless you have mortgage interest, substantial state & local taxes, or large medical expenses (over 7.5% of AGI). Based on what you listed, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- The home office deduction is not part of Schedule A — it reduces self‑employment income on Schedule C whether or not you itemize. So choosing the standard deduction does not affect the home office deduction.

Worked example (approximate)
Assumptions:
- $1,350 is a Schedule C business expense (not an employee unreimbursed expense).
- Home office actual method used: rent business portion ≈ $4,330.
- Student loan interest deductible limited to $2,500 (assume you’re under the phaseout threshold).
Calculations (rounded):
1) Self‑employment net profit:
   - Gross self‑employment income: $22,100
   - Less business expenses (non‑home): $1,350
   - Less home office (actual): $4,330
   = Net Schedule C profit ≈ $16,420
2) Self‑employment tax:
   - Net earnings subject to SE tax ≈ 16,420 × 0.9235 ≈ 15,168
   - SE tax ≈ 15,168 × 15.3% ≈ $2,320
   - Deductible half of SE tax (adjustment to income) ≈ $1,160
3) Total income before adjustments:
   - W‑2 $79,400 + net SE profit $16,420 + interest $920 + TD $290 = $97,030
4) Above‑the‑line adjustments:
   - Half SE tax ≈ $1,160
   - Student loan interest deduction (max) = $2,500 (assuming you qualify)
   = Adjusted Gross Income (approx) = 97,030 − 1,160 − 2,500 ≈ $93,370
5) Standard deduction MFJ = $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $65,670

Notes on the example:
- SE tax owed ≈ $2,320 (you may have paid quarterly estimated taxes; if not, you’ll owe this when filing).
- The exact AGI and taxable income will move if any of the assumptions are different (e.g., the $1,350 were employee expenses and not deductible on Schedule C; or if the $4,800 student interest was not all paid in 2023; or TD Ameritrade amounts are capital gains rather than interest).

Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — for student loan interest deduction and the deduction for half of SE tax (reported here)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $22,100, your business expenses, and the home office expense
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute SE tax and deductible half
- Schedule B (if you have more than $1,500 of taxable interest/dividends or other required reporting)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given amounts shown), for charitable gifts, SALT up to $10k, mortgage interest, etc.
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / 1099‑K: if clients issued you 1099s, include those amounts on Schedule C
- 1099‑INT (Chase), 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B (TD Ameritrade) as applicable
- 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — if your lender issued one for 2023
- Form 1040‑ES if you need to pay/estimated taxes for self‑employment going forward

Practical next steps I recommend
1. Gather documents: W‑2, all 1099s (NEC, INT, DIV, B), 1098‑E, rent receipts or lease, receipts for the Habitat donation, receipts for all business expenses and home office calculation (measurements, rent, utilities).
2. Confirm whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the self‑employment work (Schedule C) or employee expenses (not deductible).
3. Decide home‑office method:
   - If you want simplicity and your home office deduction is small, use the simplified method ($650 here).
   - If you want the larger deduction and have good records (rent, utilities), use actual method (≈ $4,330 here).
4. Use the standard deduction unless you have more substantial itemizable items (mortgage interest, large SALT, big charitable giving).
5. Consider estimated tax payments for next year to cover quarterly self‑employment tax and income tax.
6. If you want, I can calculate a more precise tax estimate if you provide exact 2023 payments (student loan interest paid in 2023, exact type of the TD Ameritrade amount, whether you received any 1099s, and whether the $1,350 expenses were business expenses).

If you’d like, tell me the answers to the clarifying questions and I’ll re-run the numbers precisely and prepare a checklist of the exact forms and line‑by‑line items you should fill in. I’m not a CPA — if your situation is complex or you want a definitive tax return, I can help you prepare the numbers to give to a tax preparer.

