I’m not a CPA, but here’s a clear, practical breakdown of how your 2023 tax situation looks, what you can deduct, which filing method will likely be best, how to treat the home office, and the forms you’ll need. I’ll also list questions to clarify anything that affects the outcome.

Quick summary (bottom-line)
- You’ll report: W-2 wages $79,400; self-employment (marketing) income $22,100 (Schedule C); taxable interest/dividends $920 + $290.
- You likely cannot deduct the full $4,800 student loan interest — the maximum above-the-line deduction is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phase-out; your income appears below the phaseout).
- The $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized charitable deduction — only helpful if you itemize.
- “Unreimbursed expenses” can only be deducted if they are business expenses for the self-employment work (Schedule C). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.
- Home-office: you likely qualify (130 sq ft exclusive space in a 620 sq ft rental). You can choose simplified ($5/sq ft = $650) or actual (pro rata share of rent/utilities). Based on the rent you gave, the actual-method rent allocation (≈20.97% of $20,640 = ≈$4,329) is likely more beneficial.
- Most likely result: take the standard deduction (2023 MFJ = $27,700) instead of itemizing, but take the home-office business deduction on Schedule C (which reduces self-employment tax and income tax). You will also file Forms Schedule C and Schedule SE and can likely take the 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995) — see note below.

Detailed numbers — two home-office scenarios (rounded, estimates)
Inputs:
- W-2 wages: $79,400
- Self-employment gross: $22,100
- Interest (Chase): $920
- TD Ameritrade: $290
- Rent: $1,720/mo → $20,640/year
- Home office: 130 sq ft / 620 sq ft = 130/620 ≈ 20.9677%
- Business unreimbursed expenses: $1,350 (assumed business-related in these scenarios)
- Student loan interest: only up to $2,500 deductible (see note)
- 2023 standard deduction (MFJ): $27,700

Scenario A — simplified home-office method:
- Home-office deduction = 130 × $5 = $650
- Business expenses = $1,350 + $650 = $2,000
- Net Schedule C profit = $22,100 − $2,000 = $20,100
- Self-employment tax ≈ (20,100 × 0.9235) × 0.153 ≈ $2,841
- Deductible half SE tax ≈ $1,421
- Total income before adjustments = 79,400 + 920 + 290 + 20,100 = $100,710
- Adjustments: student loan interest $2,500 + half SE tax $1,421 = $3,921
- Estimated AGI ≈ $100,710 − $3,921 = $96,789
- After standard deduction taxable income ≈ $96,789 − $27,700 = $69,089
- Estimated federal income tax (rough) ≈ $7,851 + SE tax $2,841 → total ≈ $10,692

Scenario B — actual home-office allocation (rent share only shown, no other home costs provided):
- Rent portion allocable to office ≈ 20.9677% × $20,640 ≈ $4,329
- Business expenses = $1,350 + $4,329 = $5,679
- Net Schedule C profit = $22,100 − $5,679 = $16,421
- Self-employment tax ≈ (16,421 × 0.9235) × 0.153 ≈ $2,320
- Deductible half SE tax ≈ $1,160
- Total income before adjustments = 79,400 + 920 + 290 + 16,421 = $97,031
- Adjustments: student loan interest $2,500 + half SE tax $1,160 = $3,660
- Estimated AGI ≈ $97,031 − $3,660 = $93,371
- After standard deduction taxable income ≈ $93,371 − $27,700 = $65,671
- Estimated federal income tax (rough) ≈ $7,441 + SE tax $2,320 → total ≈ $9,761

Takeaway from the numbers:
- Using the actual-rent allocation for the home office yields a materially larger deduction and lower total tax than the simplified method in your situation (savings in the example ≈ $900).
- You are still likely better off taking the standard deduction ($27,700) rather than itemizing with only $1,350 charitable donations (unless you have significant state/local taxes, mortgage interest, medical expenses, or other itemizable amounts you haven’t listed).
- You can (and should) deduct legitimate business/unreimbursed expenses on Schedule C (but not employee unreimbursed expenses for a W-2 job).

Other important items and deductions
- Student loan interest: deduction limit is $2,500 (per return), phased out at higher MAGI. You should have Form 1098-E (or records) showing interest paid.
- Charitable deduction: $1,350 is deductible only if you itemize. Keep receipts and acknowledgement if any single gift ≥ $250.
- QBI (Qualified Business Income) deduction: as a self-employed taxpayer you likely qualify for up to 20% of QBI (Form 8995) given your taxable income appears well below phase-out thresholds — this can further reduce taxable income. Use Form 8995 (simple version) in most cases.
- Self-employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax on net self-employment profit; half of that is deductible above-the-line.
- Estimated taxes: if you didn’t have enough withholding to cover income + SE tax, you may owe estimated taxes or penalties. Use Form 1040-ES to pay quarterly for future years.

Which forms you need (federal and state)
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments — student loan interest is reported here)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report self-employment income and business expenses (including home office deduction)
  - Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
  - Form 8995 (QBI deduction) — or Form 8995-A if complex
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize
  - Form 4562 only if you claim depreciation (home owners who depreciate the part of the home used for business). As a renter you generally cannot depreciate the structure, so 4562 is likely not needed unless you have depreciable business property.
- State (Illinois):
  - IL-1040 (Illinois individual tax return)
  - Keep records of state taxes paid for SALT deduction if you itemize on federal (note SALT cap $10,000).

Documentation to collect and keep
- Form W-2 from Riverside Hospital
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC / 1099-K(s) for client payments (if any) and your own records/invoices showing the $22,100
- Brokerage consolidated 1099 (TD Ameritrade)
- 1099-INT (Chase) or bank statements showing interest if 1099 not issued
- 1098-E from Sallie Mae (student loan interest)
- Receipts for charitable donations (Habitat) and for any business expenses
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills to support actual home-office allocation
- Records of the unreimbursed expenses — clarify if these are employee or business expenses

Questions I need to clarify for precise advice
1. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” tied to your self-employment (marketing) work? Or were they unreimbursed employee expenses for the W-2 job? (If employee, not deductible; if business, deductible on Schedule C.)
2. Does the $1,720/month represent rent (tenant) or mortgage? You said you “pay” that — I assumed rent.
3. Is the $4,800 student loan interest the amount paid in 2023, and do you have a 1098-E? (Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible; maximum allowed is $2,500.)
4. Did you receive any 1099-NEC, 1099-K, or other forms from clients (helps confirm self-employment income)?
5. Any other deductible expenses (state income tax paid, property tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or business vehicle expenses)?

Recommendations / next steps
- If your “unreimbursed expenses” are business-related, report them on Schedule C and use the actual home-office method (allocate rent/utilities); it appears to save you tax versus the simplified method in your case.
- Take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable deductions (state tax paid, mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical expenses, etc.).
- Make sure to account for self-employment tax and consider estimated tax payments if you didn’t have sufficient withholding.
- Consider claiming the 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995) — most likely available given your income level.
- Keep all records (receipts, 1099s, 1098-E, rent/utility records) in case of IRS questions.
- If you want, I can run a slightly more precise calculation if you provide: actual business-only unreimbursed expense details, any state tax withholding/estimated payments, whether you received 1099s, and if anyone else (spouse) had income.

