Thanks — I can walk through this and show which items are deductible, whether itemizing will help, how to treat the home office, and which IRS forms you’ll likely need. I don’t have some details yet, so I’ll list assumptions and ask a few clarifying questions at the end.

Summary / quick answers
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) unless you have significant other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, larger charitable gifts, large state/property taxes, etc.).
- Student loan interest: the maximum deduction is $2,500 (an “above-the-line” adjustment) and phases out at higher incomes — you cannot deduct $4,800 for 2023 unless that amount was interest paid in 2023 and you’re under the income limits; only interest paid in 2023 is deductible on your 2023 return.
- Charitable gift: the $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity is deductible as an itemized charitable contribution (with a receipt), but it alone won’t beat the standard deduction.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $1,350) are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law (TCJA), unless they were actually business expenses for self-employment (see below).
- Self-employment income ($22,100) must be reported on Schedule C. You’ll also owe self-employment tax (Schedule SE) on net earnings, but you get to deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- Home office (130 sq ft exclusively used) — two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 per square foot = 130 × $5 = $650 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Actual: prorate allowed home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs, depreciation if you own) by the business-use percentage = 130/620 ≈ 20.97%. If the $1,720/mo is rent, annual rent = $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,330 (plus proportional utilities, etc.). Use Form 8829 for the actual method.
  - You must use whichever method gives you the larger net tax benefit (you can compare both). The home office deduction applies only to the self-employed (Schedule C); W-2 employees cannot claim it currently.

Illustrative numbers (approximate) — based on these inputs and a few assumptions
Assumptions used for examples below:
- All amounts you listed are yours (no spouse income).
- The $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the self-employment work (if they’re employee unreimbursed expenses, they’re nondeductible).
- The $1,720/month is rent (not mortgage). Interest and property-tax differences change the itemizing decision.

Total income (rough): W-2 $79,400 + self-employment $22,100 + interest $920 + $290 = $102,710.

Example A — simplified home office method:
- Business expenses: $1,350 + simplified home office $650 = $2,000
- Net self-employment income ≈ $22,100 − $2,000 = $20,100
- Self-employment tax (approx) ≈ $2,842 → half deductible ≈ $1,421 (adjustment to income)
- Student loan interest deduction (if eligible) up to $2,500 (adjustment)
- AGI ≈ $102,710 − $1,421 − $2,500 = $98,789
- Taxable income after standard deduction $27,700 ≈ $71,089

Example B — actual home office method (using rent allocation only):
- Home-office share of annual rent ≈ $4,330 + business expenses $1,350 = $5,680
- Net self-employment income ≈ $22,100 − $5,680 = $16,420
- Self-employment tax ≈ $2,321 → half deductible ≈ $1,160
- AGI ≈ $102,710 − $1,160 − $2,500 = $99,050
- Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ $71,350

Note: these two examples show the results can be close; simplified method often is easier and sometimes even gives slightly better overall tax depending on interactions with SE tax. Exact numbers will vary if you have other business expenses, spouse income, mortgage interest, property tax, or different student-loan interest paid in 2023.

Forms you’ll likely need for 2023
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income: student loan interest deduction and the deductible half of self-employment tax.
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — to report the $22,100 self-employment (marketing) income and the business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home, if you use the actual-home-expense method (not needed for simplified method).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (to claim the $1,350 charity and any other itemizable amounts). If you take the standard deduction, you don’t use Schedule A.
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC — if you received any; whether or not you did, you must report the self-employment income.
- 1099-INT — interest from Chase / TD Ameritrade (you’ll receive these from the banks).
- 1098-E — student loan interest statement from Sallie Mae (if they issued one showing 2023 interest).

Important special notes and documentation
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in 2023 is deductible for your 2023 return. Confirm the $4,800 you mentioned — if that was cumulative interest from 2016–2020, it isn’t deductible for 2023. Keep Form 1098-E from Sallie Mae for 2023 interest paid.
- Charitable contributions: keep the receipt (you said you have one). Cash gifts under $250 require a receipt; gifts of $250+ need written substantiation.
- Home office: you must use the space regularly and exclusively for business. Keep records showing square footage and home expenses (rent/mortgage, utilities, insurance, repairs) if you use actual method.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally not deductible currently unless they’re ordinary and necessary business expenses of your self-employment.
- Estimated taxes: if you are self-employed and didn’t have sufficient withholding, you may owe estimated taxes or penalties for 2023 — check whether you made quarterly payments.

What I need from you (quick clarifying questions)
1. Is the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” for your self-employment (business) or for W-2 employment? If it’s an employee expense, it’s not deductible for 2018–2025.
2. Is the $1,720/month payment rent or mortgage (owner)? If mortgage, do you have mortgage interest and property tax statements (Form 1098, property tax bills)?
3. Was the $4,800 student loan interest paid in 2023 or is that a cumulative amount from prior years (2016–2020)? If interest was paid in 2023, how much was paid in 2023?
4. Does your spouse have income we should include (any W-2s, 1099s, etc.)?
5. Do you prefer I run a more detailed calculation (I can compute estimated tax, total tax liability, and whether actual vs simplified home office is better given final numbers)?

If you want, I can:
- Recalculate with your answers above.
- Produce a checklist of documents to gather for filing or for an accountant.
- Draft an email to your accountant with these details and the receipts listed.

Would you like me to run exact numbers if you confirm the clarifying details?

