Details and how to treat each item

1) Income
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 — report on Form 1040 (W‑2).
- Additional income $22,100 (marketing, 6 clients) — this is self‑employment income and should be reported on Schedule C. Expect to pay self‑employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE) on net earnings from that activity.
- Interest: $920 (Chase) and $290 (TD Ameritrade) — report as interest income (1099‑INT / 1099‑B reporting as applicable).

2) Home‑office (130 sq ft exclusive; house/apartment 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/month)
- You appear to meet the main tests (exclusive, regular business use, principal place of business) for the self‑employed home-office deduction.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Regular (actual expenses) — you allocate a business-use percentage and deduct the business share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Business percentage = 130 / 620 = 20.97% (≈21%).
    - Example: annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Business portion ≈ 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,330.
    - You can include proportional utilities, renter’s insurance, and other allowable home expenses. For the regular method you generally complete Form 8829 and transfer the allowable deduction to Schedule C.
- The home-office deduction reduces net Schedule C profit, which lowers both income tax and SE tax (subject to rules). If your allowable home-office deduction would exceed your net self-employed profits, some of the deduction may be limited/suspended.

3) The $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses”
- If these were unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job: generally not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- If these were business expenses you paid as the self‑employed marketer (e.g., supplies, advertising, mileage, client costs), they are deductible on Schedule C — please confirm which they are.

4) Student loan interest $4,800 (Sallie Mae, 2016–2020)
- Only interest paid in tax year 2023 is deductible on the 2023 return. Interest paid in 2016–2020 is not deductible in 2023.
