I can help — here’s a clear breakdown of how your 2023 tax picture looks, what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, forms you’ll likely need, and the follow-up questions I need to give exact numbers.

Important: I’m not a CPA or enrolled agent. This is general guidance — for filing or complex situations please confirm with a tax professional.

1) Key facts I’m using (from your message)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (MFJ)
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self-employment/marketing income: $22,100 (6 clients)
- Interest: $920 (Chase savings) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210
- Home office: 130 sq ft exclusive use; total home = 620 sq ft
- Rent (or housing cost) stated: $1,720/month (I’m treating this as rent unless you tell me it’s mortgage)
- Student loan interest you noted: $4,800 (you listed years 2016–2020 — see questions)
- Charitable gifts: $1,350 (Habitat for Humanity, you have receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $1,350 (need to know whether these are employee expenses or business expenses)

2) What you can (generally) deduct
- Self-employment business expenses: Reasonable, ordinary, and necessary expenses related to your marketing work are deductible on Schedule C. If your $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses (e.g., advertising, software, supplies), deduct them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses tied to your W-2 job, they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Home office: If you are self-employed (you appear to be), you can take a home-office deduction for the portion of your home used regularly and exclusively for business. You have 130/620 = 20.97% of the home devoted to business.
  - Two methods:
    - Actual-expense method (Form 8829): allocate a share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Example (rent = $1,720/mo): annual rent = $20,640; business portion ≈ 20.97% → ≈ $4,328 of rent would be allocated to the home office and deductible on Schedule C (plus allocated utilities etc., if applicable). This is typically larger than the simplified method if your allocated rent/utilities are substantial.
    - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 130 sq ft = $650 (cap 300 sq ft). You’d choose whichever yields the larger deduction (subject to the rules and any limits).
- Self-employment tax: Your self-employment income is subject to self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You compute Schedule SE; half of that SE tax is an “above-the-line” deduction on Form 1040 (reduces AGI).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: If your marketing income is qualified business income (QBI), you may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995 or 8995-A). For most MFJ filers with modest business income below the phase-in thresholds, the simple 20% deduction applies. QBI is computed on net business income after business expenses (including the home office deduction).
- Student loan interest: The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (up to $2,500 in most cases for 2023). Important: you can only deduct interest actually paid during the 2023 tax year (and the deduction phases out at higher MAGI). Also, only up to $2,500 can be claimed on 2023 return even if you paid more.
- Charitable contribution: Your $1,350 gift to Habitat is an itemized deduction (Schedule A). You must have the receipt (you do).
- Interest income ($1,210) is taxable and reported on Form 1040 (1099-INT statements, if you received them).

3) Standard deduction vs. itemizing (likely result)
- Standard deduction for MFJ in 2023 = $27,700.
- Your itemizable items (based on info provided): charitable $1,350 + any state/local taxes (SALT) up to $10,000 + mortgage interest or property tax (you did not list any) + other medical/qualified items.
- Unless you have additional large itemizable amounts (significant state income or property taxes, mortgage interest, big medical expenses, etc.), the standard deduction ($27,700) is almost certainly larger than your itemized total. So recommendation: most likely take the standard deduction. If you provide state tax/property tax/mortgage interest totals, I’ll run a check with exact numbers.

4) Approximate effect (example scenario)
This is an illustrative calculation assuming:
- Self-employment business expenses = $1,350 (you reported)
- Home-office actual allocation (rent portion only) ≈ $4,328
Net self-employment income ≈ 22,100 − 1,350 − 4,328 = 16,422
Estimated SE tax ≈ $2,321 → half deductible ≈ $1,160
AGI roughly = 79,400 (W-2) + 16,422 (net SE) + 1,210 (interest) = 97,032
Less half SE tax (1,160) and (if eligible) student loan interest up to $2,500 → AGI ≈ 93,372
Standard deduction 27,700 → taxable income ≈ 65,672
Then QBI (20% of business income) could reduce taxable income further (approx $3,284 in this example).
Note: These are only illustrative and depend on whether the student loan interest you listed is actually paid in 2023 and whether your “unreimbursed expenses” count as Schedule C expenses.

5) Forms you will likely need for 2023 filing
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — e.g., student loan interest shows on Schedule 1
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report self-employment income and business expenses, home office expense flows here
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual-expense method; if you use the simplified method, report on Schedule C without Form 8829
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) for the qualified business income (QBI) deduction (likely Form 8995 if you are below thresholds)
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (seems unlikely based on current numbers)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if you received any for self-employment payments), 1099-INT (for interest), W-2 from Riverside, and 1098-E (student loan interest statement) if you received one for 2023
- If you paid quarterly estimated taxes in 2023, keep records (Form 1040-ES vouchers or payment confirmation)

6) Important tax rules/points and potential pitfalls
- Student loan interest: You can only deduct interest paid in 2023 on your 2023 return. If the $4,800 interest is for prior years (2016–2020), that cannot be claimed on your 2023 return. Look for Form 1098-E for 2023 — lenders usually provide it if you paid $600+ interest in 2023.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for W-2 job) are not deductible for 2018–2025. If they are business expenses for your marketing work, deduct on Schedule C.
- Home office requires exclusive and regular use for business and either principal place of business or used for meeting clients. You said the 130 sq ft is exclusive — so that sounds eligible.
- QBI deduction: depends on taxable income thresholds, type of business and wages/qualified property. With modest business income you will likely get the 20% deduction.
- Estimated tax: Because of self-employment income you may owe self-employment tax and additional income tax — consider quarterly estimated payments for 2024 to avoid penalties (for 2023 you’ll reconcile on your return).
- Keep receipts and documentation for donations, business expenses, home office calculations, and a 1098-E for student loan interest.

7) Next steps / questions I need to finalize exact recommendations
Please confirm:
- Did you actually pay $4,800 in student loan interest during calendar year 2023, or are those interest amounts from 2016–2020? Do you have a 1098-E for 2023?
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” related to your self-employment/business (marketing) or to your W-2 employment at Riverside Hospital?
- Is the $1,720/month rent a rental payment (you rent) or mortgage payment? If mortgage, do you have mortgage interest reported on Form 1098?
- Do you (or your spouse) have any state income taxes or property taxes paid in 2023 (approx amounts)? Any other deductible items (large medical bills, casualty losses, etc.)?
- Do you have 1099s for the 22,100? (If you were paid > $600 by any client, you may have a 1099-NEC.)
- Do both spouses have income, or is the $79,400 total household W-2? (You said married filing jointly — confirm if other spouse had income)

If you want, I can:
- Walk through a more exact calculation if you provide answers above and any additional expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance, state tax paid, mortgage interest, property taxes).
- Draft a checklist of documents to collect (W-2, 1099s, 1098-E, receipts, rent/mortgage statements) and steps to prepare.
- Provide a sample Schedule C / Form 8829 setup for your situation.

Which would you like next?

