Below is a clear breakdown for your 2023 federal return (married filing jointly). I’ll explain what’s deductible, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I assume the $22,100 is self‑employment/1099-type income (you said “marketing, 6 clients”) — if that’s actually W-2 income, tell me and I’ll adjust.

1) Summary of your reported 2023 income
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self‑employment/marketing income: $22,100
- Interest from Chase savings: $920
- TD Ameritrade: $290
- Total gross income = $102,710

2) Adjustments / above-the-line deductions
- Student loan interest: you paid $4,800, but the student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 per year. Because your combined income (~$102,710) is well below the 2023 phaseout for MFJ (phaseout begins at $145k), you should be allowed the full $2,500 student loan interest deduction. This is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1) and reduces your AGI.

3) Charitable contributions
- $1,350 cash to Habitat for Humanity — deductible as an itemized charitable contribution only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt (bank/charity record) — you’ll need it if you itemize.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are business expenses related to your self‑employment/marketing work, they belong on Schedule C as ordinary and necessary business expenses and are deductible against the $22,100.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on your 2023 federal return (miscellaneous itemized deductions for unreimbursed employee expenses were suspended through 2025).

5) Home office (130 sq ft exclusive; total home 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/month)
- Qualification: A home office deduction is allowed only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your business and is your principal place of business (or meets one of the other allowed tests). It can be claimed only for the self‑employment income (Schedule C), not for W‑2 employment.
- Percentage: 130 / 620 = 0.2097 → about 21.0% business use.
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Business portion of rent (regular method) ≈ $20,640 × 0.2097 ≈ $4,328.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 (no Form 8829). Easy, but smaller than the regular method in your case.
  - Regular method: allocate the business percentage (≈21%) of rent, utilities, renters insurance, etc. That yields roughly $4,328 from rent alone and may be larger than simplified.
- IMPORTANT: The home office deduction (regular method) cannot exceed your net business profit from that self‑employment business for the year. If your allowable business deductions would reduce the self‑employment profit to zero, the disallowed portion of the home office deduction may carry forward to future years (see Form 8829 instructions).
- If you use the regular method, compute and report the home office deduction on Form 8829 and transfer to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, report the simplified amount on Schedule C (no 8829).

6) Itemize vs Standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your known itemizable amounts: charitable $1,350 (you didn’t list mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax paid, large medical expenses, etc.). Based on the information given, your itemized deductions are far less than $27,700, so the standard deduction will be better.
- Conclusion: Take the standard deduction unless you also have significant additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, or much larger charitable giving). Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction and does not affect the itemize decision (it reduces AGI regardless).

7) Self-employment tax and estimated payments
- Because you have self‑employment income, you will likely owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings and should pay estimated taxes if you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file. You’ll file Schedule SE to compute self‑employment tax; half of that SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- W-2(s) from Riverside and any other employers (attach per instructions).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student loan interest (and other items).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report $22,100 self‑employment income and related business expenses (unreimbursed expenses, home office deduction, etc.).
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home, if you use the regular method to calculate the home office deduction. (If you use simplified method, you do NOT file Form 8829.)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (doesn’t look beneficial here).
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (for the self‑employment income if issued), 1099‑INT (Chase), 1099‑DIV/1099‑B (TD Ameritrade) — report these on Form 1040.
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) showing interest paid; that supports the $2,500 student loan interest deduction.

9) State tax
- You will also file Illinois Form IL‑1040 (state return). Illinois taxes income (flat rate) and will want your federal AGI as a starting point. Also report interest and self‑employment income on the IL return.

10) Recordkeeping & documentation
- Student loan interest statement (1098‑E) for the $4,800 interest (you’ll use only $2,500 for the federal deduction).
- Charity receipt for $1,350.
- Receipts/invoices for the $1,350 business expenses and any other business cost.
- Rent statements and any utility/renter’s insurance bills to support the home office percentage.
- 1099s, W-2s, bank/brokerage forms.

11) Recommendations / next steps
- Likely take the standard deduction ($27,700) for 2023.
- Put business expenses ($1,350) and home office (choose simplified $650 or regular ~$4,328) on Schedule C — but remember the home office deduction cannot exceed the profit of that business.
- File Schedule SE and pay self‑employment tax; make estimated payments this year if you expect to owe tax for 2024.
- If you want, I can:
  - Estimate your federal tax and self‑employment tax owed based on assumptions (I can run through a rough calculation if you want).
  - Draft an email to your CPA listing these facts and asking for preparation help.
  - Prepare a checklist of documents to gather for filing.

If you’d like, tell me whether the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are for the self‑employment activity (marketing clients) or for your W‑2 job, and whether you want me to run a rough tax/SE tax estimate (I can use the home office regular or simplified method per your choice).

