I can walk through what’s deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal (and state) forms you’ll likely need — but first two quick clarifying questions (they change the result):

- Is the $4,800 in student loan interest the amount you actually paid in 2023, or is it a cumulative amount paid in earlier years (2016–2020)? Only interest paid in the tax year is eligible for the 2023 student‑loan interest deduction.  
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to your self‑employment (marketing clients), or are they employee unreimbursed expenses paid while working for an employer? (Employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for 2018–2025.)

Summary of your situation (what you gave)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (MFJ) — Illinois, Chicago  
- W‑2 wages: $79,400  
- Self‑employment (marketing) gross receipts: $22,100 (6 clients)  
- Interest / investment: $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210  
- Rent: $1,720/month for 620 sq ft → $20,640/year  
- Home office: 130 sq ft exclusive use (130 / 620 = 20.9677% of home)  
- Charitable contribution: $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity (you have receipt)  
- Student loan interest: $4,800 (see question above)  
- Unreimbursed expenses: $1,350 (need to know whether business or employee)

What you can (generally) deduct
- Self‑employment business expenses: Deduct ordinary and necessary expenses attributable to the marketing work on Schedule C. If the $1,350 is a business expense (e.g., supplies, advertising, travel for clients), it is deductible on Schedule C. If it’s unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, those are not deductible in 2018–2025.  
- Home office for self‑employment: Allowed if it is used regularly and exclusively for your trade and is your principal place of business. You have two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction.
  - Regular (actual-expense) method: allocate a share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Your rent share alone = 20.9677% × $20,640 ≈ $4,329. (Add your share of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet if you use it for business, etc.) Because your rent is fairly high, the regular method will likely give a larger deduction than the simplified method. Use Form 8829 (and Schedule C) to compute the regular-method deduction.
  - Note: Because you rent, you cannot claim depreciation on the residence; depreciation applies to owners.
- Self‑employment tax: You must pay self‑employment (SE) tax on net SE earnings (Schedule SE). The self‑employment tax is separate from income tax; however, you may deduct 50% of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest deduction: Up to $2,500 maximum (adjustment to income) for qualified interest paid in the tax year, subject to income phaseouts. For MFJ the phaseout for 2023 begins at MAGI around $145,000 and is fully phased out around $175,000 (if your MAGI is below the phaseout you may take the full $2,500). If your $4,800 is not interest paid in 2023, it is not deductible for 2023.
- Charitable contributions: Cash gift to a qualified charity (Habitat) is deductible if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct those gifts (with a few limited exceptions which generally don’t apply here).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) 199A: As a sole proprietor you may be eligible for a 20% deduction of qualified business income (QBI) subject to rules and income thresholds. With your income level as reported, you will likely be under the MFJ threshold for phaseouts, so you probably can claim a QBI deduction equal to 20% of your qualified business income (that is your net Schedule C income after allowable business deductions). QBI is complex (notably with wage/UBIA limits when income is high), but at your numbers it’s likely available.

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Standard deduction for MFJ (2023): $27,700.
- Likely itemizable items you listed: charitable $1,350. (State & local tax — SALT — is limited to $10,000; mortgage interest and large medical expenses were not listed.) Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment, not an itemized deduction.
- Conclusion (based on provided info): It is very likely advantageous to take the standard deduction ($27,700) because your listed itemizable amount ($1,350 charitable + any SALT up to $10k if you have state/local taxes withheld) is unlikely to exceed $27,700. The home office deduction does NOT factor into the decision to itemize vs. standard because it’s a business expense reported on Schedule C (it lowers AGI or taxable income through business income reduction, not via Schedule A).
- If you have large state income or property taxes (or other itemizable items you haven’t listed), re-evaluate.

Rough tax-impact estimate (approximate)
(These are illustrative approximations to show scale — I can run more precise numbers if you confirm the clarifying questions and any spouse income.)
- Example assumptions: $22,100 SE receipts, business expenses = home-office rent portion (~$4,329) + $1,350 business expenses = net SE profit ≈ $16,421.  
- SE tax (approx): net SE earnings × 92.35% × 15.3% ≈ ~$2,320. Half deductible (~$1,160) as adjustment to income.  
- Student loan interest: maximum $2,500 (if paid in 2023 and you’re under phaseout limits).  
- AGI effects and taxable income will then flow through standard deduction and possible 20% QBI deduction. If you’d like, I can run a line-by-line estimate after you confirm the two clarifying items (student loan interest year and whether the $1,350 is business expense).

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).  
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments such as student loan interest deduction and deduction for half of self‑employment tax.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $22,100 and business expenses, including home‑office deduction (or you can use the simplified method).  
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method to figure business use of home (home office). If you use the simplified method, you do not use Form 8829.  
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — if you claim the QBI deduction (Form 8995 is the simpler form generally used when under the threshold).  
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given your figures, but needed if you do).  
- Keep 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B / 1099‑NEC documents you receive from banks, brokerages, or clients — those support the interest and investment amounts and self‑employment income.

Illinois (state)
- Illinois: file IL‑1040, reporting your federal AGI and following IL instructions. Illinois has a flat tax rate (check current rate for the year; for 2023 it was 4.95%), and you will need your federal data to complete IL‑1040. Chicago has no local income tax.

Other practical notes and recommendations
- Keep documentation: receipts for the Habitat donation, a Form 1098‑E (or records) showing student loan interest paid in 2023, rent receipts and bills for utility allocations, and records for any business expenses. For home office, document the exclusive use and square footage. Keep 1099s and W‑2.  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible for 2018–2025. If those $1,350 are actually business expenses for your self‑employment work, claim them on Schedule C.  
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (and self‑employment tax). If you expect tax due when filing, consider estimated payments next year to avoid penalties.  
- If you want, I can run a detailed estimate of AGI, taxable income, SE tax, QBI deduction, and federal income tax based on the clarified answers (and any spouse income). I can also prepare a checklist of documents to give to a tax preparer or to upload to tax software.

Would you like me to:
1) Run a detailed numeric tax estimate now (I’ll need confirmation about the student‑loan interest year and whether the $1,350 is business expenses), or  
2) Prepare a checklist of all documents/forms to gather for filing, or  
3) Recommend next steps to claim the home office and set up bookkeeping going forward?

I’m not a CPA — this is general guidance. If you want definitive tax preparation or complex planning (state interactions, retirement contributions, large deductions, or QBI edge cases), I recommend consulting a tax professional.

