Thanks — I can help. Below is a clear, practical summary of what looks deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, what forms you’ll need, and a short worked estimate so you can see the effect. I’m not a tax preparer, so for final filing or if your situation has extra complications I recommend a CPA or tax software review.

Key facts you gave (used below)
- Filing: Married filing jointly, Chicago, IL
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self-employment / marketing income: $22,100 (6 clients)
- Interest: $920 (Chase savings) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210
- Home: rent $1,720/month for 620 sq ft; home office exclusive use 130 sq ft
- Student loan interest paid: $4,800 (Sallie Mae; 2016–2020)
- Charitable gift: $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity (you have the receipt)
- Unreimbursed expenses: $1,350 (unclear whether these are employee expenses or business expenses)

What you can (generally) deduct
- Self-employment business expenses: Deduct ordinary and necessary expenses directly related to your marketing business on Schedule C. That includes supplies, advertising, software, and any unreimbursed business expenses that relate to the self-employment work (if that $1,350 is for the business). If the $1,350 is an unreimbursed employee expense for your W-2 job, it is NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension).
- Home office: If the 130 sq ft is used exclusively and regularly for your marketing business, you can take a home office deduction on Schedule C. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650 (easy).
  - Actual: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses. Percentage = 130 / 620 = 21.0% (approx). If you use actual method and only have rent as the home expense shown ($1,720 × 12 = $20,640), the home-office portion of rent ≈ $20,640 × 0.210 = ≈ $4,328. The actual method usually yields a bigger deduction when rent is substantial. Form 8829 is used when calculating actual home-office expenses for Schedule C.
- Self-employment tax: You’ll owe self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self-employment earnings (reported on Schedule SE). You can deduct one-half of self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest: The maximum student loan interest deduction is up to $2,500 per year (above-the-line adjustment on Schedule 1), subject to income phaseouts. Even though you paid $4,800, the deductible amount is capped (and may be reduced by MAGI phaseouts). You’ll need Form 1098-E from the lender to support the deduction.
- Charitable contributions: Your $1,350 donation is deductible only if you itemize (reported on Schedule A). There’s no above-the-line charitable deduction for 2023 for most taxpayers.

Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Based on what you reported, you only listed $1,350 in charitable contributions and no mortgage interest or large state/local taxes beyond the SALT cap. That is far below the standard deduction, so in almost all cases you will take the standard deduction.
- Note: student loan interest and half of self-employment tax are above-the-line adjustments and reduce AGI whether or not you itemize.

Forms you will likely need to file for 2023
- Form 1040 — Individual income tax return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — For adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax, etc.).
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — Profit or Loss from Business (report self-employment income and related business expenses).
- Schedule SE — To calculate self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — If you use the actual expenses method for the home office (not used with simplified method).
- Schedule A — Only if you decide to itemize (e.g., if you have more itemizable deductions than the standard deduction).
- Schedule B — Only if required (interest/dividends > $1,500 or certain other conditions). Your combined interest ($1,210) is under $1,500 so Schedule B probably not needed, but the interest still gets reported on Form 1040.
- Form 1098-E — Student loan interest statement from Sallie Mae (used to compute the student loan interest deduction).
- 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC — If you received them for self-employment income (if not, you still report the income on Schedule C).
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Potential Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income), if you qualify — this may reduce taxable income further.

Rough numbers (illustrative estimate)
These are approximate to show how deductions interact. I make assumptions: the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business-related and you use the actual home office portion of rent. If your $1,350 unreimbursed were employee expenses, ignore that line for business deductions and results change.

