I can walk you through how these items are treated for 2023 and give a clear recommendation and an estimate — but I’m not a CPA or tax preparer, so consider this general guidance and check with a tax professional for filing.

Short answer / recommendation
- You should report the $22,100 marketing income as self‑employment (Schedule C) and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for that marketing activity, deduct them on Schedule C.
- The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (Schedule 1) but is limited to $2,500 for 2023 and only applies to interest paid in 2023 (not interest you paid in prior years).
- The $1,350 donation to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized charitable deduction (Schedule A) if you itemize.
- With the numbers you gave and no spouse income included, the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly in 2023 ($27,700) will almost certainly be larger than your likely itemized deductions (charity $1,350 + any state/local taxes, property tax, mortgage interest, etc.). So you will likely take the standard deduction.
- The home‑office deduction can reduce your self‑employment income; you can choose the simplified method ($5/sq ft → $650 for 130 sq ft) or the actual method (pro rata share of rent/utilities/etc.). The actual method in your case (130/620 ≈ 20.97% of rent) gives a substantially larger deduction of roughly $4,328 of your rent, but it must be computed on Form 8829 (or on Schedule C if using simplified). The home office deduction only applies to self‑employment (or certain other qualifying circumstances) and is subject to limits if it would create a loss for the business.

What gets reported where (forms)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule C — report self‑employment (marketing) income and business expenses (unreimbursed expenses, home‑office expense portion, supplies, etc.).
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You can deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Schedule 1 (Part II) — for adjustments to income such as student loan interest deduction (Form 1040’s adjustment).
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual method for business use of home (deduction for rent, utilities, etc.). If you use the simplified method you do NOT use Form 8829.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable gift $1,350 would go here).
- 1099s / 1099‑NEC / 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B — attach / report amounts shown on any information returns you received (interest $920 and $290 reported on 1099‑INT or 1099‑DIV/1099‑B as applicable). If total taxable interest is small, you still report it on Form 1040 (Schedule B only required if > $1,500).
- Form 1040‑ES — estimated tax payment vouchers if you expect to owe tax and need to make quarterly estimated payments for next year.

Important rules & limits to note
- Student loan interest: deductible only for interest you actually paid in 2023. The maximum deduction is $2,500 and it phases out for MFJ starting at higher MAGI (for 2023 the phaseout for MFJ is between $145,000 and $175,000 of MAGI). If your combined MAGI is below the phaseout start, you can take the full $2,500 (not $4,800).
- Home office: must be used regularly and exclusively for business. If you use actual method, allowable home office deductions cannot exceed net profit from that business (excess may be carried forward). If you use a willful simplified method, no Form 8829 is needed and it’s simpler to claim.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for W‑2 employees) are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law; only self‑employment business expenses are deductible on Schedule C.
- Self‑employment tax: roughly 15.3% on net self‑employment earnings (with the 92.35% adjustment), and you can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income.

Rough calculations (estimate) — using only amounts you gave and assuming:
- spouse has no other income (you said “married filing jointly” but you didn’t give spouse income),
- the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses,
- student loan interest is eligible but capped at $2,500 (because 2023 limit),
- the $920 + $290 = $1,210 interest is taxable.

Scenario A — simplified home‑office ($5/sq ft = $650):
- Net self‑employment profit = 22,100 − 1,350 (business expenses) − 650 (simplified home office) = 20,100
- Approximate self‑employment tax ≈ $2,840; half SE tax ≈ $1,420 (deductible)
- AGI ≈ wages 79,400 + net SE 20,100 − half SE tax 1,420 − student loan interest 2,500 = ~95,580
- Standard deduction (MFJ) = 27,700 → taxable income ≈ 67,880

Scenario B — actual home‑office (130/620 ≈ 20.97% of rent):
- Home office portion of rent ≈ 20.97% × $20,640 = ~ $4,328
- Net self‑employment profit ≈ 22,100 − 1,350 − 4,328 = ~16,422
- Approx SE tax ≈ $2,320; half SE tax ≈ $1,160
- AGI ≈ 79,400 + 16,422 − 1,160 − 2,500 ≈ 92,162
- Standard deduction 27,700 → taxable income ≈ 64,462

Takeaway from these estimates: using the actual home‑office method reduces taxable income more than the simplified method in this case — but the actual method requires documentation and Form 8829 and may be limited if the deduction exceeds net profit. Even with the actual method, your likely taxable income (after standard deduction) is still significantly lower than if you tried to itemize instead of taking the standard deduction, because your only listed likely itemized deduction is $1,350 in charitable giving.

Questions I need to finalize precise answers and prepare numbers you can use for filing
1. Is the $4,800 student loan interest amount the interest you paid during 2023, or is it the total interest paid in earlier years (2016–2020)? Only interest actually paid in tax year 2023 is deductible on the 2023 return, and the limit per return is $2,500.
2. Does your spouse have income? If yes, how much? (That affects MAGI and the student loan interest phaseout and other calculations.)
3. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the marketing/self‑employment work (i.e., expenses you paid as part of running your marketing business)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job? (If employee unreimbursed expenses, they’re not deductible for 2018–2025.)
4. Do you have other deductible items you didn’t list (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state income tax or local taxes, medical expenses, etc.) that would increase itemized deductions?
5. Did you receive 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC/1099‑INT or 1099‑DIV forms for the amounts you listed? (You should report income even if you didn’t receive 1099s.)

If you want, I can:
- Recalculate with your spouse’s income and answers to the clarifying questions and produce a more exact AGI/taxable income estimate.
- Draft a checklist of the records and forms you’ll need to file (what receipts to keep, which forms to look for — 1099‑NEC, 1099‑INT, Form 1098 if mortgage interest, etc.).
- Summarize line‑by‑line what to enter on Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if you pick actual method), and Schedule 1 for student loan interest.

Would you like me to run the numbers again if you tell me:
- whether the $4,800 was paid in 2023 or earlier,
- whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses,
- whether your spouse had any 2023 income (and how much)?

