I’m not a tax professional, but I can walk you through how these items are normally treated on a 2023 federal return and what forms you’d likely need. I’ll also show a quick worked example with the numbers you gave and flag a few questions where I need clarification.

Quick summary (short answers)
- Report W-2 wages ($79,400) on Form 1040.
- If the $22,100 is self-employment (1099/independent contractor) income, report it on Schedule C and pay self-employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE). If it’s employee income reported on a W-2, don’t use Schedule C.
- Home office: if you are self-employed and the office is used regularly and exclusively for business, you can deduct a portion of rent/utilities. For your numbers the actual-expense method gives a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Interest ($920 Chase + $290 TD Ameritrade = $1,210) is taxable interest reported on Form 1040 (Schedule B only required if interest+dividends > $1,500).
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in 2023 is deductible (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts). Interest paid in earlier years (2016–2020) cannot be deducted on 2023 return.
- Charitable gift of $1,350 deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers (TCJA suspended this deduction).
- Likely outcome: with the items you listed, you will probably be better off taking the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) unless you have additional itemizable deductions (large mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, big medical expenses over threshold, etc.).

Detailed treatment and forms
1) Self-employment income and business deductions
- If the $22,100 is self-employment income:
  - File Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) to report income and business expenses.
  - Home office: because you have exclusive use of 130 sq ft of a 620 sq ft home, business-use percentage = 130/620 ≈ 20.97%.
    - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
    - Actual home-office portion of rent = $20,640 × 130/620 ≈ $4,328. (You can also use the simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650; so actual method is better here.)
    - Report the home-office expense on Schedule C. (To compute allowable home-office deduction using actual expenses you generally complete Form 8829 — “Expenses for Business Use of Your Home”—which flows to Schedule C.)
  - Unreimbursed expenses of $1,350: if these are ordinary and necessary business expenses related to the self-employment work, report them on Schedule C (they reduce self-employment net profit).
  - Self-employment tax: net self-employment income is subject to SE tax (calculated on Schedule SE). You can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (Form 1040).
  - You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) — typically up to 20% of qualified business income — computed on Form 8995 or 8995-A depending on complexity.

2) Interest income
- Report $920 and $290 on Form 1040 as interest income. If total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500, use Schedule B; your total ($1,210) is below that threshold.

3) Student loan interest
- Only interest paid in tax year 2023 is deductible (maximum $2,500). You should have Form 1098-E from the lender for interest paid in 2023.
- For married filing jointly in 2023 the student loan interest deduction begins to phase out at MAGI $145,000 and is completely phased out at $175,000. If your 2023 MAGI is below $145k, you can take the full $2,500 (subject to actually having paid that much in 2023).

4) Charitable contributions
- Your $1,350 cash gift is deductible if you itemize and you have a receipt. If you take the standard deduction, you do not separately deduct the $1,350.

5) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- Under current law (post-TCJA), unreimbursed employee business expenses are not deductible for most employees. If those expenses relate to self-employment, they belong on Schedule C (as above). If they are employee expenses and not reimbursed, they are not deductible on your federal return (there are limited exceptions for some categories of employees).

6) Illinois state tax
- You’ll also file Illinois Form IL-1040; state rules differ (but you still report federal income as a starting point).

Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — e.g., half of SE tax, possibly student loan interest if that’s an adjustment)
- Schedule C (if $22,100 is self-employment income)
- Schedule SE (self-employment tax)
- Form 8829 (if using actual expenses for home-office deduction) — or you can use the simplified method directly on Schedule C
- Form 8995 or 8995-A (if claiming the QBI deduction)
- Schedule A (only if you itemize)
- Schedule B (only if interest + dividends > $1,500)
- Form(s) 1099-NEC/1099-MISC (if you received them), 1099-INT (for interest), W-2, 1098-E (student loan interest) — keep these for your records

Worked example (with the assumptions below)
Assumptions: $22,100 is self-employment income; $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses; the $4,800 student loan interest was paid in earlier years (2016–2020) and none in 2023 (so no student loan interest deduction for 2023). If you actually paid student loan interest in 2023, tell me and I will re-run the numbers.

- Business deductions:
  - Home-office (actual): $4,328 (rent × 130/620)
  - Other business expenses: $1,350
  - Total business expenses = $5,678
- Net self-employment income = $22,100 − $5,678 = $16,422
- Self-employment tax (approx):
  - SE tax base = 0.9235 × $16,422 ≈ $15,169
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × $15,169 ≈ $2,320
  - Deduction for half SE tax ≈ $1,160 (above-the-line on Schedule 1)
- Interest income = $920 + $290 = $1,210
- Preliminary AGI:
  - Wages $79,400 + net SE income $16,422 + interest $1,210 = $97,032
  - Minus half SE tax $1,160 → AGI ≈ $95,872

Itemize vs standard (quick check)
- Known itemizable amounts you listed: charitable $1,350. (Student loan interest — if actual 2023 interest exists — is an above-the-line adjustment, not an itemized deduction.)
- With only those itemizable items, total itemized < standard deduction for MFJ ($27,700) → standard deduction is larger. So you’ll likely take the standard deduction unless you’ve got substantial other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses above the AGI threshold, etc.).

Other practical notes
- If you are self-employed, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (Federal + Illinois) if withholding isn’t covering your tax and SE tax liability.
- Keep receipts and documentation: Form 1099s, W-2, 1099-INT, 1098-E, donation receipts, records for the home-office calculation (floor plans, square footage), and records of business expenses.
- Consider retirement contributions (SEP-IRA, solo 401(k)) which can reduce taxable/self-employment income and help with estimated tax planning.
- You may qualify for the 20% QBI deduction on net business income; it can further reduce taxable income (Form 8995).

What I need from you to finalize recommendations / run exact numbers
1. Was the $22,100 reported on 1099s (self-employment) or was it paid as wages (W-2)? Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” related to that work?
2. Did you pay any student loan interest in 2023? If so, how much and do you have Form 1098-E for 2023?
3. Do you (or your spouse) have mortgage interest, property taxes, or large state/local tax payments in 2023? Any other deductible items (medical expenses, large casualty losses, etc.)?
4. Do both spouses have income? (You provided one W-2; if spouse also has income, please provide amounts.)
5. Do you want me to estimate your federal tax due/refund for 2023 (I can run a rough calculation) or prepare a checklist of documents to give to a tax preparer?

