Thanks — I can walk through this. I’m not a CPA, so consider this general guidance and confirm with a tax professional, but based on what you gave here:

Quick summary answers
- Standard vs. itemize: You will almost certainly be better off taking the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) unless you have large additional itemizable deductions (significant mortgage interest, >$10k SALT, large medical expenses, or much bigger charitable gifts). Based only on the items you listed (charitable $1,350 and no other large itemized items reported) itemizing will be lower than the standard deduction.
- Student loan interest: The maximum federal student loan interest deduction is $2,500 in a year (above-the-line). You cannot deduct more than that on a 2023 return. Phaseouts apply based on MAGI — with the income amounts you provided you are likely under the phaseout, but I need your exact MAGI to confirm eligibility.
- Home office: If the marketing income is self-employment income, you can claim a home office deduction (you said the 130 sq ft office is used exclusively — that satisfies the “exclusive use” rule). Because you rent, the portion of rent attributable to the business is deductible on Schedule C (or you may use the simplified $5/sq ft method). If the marketing income is not self-employment (i.e., it’s W-2 work), the home office deduction is generally not allowed for 2018–2025.
- Unreimbursed expenses: Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W-2 job) are generally not deductible for 2018–2025. If those $1,350 are business expenses for your self-employment marketing work, they are deductible on Schedule C. Clarify which they are.
- Forms you will likely need: Federal Form 1040; Schedule 1 (adjustments) for above-the-line items; Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) for the marketing/self-employment income and expenses; Schedule SE (self-employment tax); Form 8829 (if you use the actual-expense method for business use of home) or you can use the simplified method (no Form 8829 if simplified). State: IL Form IL-1040. Also keep any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, 1099-INT (Chase), and brokerage 1099s.

Details and calculations (estimates)
Assumptions used here: your 6 marketing clients’ $22,100 is self-employed (Schedule C). Interest/other income included as reported. I treat the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses as business expenses (if they are employee unreimbursed expenses, see note above that they’re not deductible).

1) Home office calculation
- Home total = 620 sq ft; office = 130 sq ft => business percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97%.
- Rent = $1,720/month => annual rent = $20,640.
- Actual-expenses portion of rent for the office = 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,328 (deductible on Schedule C if you use actual-expense method).
- Simplified method would be 130 sq ft × $5 = $650. Because your actual allocated portion of rent is higher ($4,328), the actual-expense method gives a larger deduction in your case.

If you use actual-expense method you’d also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., by the same percentage; use Form 8829 to compute the allowable business-use-of-home deduction.

2) Self-employment and self-employment tax (approx.)
- Self-employment gross income: $22,100
- Less business expenses: home-office rent portion (~$4,328) + other business expenses you listed ($1,350) = $5,678
- Estimated net self-employment income ≈ $22,100 − $5,678 = $16,422
- Self-employment tax ≈ 15.3% × (net × 0.9235). Rough estimate: SE tax ≈ $2,320. Half of that (~$1,160) is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040.
- That reduces your AGI and reduces income tax and SE tax base.

3) Student loan interest
- You reported $4,800. The maximum deduction per year is $2,500 for 2023. Also the deduction only applies to interest you actually paid in 2023 on qualified student loans — interest paid in prior years is not deductible on a 2023 return. If the $4,800 is the total paid across earlier years (2016–2020), that is not deductible on 2023. If you actually paid $4,800 in 2023, you can deduct up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseout). With the income numbers you provided your MAGI should be well under typical phaseout thresholds for MFJ, but tell me your exact AGI or if your spouse has income and we can confirm.

4) Charitable contribution
- Your $1,350 donation to Habitat for Humanity is deductible if you itemize (with receipt). Since your likely itemized total is small, you’ll probably be better off taking the standard deduction instead of itemizing just for this gift.

5) Whether to itemize vs standard (practical recommendation)
- Likely take the 2023 standard deduction for MFJ ($27,700) unless you have additional large deductible items (mortgage interest, >$10k state & local tax or substantial medical expenses over threshold). From the items you listed, itemized deductions will be well under $27,700.

6) Reporting and paperwork to collect
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital.
- Any 1099-NEC or records for the $22,100 marketing income (report all income even if you didn’t receive 1099s).
- 1099-INT from Chase and 1099 (brokerage) from TD Ameritrade.
- Receipts and logs for home-office use (sketch of floor plan, square footage calculations, dates of use), receipts for utilities if allocating, receipts for the $1,350 business expenses (or documentation of what those expenses were), receipt for the Habitat donation, and Form 1098 (mortgage interest) if applicable (you didn’t report a mortgage).
- Documentation from Sallie Mae showing interest paid in 2023 (1098-E) — that indicates interest paid in the tax year and makes the $2,500 limit applicable.

7) Recommended forms to file (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — for student loan interest and the deduction for half of self-employment tax
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses including home office allocation if you’re self-employed
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use actual expenses for the home office (not needed with the simplified method)
- If you have a state filing requirement: IL Form IL-1040

Questions I need to finalize exact treatment and give precise numbers
1. Is the $22,100 marketing income from self-employment (you were an independent contractor) or was any of it paid as W-2 wages? Were 1099-NEC forms issued?
2. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses for your self-employment (marketing) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job at the hospital?
3. The $4,800 student loan interest: was that paid in 2023 or is that the total interest paid over the years 2016–2020? (Only interest paid in 2023 may be deducted on your 2023 tax return; you should have a 1098-E showing interest paid in 2023.)
4. Any other large deductible items (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax paid, large medical expenses, large charitable gifts) or spouse income we should include?

If you want, I can:
- run a more detailed estimate of your federal tax and self-employment tax with the numbers above (and show line-by-line calculations),
- prepare a checklist/email you can send to a CPA with the items and questions above,
- or draft the Schedule C/home-office worksheet numbers if you confirm the clarifying details.

Which would you like me to do next?

