- You said the office is exclusive and 130 sq ft and the home is 620 sq ft — that meets the “exclusive and regular use” and “principal place of business” tests for self-employed home office deduction.

Home office calculation
- Office % = 130 / 620 = 20.97% (≈ 21.0%).
Two methods to claim the home office:
- Actual-expense method (Form 8829): allocate a share of direct/indirect home costs (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) to the office. Example using your rent only: rent $1,720/month × 12 = $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,330 deductible as a business expense on Schedule C (you could also include allocable utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., which would raise this amount).  
- Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 home-office deduction (reported on Schedule C).  
Which to use: Based on rent alone, the actual-expense method (≈ $4,330) gives a much larger deduction than the simplified ($650). So actual-expense method is likely better if you’re comfortable keeping records for rent/utility/etc. — and you’ll use Form 8829.

Treatment of the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses”
- If those are business expenses for your self-employment (marketing, supplies, travel, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C.  
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to a W-2 job, they are NOT deductible on the 2018–2025 individual return (suspended by TCJA), unless you are in a special category (very rare). So clarify which they are.

Student loan interest ($4,800)
- Only interest you actually paid in 2023 is potentially deductible — up to $2,500 (above-the-line). Interest paid in earlier years (2016–2020) cannot be claimed on the 2023 return.  
- The student loan interest deduction phases out for married filing jointly at higher incomes (phase-out roughly starts around $145k MAGI in 2023). Based on the income numbers you gave, you’d likely be under the phase-out and could claim up to $2,500 of interest paid in 2023 — but I need the exact 2023 interest paid and spouse income to be sure.

Self-employment tax and other effects
- Self-employment net profit flows to Schedule C. You then compute self-employment tax on Schedule SE (Social Security + Medicare), but you can deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income (on Schedule 1).  
- Example rough calculation (using assumptions above):
  - Self-employment gross: $22,100  
  - Less business expenses: $1,350 (unreimbursed business expenses)  
  - Less home-office allocation (actual method from rent): ≈ $4,330  
  - Net self-employment income ≈ $22,100 − $1,350 − $4,330 = $16,420  
  - SE tax roughly: 15.3% × (0.9235 × net earnings) → 0.9235 × 16,420 ≈ 15,168 × 15.3% ≈ $2,320 (approx.)  
  - Deductible half SE tax ≈ $1,160 (adjustment to income)  
  - That net income (after deductions) increases your taxable income, but you get the above adjustment.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly in 2023: $27,700.  
- Your known itemizable items (from what you listed): charitable gifts $1,350. You didn’t list mortgage interest, property taxes, or state income tax amounts — those are often the biggest itemized deductions. If you don’t have significant mortgage interest or high property/state taxes, your itemized total is likely far less than $27,700, so you’d take the standard deduction.  
