Below is a focused, practical walkthrough for your 2023 federal tax situation (married filing jointly, Chicago, IL) based on the numbers you gave. I’ll show what’s deductible, whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag a few items I need you to confirm so the advice is accurate.

Summary of income you gave
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self-employment (marketing, 6 clients): $22,100
- Interest: Chase savings $920 + TD Ameritrade $290 = $1,210
Total gross income = $102,710

What you can (likely) deduct
- Home office (self-employed): Deductible if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business and is your principal place of business. You have two options:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650.
  - Regular method: business % of actual home expenses. Based on rent $1,720/month → annual rent $20,640. Business % = 130 / 620 = 20.9677%. Rent portion = 20.9677% × $20,640 ≈ $4,331. (You can also include the business portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have them.)
  - Conclusion: The regular method (≈ $4,331) is much larger than the simplified ($650) given your rent, so regular method is likely better unless you have other reasons to prefer simplified.

- Business expenses (self-employment): The $1,350 you listed as “unreimbursed expenses” is deductible if those costs were business expenses for your marketing work. (If instead they were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are NOT deductible under current law for tax years 2018–2025.)

- Self-employment tax: You must pay self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on your net self-employment earnings. You calculate Schedule SE; half of the self-employment tax is an “above-the-line” deduction that reduces your AGI.

- Student loan interest: Only interest paid in 2023 is potentially deductible (up to $2,500) as an adjustment to income and is subject to income-phaseouts for MAGI. You said $4,800 (Sallie Mae, 2016–2020) — if that $4,800 was paid in earlier years then it cannot be deducted on your 2023 return. If you actually paid student loan interest in 2023, tell me the amount paid in 2023 and I’ll check phase-outs. (For 2023 the student loan interest deduction phases out at higher incomes for MFJ — you may or may not be eligible depending on your final MAGI.)

- Charitable contribution: $1,350 to Habitat for Humanity is deductible only if you itemize (reported on Schedule A). Keep your receipt (you said you have it).

- Unreimbursed employee expenses: Not deductible for most employees (suspended through 2025). If those $1,350 are for your business (see above), they are fine as Schedule C expenses; otherwise not deductible.

Itemize vs. Standard Deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your known possible itemized deductions (charity $1,350, and you have no mortgage interest or property tax shown) are far below $27,700, so you will very likely take the standard deduction.
- Important: the home office deduction is taken on Schedule C (reduces your business income / AGI) and is independent of the decision to itemize. So even if you take the standard deduction, you still can (and should) claim the home office deduction on Schedule C.

Quick approximate comparison (to illustrate effect of home-office choices)
- Assume the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses.
- Simplified home office:
  - Net self-employment income = $22,100 − $1,350 − $650 = $20,100.
  - Approx net earnings subject to SE tax = 20,100 × 0.9235 ≈ 18,574.
  - SE tax ≈ 18,574 × 15.3% ≈ $2,841. Half deductible ≈ $1,421.
  - AGI estimate after half-SE adjustment ≈ (79,400 + 1,210 + 20,100) − 1,421 ≈ $99,289.
- Regular-method home office (rent portion only):
  - Home-office = $4,331 → Net self-employment = $22,100 − $1,350 − $4,331 = $16,419.
  - Net earnings subject = 16,419 × 0.9235 ≈ 15,171.
  - SE tax ≈ 15,171 × 15.3% ≈ $2,322. Half deductible ≈ $1,161.
  - AGI estimate ≈ (79,400 + 1,210 + 16,419) − 1,161 ≈ $95,868.
- Outcome: Regular method reduces AGI more and reduces your SE tax — it looks clearly better with the rent numbers you gave.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — Schedule C flows onto Schedule 1; student loan interest deduction is claimed here as an adjustment if eligible.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $22,100 and your business expenses, including home office (if using the simplified method you report the simplified deduction on Schedule C; for regular method you still report the business use but the detailed calculation is on Form 8829).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use this if you choose the regular method for the home office deduction.
- Schedule SE — calculate self-employment tax.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (doesn’t look beneficial given your numbers).
- Illinois: IL-1040 (state return). Illinois has a flat income tax (4.95% for 2023) — you’ll report your federal AGI and any adjustments per IL rules. You may owe state tax on net income; check IL-1040 instructions.

Other practical notes
- Report the Chase and TD Ameritrade interest according to your 1099-INT/1099-DIVs (you must report even if you didn’t receive a form).
- For self-employment income, report income even if you did not receive a 1099-NEC. If you did receive 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, keep them with your records.
- Keep documentation: home office square footage, rent receipts, utility bills, receipts for business expenses, the Habitat receipt, and any 1098/1099 forms.
- If you paid student loan interest in prior years and forgot to deduct it, you may need to consider amended returns for those years (not on the 2023 return).
- Consider whether you made estimated tax payments during 2023 for the self-employed income — if not, you could owe penalties for underpayment.

Questions I need to finalize or to give a more precise estimate
1. Were the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your self-employment work, or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job?
2. Did you actually pay any student loan interest in 2023? (You mentioned $4,800 over 2016–2020 — that won’t be deductible on the 2023 return.)
3. Do you have any other deductible items in 2023 (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax paid, large medical expenses, etc.)?
4. Did you receive any 1099-NEC, 1099-MISC, 1099-INT, 1099-DIV, or other information forms? Any estimated tax payments during 2023?

