3) Which deductions you can likely take (and which you cannot)
- Home office (self‑employed): Allowed if the 130 sq ft is used regularly and exclusively for your marketing business. This is reported on Schedule C (actual method) or you can use the simplified method. It is not an itemized deduction — it reduces your business income on Schedule C (so it lowers both income tax and self‑employment tax).
- Business expenses (self‑employed): If the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the marketing work, you deduct them on Schedule C (above‑the‑line for business). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 under the Tax Cuts & Jobs Act.
- Student loan interest: Deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment (up to $2,500) only for interest paid in the tax year. Past years’ interest payments are not deductible now. Also subject to income phaseouts — for 2023 the phaseout for MFJ begins at $145,000 MAGI and is gone at $175,000 MAGI. Based on the income you listed, you’d be under the phaseout threshold IF the interest was paid in 2023.
- Charitable contributions: $1,350 cash gift to a qualified charity is an itemized deduction (Schedule A).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally not deductible for 2018–2025 (except a few specific categories — not common).
- State/local tax (SALT): deductible on Schedule A but capped at $10,000 (SALT cap). You didn’t list state tax or property tax amounts — include them if you have them.
- Half of self‑employment tax: deductible as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (not an itemized deduction).

4) Home office calculation — two methods (with your numbers)
- Square footage ratio: 130 sq ft / 620 sq ft = 0.209677 (≈ 20.97%).
- Annual rent: $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
- Actual method (rent share only): 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,328.45 would be the portion of rent allocable to the home office. (If you also pay utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., a portion of those can be allocated too; depreciation is not relevant for renters.)
- Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650.
- Which to use: The actual method based on rent alone yields about $4,328 which is larger than the simplified $650, so actual method usually gives a bigger deduction — but you must keep good records and allocate utilities if claiming more. The home office deduction cannot exceed your net self‑employment income from that business (before the QBI deduction), so you can’t claim a loss from the home office beyond business profit without additional rules (carryovers and passive loss rules may apply).

Example impact if your $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses:
- Gross self‑employment income: $22,100
- Business expenses (example): $1,350
- Home office (actual): $4,328
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $22,100 − $1,350 − $4,328 = $16,422 (approx.)
- Self‑employment tax is computed on 92.35% of net SE income; half of SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1. You may also be eligible for the 20% Qualified Business Income deduction (Form 8995) on up to 20% of your qualified net business income (subject to rules).

5) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Your known itemizable deduction listed is $1,350 (charity). Unless you have significant mortgage interest, property taxes, or SALT up to $10,000, your likely itemized total is much less than $27,700.
- Conclusion: Based on the numbers you gave, you almost certainly should take the standard deduction for 2023 rather than itemizing. Important: the home office deduction and student loan interest (if paid in 2023 and eligible) are ABOVE‑THE‑LINE or business deductions and do not depend on whether you itemize — you can take those and still use the standard deduction for Schedule A.

6) Forms you’ll probably need (federal + state)
