I can walk you through how these items are treated for 2023, give a quick estimate, and tell you which forms you’ll need — but a couple of clarifying questions first so my guidance is accurate:

Quick clarifying questions
- Are you filing with a spouse who has additional income? (You said “married filing jointly” but gave only your incomes.)
- The $4,800 student‑loan interest: was that interest paid in tax year 2023, or are those totals from earlier years (2016–2020 as you wrote)? The deduction only applies to interest you actually paid in 2023 (reported on Form 1098‑E for 2023).
- The $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” — were those out‑of‑pocket costs related to your freelance/marketing work (so business expenses), or unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer job? (The tax treatment is very different.)
- Does your $1,720/month rent include utilities, or are utilities billed separately?

High‑level summary (straight to the point)
- You will report:
  - W‑2 wages ($79,400) on Form 1040 (W‑2).
  - Self‑employment/marketing income ($22,100) on Schedule C (profit or loss from business). Report related business expenses on Schedule C (see home office, unreimbursed expenses if they are business-related).
  - Interest income ($920 + $290 = $1,210) on Form 1040 (Schedule 1 input).
- Home office: you can take the home office deduction because you have self‑employment income and you say the office is used exclusively (130 sq ft of a 620 sq ft home ≈ 21.0% of the home). You may use either:
  - Actual expense method (allocate rent, utilities, etc. by the business percentage) — requires Form 8829 (if you claim depreciation or more complex allocations) and the Schedule C to show the business expense; OR
  - Simplified method ($5 × number of office sq ft, capped at 300 sq ft → 130 × $5 = $650).  
  Given your rent ($1,720/mo → $20,640/yr), the actual allocation of rent to the office (~$4,331/yr = 20.9677% × $20,640) will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified method if you can allocate rent/utilities to the business. Use the actual method unless you prefer simplicity.
- Student‑loan interest: up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest paid in 2023 can be claimed as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (so it reduces AGI), subject to MAGI phaseouts. Based on the incomes you reported, you’re likely under the MFJ phaseout threshold (but I’ll confirm if you have spouse income).
- Charitable gift ($1,350): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There is no universal above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2023. If you itemize, this adds to your Schedule A total.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA) if they are employee/unreimbursed from an employer. If those $1,350 are ordinary business expenses for your self‑employment (marketing) they should be deducted on Schedule C instead.
- Self‑employment tax: you must pay self‑employment tax on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Standard vs. itemize: for 2023 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $27,700. Compare that to your itemizable deductions (charitable $1,350 + state & local taxes up to $10,000 cap + any mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses >7.5% of AGI). From what you’ve given (only $1,350 charitable and no mortgage interest or large property tax amounts reported), it is very likely you will take the standard deduction rather than itemize.

Rough example calculation (estimate so you see the effect)
- Gross income: 79,400 (W‑2) + 22,100 (self‑emp) + 1,210 (interest) = 102,710
- Business expenses (example using actual home‑office allocation + business expenses):
  - Home office (rent allocation): 130/620 × $20,640 = ≈ $4,331
  - Other business expenses (you listed $1,350) = $1,350
  - Total business expenses ≈ $5,681
- Net self‑employment income ≈ 22,100 − 5,681 = 16,419 (this flows to Schedule 1 / Form 1040)
- Self‑employment tax (approx): net × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $2,323; half (~$1,161) is an adjustment to income
- If you paid up to $2,500 student loan interest in 2023, deduct up to that (subject to phaseouts)
- After these adjustments, you subtract the standard deduction $27,700 (likely larger than your itemized total). This leaves your taxable income and determines income tax owed; self‑employment tax is in addition.

Forms you will likely need for Federal
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and your business expenses (including home‑office allocation if you use the actual method)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expenses method for the home office (allocation of rent, utilities, depreciation, etc.). If you use the simplified $5/sqft method, you do not use Form 8829.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize (charitable gift goes here)
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — keep for your records and to support the student loan interest deduction (and report the amount on Schedule 1)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if you received any for your freelance work) and 1099‑INT for interest income (if received)

State return
- IL Form IL‑1040 to report Illinois income and pay state income tax. Illinois has a flat tax; keep state withholding amounts and state tax paid handy (SALT limit affects federal itemized deduction).

Other items and planning points
- Quarterly estimated payments: because you have self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (federal and state) if your withholding doesn’t cover your tax liability or you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file.
- Keep documentation: receipts for charitable donation, invoices/receipts for business expenses, a copy of your lease showing rent payments, utility bills if you allocate utilities to business, and Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer for student loan interest paid in 2023.
- QBI deduction: you may be eligible for the 20% Qualified Business Income deduction on your net self‑employment income (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). This is somewhat complex and depends on taxable income and the type of business. If eligible, it could reduce taxable income further.
- If any of the items I assumed (e.g., that the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business-related, or that the $4,800 student interest is not 2023 interest) are different, the outcome may change materially.

Recommended next steps
1. Confirm the clarifying items I asked above (spouse income, whether the $4,800 was paid in 2023, and whether the $1,350 is a business expense).
2. Gather your documents: W‑2, any 1099s, 1098‑E (student loan interest statement), 1099‑INTs, rent/utility records, receipts for business expenses and charitable donations.
3. Decide whether to use simplified ($650) versus actual method for the home office. Based on the rent you reported, actual expenses will almost certainly give a bigger deduction.
4. If you’d like, I can:
   - Estimate taxes owed with the numbers you provided (I can run a quick worksheet showing federal income tax + self‑employment tax estimates), or
   - Draft a checklist of documents and line‑by‑line where to enter each item on the tax forms, or
   - Recommend a short script / email you can use to communicate with a tax preparer and send them your numbers.

Tell me the answers to the clarifying questions (spouse income, year of student‑loan interest payments, whether the $1,350 are business expenses) and I’ll give a tighter calculation and a suggested filing checklist.

