- Likely best to take the standard deduction ($27,700 for MFJ in 2023) unless you have substantial additional itemized deductions (mortgage interest, big state/property taxes, larger charitable gifts, etc.).
- Self-employment income (the $22,100) is reported on Schedule C; you’ll owe self-employment tax (Schedule SE) on net self-employment profit and can deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- Home office: if the 130 sq ft is used regularly & exclusively for your self-employment marketing work, you can take a home office deduction (either simplified method or actual expenses). This deduction flows through to reduce your Schedule C net income.
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in 2023 on a qualified student loan is deductible (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts). Past-year interest (2016–2020) is not deductible now and cannot be carried forward. Clarify how much interest you paid in 2023.
- Charitable gift ($1,350): deductible only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct the charitable contribution on Schedule A.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350): not deductible as an itemized miscellaneous deduction for employees for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA). If those expenses are business expenses related to your self-employment work, they should be deducted on Schedule C instead.

Detailed points and approximate calculations

1) Income reporting
- W-2: report on Form 1040 (with W-2 attached/entered).
- Self-employment / marketing $22,100: report on Schedule C. If you received 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, include those amounts. Any business expenses (see below) reduce this income.
- Interest: report the $920 and $290 on Form 1040 (Schedule B only required if total interest/dividends > $1,500).

2) Home office deduction (you said 130 sq ft exclusive; total 620 sq ft; rent $1,720/month)
- Qualification: must be regular & exclusive use and principal place of business (or used to meet clients). If so, you can deduct.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 × square footage (up to 300 sq ft). For 130 sq ft → $650 deduction. Simple and often preferred.
  b) Actual expense method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses to the office. Percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97% (~21%). Example using rent only:
     - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640
     - Home office portion of rent = 20.97% × $20,640 ≈ $4,329
     - You may also allocate utilities and renter’s insurance similarly (you did not provide utility amounts).
  - If you use actual method, you normally compute via Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) and attach to Schedule C. If you use simplified method, you report the simplified amount on Schedule C (no Form 8829 needed).
  - Which to choose? If you only have rent and utilities are moderate, actual method often yields larger deduction given your numbers (e.g., rent portion ≈ $4,329 vs simplified $650). But compare actual combined share of rent + utilities + renter’s insurance — actual could be bigger. If you use actual, keep records of rent and utilities.

3) Self-employment tax and other Schedule C items
- Net profit on Schedule C = $22,100 − (business expenses including home office deduction if for this business) − other business expenses (you mentioned $1,350 unreimbursed — if these are self-employment business expenses, they go here).
- Self-employment tax = 15.3% applied to 92.35% of net SE income (reported on Schedule SE). You may deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income) — subject to rules, but at your income level you’re likely eligible. QBI is claimed using Form 8995 (simplified) in most lower-income cases.

