  - Self‑employment gross receipts: $22,100
  - Interest/other: $920 + $290 = $1,210
  - Total gross income ≈ $102,710

Two home‑office scenarios (simplified vs actual). Both reduce Schedule C net income (and thus self‑employment tax).

A. Simplified home‑office method
- Home‑office deduction = $650
- Business expenses (other) = $1,350 (assumed)
- Schedule C net profit = $22,100 − $650 − $1,350 = $20,100
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × net ≈ 0.1412355 × $20,100 ≈ $2,840
- Deduction for half SE tax (adjustment to income) ≈ $1,420
- Student loan interest deduction (adjustment) = $2,500 (max)
- Adjusted Gross Income (approx) = $102,710 − $1,420 − $2,500 ≈ $98,790

B. Actual method (using rent share)
- Home‑office rent portion ≈ $4,330 (20.97% of $20,640)
- Business expenses (other) = $1,350
- Schedule C net profit = $22,100 − $4,330 − $1,350 = $16,420
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 0.1412355 × $16,420 ≈ $2,320
- Half SE tax deduction ≈ $1,160
- Student loan interest = $2,500
- Adjusted Gross Income ≈ $102,710 − $1,160 − $2,500 ≈ $99,050

Note: these are approximate. The actual method here reduces your Schedule C profit more (because of the large rent share), which lowers SE tax and overall taxable income. If you use actual method, you’ll need to make sure the deduction does not exceed your net business income (any excess can be carried forward under certain rules).

3) Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly in 2023 = $27,700.
- Your only clearly itemizable federal deduction you mentioned is charitable $1,350 (plus possibly state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes, etc., if you have them — you didn’t list those).
- With only $1,350 of itemizable charitable giving (and no other itemizable amounts given), itemizing would be far less than the $27,700 standard deduction. So in that case you should take the standard deduction.
- Important: taking the standard deduction does NOT prevent you from deducting business expenses (Schedule C) or taking the student loan interest above‑the‑line deduction. The home office deduction (if legitimate) and Schedule C expenses will reduce taxable income either way.

4) How to treat the home office (practical guidance)
- Confirm you meet the IRS rules: exclusive and regular use of that 130 sq ft space for your marketing business, and the space is your principal place of business (or used to meet clients, etc.).
- Choose a method:
  - Simplified: easy recordkeeping, claim $650 for 130 sq ft.
  - Actual: you’ll need records for rent, utilities, renters’ insurance, repairs, etc.; you prorate by the business percentage (~21%). For renters, depreciation of the building is not applicable; so actual method will mostly be rent + utilities apportioned. Use Form 8829 when using the actual method; that form computes the deduction and connects to Schedule C.
- If your Schedule C net profit ends up zero or negative, some home‑office actual deductions might be limited — the disallowed amount may carry forward to future years.

5) Forms you will need (federal and state)
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
