Short answer up front
- Most of what you listed can be properly deducted, but how depends on whether the $22,100 is self‑employment income (it looks like it is). If it is self‑employment income, report it on Schedule C and you can deduct the home office and business expenses there. If it’s W‑2/employee income, you cannot take the home‑office or unreimbursed employee expenses on your federal return (those unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible 2018–2025).
- With the information you gave, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction for 2023 (Married Filing Jointly = $27,700) rather than itemizing (your itemizable deductions listed are small).
- Use Schedule C (business income), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and Form 8829 (if you use the actual‑expense method for the home office). Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction up to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). Charitable giving is deductible only if you itemize.
- I can show approximate numbers and the tax impact if you confirm a couple of details below.

What you can deduct (federal, 2023) — based on your list
1) Business income / expenses (if the $22,100 is self‑employment/contractor income)
   - Report gross $22,100 on Schedule C.
   - Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses (supplies, travel, marketing, etc.) they belong on Schedule C and reduce self‑employment income.
   - Home office: allowed if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business. Two methods:
     - Actual method (Form 8829): you may deduct your business share of rent, utilities, insurance, etc. Your ratio = 130 sq ft / 620 sq ft ≈ 20.97%. Annual rent at $1,720/mo = $20,640. Rent portion allocable to the office ≈ $20,640 × 0.2097 ≈ $4,328 (plus any utilities etc. allocated).
     - Simplified method: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650 (no Form 8829).
   - Home office and other Schedule C expenses reduce net self‑employment income and lower both income tax and self‑employment tax.
   - Self‑employment tax (Social Security/Medicare) is computed on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

2) Student loan interest
   - Deductible up to $2,500 (adjustment to income) on Schedule 1, subject to MAGI phaseouts.
   - Phaseout for MFS/MFJ in 2023: full deduction allowed for MAGI well under $145,000; phases out $145k–$175k (MFJ) and is eliminated above $175k. (If your family MAGI is below $145k you can claim the full up to $2,500.)
   - Note: only interest paid in the tax year (2023) qualifies. If the $4,800 you quoted is interest paid in prior years, only 2023 payments count.

3) Charitable contribution — $1,350
   - Only deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take standard deduction, this does not reduce your federal taxable income.
   - You have the receipt — good. Keep it for records.

4) Interest income — $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210
   - Report as interest income on Form 1040. Schedule B is required only if total taxable interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (so you do not need Schedule B with $1,210).

5) Unreimbursed employee expenses — $1,350
   - If these are employee unreimbursed job expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.
   - If they relate to your self‑employment work, report them on Schedule C.

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Based on what you listed (charitable $1,350, no mortgage interest or large property taxes shown), the standard deduction for MFJ in 2023 ($27,700) is almost certainly better than itemizing.
- Business deductions (Schedule C) are separate from Schedule A itemizing and will reduce your taxable income regardless.

Which forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you choose the actual‑expense method for the home office (not needed for simplified method)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — half of SE tax deduction and student loan interest deduction are reported here
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (charitable contribution goes here)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if you received them) and 1099‑INT from banks (report interest)
- IL‑1040 — Illinois state return (state forms differ; check IL rules and instructions)

Quick illustrative numbers (estimates) — assume the $22,100 is self‑employment and the $1,350 is business expense
- Option A — actual home‑office method (use rent portion):
   - Home office rent portion ≈ $4,328
   - Other business expense $1,350
   - Net self‑employment income ≈ 22,100 − 4,328 − 1,350 = 16,422.50
   - Self‑employment tax ≈ $2,322; half deductible ≈ $1,161
   - Wages + net SE + interest = 79,400 + 16,422.50 + 1,210 = 97,032.50
   - Subtract half SE tax ≈ 1,161 → AGI ≈ 95,871.50 (before student‑loan interest deduction)
   - Subtract standard deduction 27,700 → taxable income ≈ $68,171.50 (before student‑loan interest deduction)
   - If eligible for student loan interest deduction, reduce AGI by up to $2,500 (if your MAGI is under the phaseout).

- Option B — simplified home office ($650) instead of actual:
   - Net SE ≈ 22,100 − 1,350 − 650 = 20,100 → higher AGI and higher SE tax than Option A.

These are illustrative estimates to show the relative impact — I can run a more precise calculation for you (including exact SE tax, estimated federal income tax) if you want.

Key action items / questions for you
1. Confirm whether the $22,100 is self‑employment/1099 income (not W‑2). If yes, confirm that the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for that work.
2. Confirm whether the $4,800 student loan interest was paid in 2023 (only interest paid in the tax year can be deducted). If it was paid in prior years, it does not apply to the 2023 return.
3. Confirm whether your $1,720/month is rent (not mortgage) and whether utilities are separate — this affects the home office calculation.
4. Do you want me to run a full estimated tax calculation (federal tax owed / refund estimate) for 2023 using the options (actual vs simplified home office, itemize vs standard)?

Brief compliance note
- I’m giving general guidance. For definitive tax filing and advice specific to your full facts (e.g., carryovers, spouse’s income/credits, AMT issues, state specifics), consider working with a CPA or tax preparer.

If you want, I can:
- Recalculate with whichever assumptions you confirm above, and give an estimated federal tax due/refund.
- Draft a checklist/email you can send to a tax preparer with the documents they’ll need.

