- Rent (or mortgage payment?) $1,720/month → $20,640/year.  
- Student loan interest: $4,800 (unclear which years paid).  
- Charitable cash contribution: $1,350 (you have receipt).  
- Unreimbursed expenses: $1,350 (unclear whether business-related).

Which items are deductible for 2023
- Self-employment business expenses: Deductible on Schedule C if they are ordinary and necessary business expenses for your freelance marketing work. That would include the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses if they are business costs.  
- Home office: If you use the space exclusively and regularly for your self‑employment work, you can claim a home office deduction (for self‑employed only). See details and comparison below.  
- Self‑employment tax: You’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment income; half of the SE tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).  
- Student loan interest: Deductible up to $2,500 (above the line), subject to MAGI phaseouts for MFJ in 2023 (phaseout roughly $145,000–$175,000). Only interest actually paid in 2023 counts.  
- Charitable contribution: $1,350 deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).  
- Interest income from Chase/TD: Taxable and reported on Form 1040 (and reported on any 1099‑INT you receive).  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses tied to your W‑2 job: Not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA), unless you have some very specific exception.

Standard deduction vs. itemizing (quick conclusion)
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $27,700.  
- Your known itemizable amounts: charitable $1,350 plus any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses, etc. You didn’t list mortgage interest, property taxes, or large SALT amounts. Based on the numbers you gave, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than your itemized total. So you will likely take the standard deduction unless you have additional large itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, big state/local taxes (subject to $10k SALT cap), large medical expenses above threshold, or other sizable charitable gifts).

Home office — how to treat it (two methods)
You qualify only if (1) the part of your home is used regularly and exclusively for business and (2) it is your principal place of business for the self‑employment work.

Compute the business percentage:
- 130 sq ft / 620 sq ft = 0.20968 → about 21.0%

Two options:

1) Simplified method
- $5 per sq ft × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction for 2023.
- Easier recordkeeping; no Form 8829 depreciation calculation. Good for small/home offices.

2) Actual expense method
- Allocate actual home expenses by business percentage (21%).
- If your $1,720/mo is rent: annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Business share of rent ≈ $20,640 × 21% ≈ $4,330.
- You may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., by the business percentage — that raises the deduction further.
- If you own, you can also allocate mortgage interest, property taxes, depreciation, etc., but that’s more complex.

Which is better for you:
- Based on the single number you gave ($1,720/mo rent), the actual method (business share ≈ $4,330 of rent alone) yields a larger deduction than the simplified $650. So the actual method will likely give a bigger home‑office deduction for 2023. Use actual method if you want the larger deduction and can document expenses. Use Form 8829 to compute the deduction (or report per Schedule C as required). Note: the home office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from that business for the year (excess may carry forward in some cases).

How this affects taxes generally
- Home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit → reduces both income tax and SE tax (so you save both).  
- You’ll owe SE tax on net self‑employment income (after business expenses including the home office). SE tax is ~15.3% on net earnings (subject to the 92.35% adjustment). Half of SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income.  
- Student loan interest (if paid in 2023 and if your MAGI is under the phaseout) reduces your AGI (above-the-line).  
- Charitable gifts help only if you itemize — unlikely to beat the standard deduction here.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).  
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student loan interest and the deductible half of self‑employment tax.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses (including unreimbursed business expenses if they belong to the business).  
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expense method for the home office. (If you use the simplified method, you don’t need to complete Form 8829 but must still report the deduction properly on Schedule C.)  
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably won’t be necessary unless you have other large deductions).  
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC: check for any 1099s from clients; those support your Schedule C income.  
- 1099‑INT & brokerage statements (1099‑INT, 1099‑B, 1099‑DIV) for the Chase and TD Ameritrade income.

State return
- Illinois: you’ll also file an IL state return (IL uses a flat tax — check IL filing requirements). State taxable income starts from federal AGI with certain modifications.

Estimated taxes / payments
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe estimated quarterly taxes for 2023 (if you hadn’t paid enough through withholding). If you expect similar income in 2024, plan for quarterly payments to avoid penalties.

Other items to consider
- 20% QBI deduction (Section 199A): your qualified business income from the sole proprietorship may be eligible for up to a 20% deduction. This is income‑based and can be beneficial; mention it to your preparer or I can compute an estimate once you confirm net profit and other items.  
- Keep good records: receipts for business expenses, your Habitat for Humanity receipt, student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E if you received one), bank/broker 1099s, rent receipts or lease showing monthly rent, utility bills, etc.  
- If you prefer a precise tax estimate (AGI, tax, SE tax, expected refund/amount due), I can run the numbers for you if you answer the clarifying questions above and allow me to use the amounts you provided.

Summary recommendation (based on info you gave)
- Likely take the standard deduction ($27,700 MFJ) unless you have sizable additional itemized deductions (mortgage interest, big SALT, etc.).  
- Claim self‑employment income and expenses on Schedule C; if the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business-related, include them on Schedule C.  
- Claim home office deduction — actual method likely yields a larger deduction (≈ $4,330 of rent allocation) than the simplified ($650), assuming those numbers and that you rent. Use Form 8829 for actual method.  
- Deduct student loan interest only if it was paid in 2023 and subject to the $2,500 cap and income phaseout.  
- File Form 1040 + Schedule C + Schedule SE (+ Schedule 1 and Form 8829 if applicable). File state return for IL too.

If you want, I can:
- Walk through a sample calculation (AGI, taxable income, SE tax) using the assumptions above, or
- Prepare a checklist of documents and line items to give to a tax preparer, or
- Estimate your self‑employment tax and federal income tax based on either the simplified or actual home office method.

Which of those would you like next? And please answer the clarifying questions about the student loan interest, whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business-related, and whether you rent or own.

