Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’ll cover what’s deductible, whether you should probably itemize, how the home-office deduction works, what forms you’ll need, and a rough example of how the numbers flow. A few quick clarifying questions are at the end so I can make the answer more precise.

Summary / short answer
- Most likely you will take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) unless you have additional large deductible items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, large medical expenses, or much more charitable giving).
- You can and should report your self-employment (marketing) income on Schedule C; that’s where you deduct business expenses (including a home-office deduction).
- The home-office deduction looks beneficial here if you use the actual-expenses method (your rent allocation for the office is likely larger than the simplified-method amount).
- Student loan interest is an adjustment to income (not an itemized deduction) but is limited to $2,500 per year and phases out at higher AGI — you must have paid that interest in 2023 to claim it.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W-2 job) are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current federal law; if your “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the self-employment income, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Details and how to treat each item

1) Income you reported
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 — report on Form 1040 (wages).
- Self-employment / marketing income: $22,100 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Any business-related expenses (supplies, advertising, home office, etc.) reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $290 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,210 — report as interest income on Form 1040. (If you have 1099-INT / 1099-B statements include those.)

2) Home office
- You said 130 sq ft exclusive office in a 620 sq ft rental. Business-use percentage = 130 / 620 ≈ 20.97%.
- Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640. Allocated rent for the office (actual-expense method) ≈ $20,640 × 20.97% ≈ $4,330.
- Two methods:
  - Actual-expense method: allocate a share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. to the business. For a renter, the rent portion allocated to the business is a Schedule C business expense. If you use actual expenses, you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.
  - Simplified method: $5 × square feet (max 300 sq ft). For 130 sq ft = $650. Simpler but likely smaller than the actual-expense rent allocation in your case.
- Important: The space must be used regularly and exclusively for business to qualify (you said exclusive — good). The home-office deduction for a self-employed person reduces net Schedule C profit (thus lowering both income tax and self-employment tax).

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If those are unreimbursed employee expenses tied to your W-2 job: not deductible under current federal law (suspended).
- If they are business expenses related to your self-employment/marketing work: deductible on Schedule C (add them to your other business expenses).

4) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (reported on the 1040 / Schedule 1) and is limited to $2,500 per year (maximum) for tax year 2023.
- You must have actually paid interest during 2023 to claim it. If $4,800 refers to cumulative interest paid across multiple years (2016–2020), that does not qualify for 2023. Use the 2023 interest paid amount from your lender’s 1098-E (or statement).
- There is an AGI/MAGI phaseout for the deduction; depending on your modified AGI, the $2,500 may be reduced or eliminated. Check the MAGI rules or have software/CPA confirm.

5) Charitable donation ($1,350)
- Cash donation to Habitat for Humanity is deductible only if you itemize. For 2023 there is no above-the-line universal charitable deduction for non-itemizers (that was a temporary measure in 2020).
- If you itemize, keep your receipt and documentation.

6) Self-employment tax and special deductions
- Net self-employment income (Schedule C profit) is subject to self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare), calculated on Schedule SE (about 15.3% on net earnings after the 92.35% adjustment).
- You may deduct one-half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1). This reduces your AGI.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) of up to 20% of qualified business income depending on taxable income limits and business type. That’s a more complex calculation; check with software or a tax pro.

7) Unreimbursed employee expenses, moving expenses, etc.
- As noted: miscellaneous employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for 2018–2025.

Itemize vs standard deduction — quick assessment
- 2023 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $27,700.
- Your known itemizable amounts appear to be: charitable $1,350, plus any state/local taxes you paid (including state income tax withheld on your W-2, any property taxes you paid, etc.), mortgage interest (if any), large medical expenses (if >7.5% of AGI), etc.
- Unless you have additional substantial deductible items (mortgage interest, big SALT amounts up to the $10,000 cap, high medical expenses, or much larger charitable giving), the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than your itemized total. Based on what you gave me, you’re likely better off taking the standard deduction.

What forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — individual tax return.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report $22,100 and related business expenses (home office, supplies, unreimbursed business expenses if those are business-related).
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use actual-expense method for home-office deduction).
- Schedule 1 — to report adjustments to income such as student loan interest deduction and half of self-employment tax (Schedule 1 is attached to Form 1040).
- If you have investment sales or dividends you may need Schedule D or Form 8949, but for $290 interest you likely just report it on Form 1040 interest lines (Schedule B is required only when interest + ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 or there's other conditions).
- If you receive 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients, include those amounts on Schedule C.
- State: IL-1040 (Illinois state income tax return) — file for Illinois taxable income. (Chicago has no municipal income tax.)

Rough example calculation (illustrative, with assumptions)
Assumptions:
- Self-employment gross = $22,100.
- Business expenses = home-office allocation $4,330 (actual-expense method) + $1,350 other business expenses = $5,680 total business expenses.
- Net Schedule C profit = $22,100 − $5,680 = $16,420.
- Interest income = $1,210.
- Wages = $79,400.
Gross income = $79,400 + $16,420 + $1,210 = $96, 1? Wait — that mixes net vs gross. Better to compute from original totals:
Total income = 79,400 + 22,100 + 1,210 = 102,710.
Adjustments:
- Self-employment tax (approx): SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (92.35% × $16,420) ≈ $2,323 (approx). Half of SE tax deductible ≈ $1,161.
- Student loan interest (if you actually paid in 2023 and qualify) — max deductible up to $2,500 (but subject to phaseout).
Example adjustment subtotal ≈ $1,161 + up to $2,500 = $3,661 (if you qualify for full student interest deduction).
Estimated AGI ≈ 102,710 − 3,661 ≈ 99,049.
Subtract standard deduction (if you take it) 27,700 → estimated taxable income ≈ 71,349.
This is an illustrative path — your taxable income and tax owed will differ depending on:
- The actual amount of student loan interest paid in 2023.
- Whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses.
- Whether you elect simplified home-office ($650) instead of actual.
- Any other deductions/credits (child credits, retirement contributions, etc.)
Use tax software or a preparer to compute exact tax and self-employment tax.

Practical next steps / checklist for you
1. Confirm whether the $4,800 student loan interest is interest paid in 2023 (get 1098-E / lender statement for 2023). Only 2023 interest paid matters for 2023 return.
2. Confirm whether the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are:
   - employee unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job (not deductible), or
   - business expenses for your self-employment (deductible on Schedule C).
3. Gather documentation:
   - 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC from clients (if any).
   - 1099-INT (Chase) and 1099 (TD Ameritrade) statements.
   - Rent receipts / lease and utility bills (for actual home-office expense allocation).
   - Charitable donation receipt.
   - W-2 from Riverside Hospital (and spouse’s W-2 if applicable).
   - 1098-E for student loan interest for 2023.
4. Decide whether to use the actual home-office method (Form 8829) or the simplified ($5/sqft). With your rent the actual method likely gives a larger deduction, but use your documented amounts to confirm.
5. Consider estimated tax payments for current year if you expect similar self-employed income (to avoid penalties).
6. If you want, I can:
   - Run through a more precise numeric example if you confirm the missing details (2023 student loan interest paid, whether unreimbursed are business expenses, any other deductible items, spouse income).
   - Or draft a concise email to your tax preparer summarizing the facts and asking for filing guidance (I can draft that for you).

Caveat
This is general tax information, not formal tax advice. For exact calculations and to address phaseouts (student loan interest MAGI limits, QBI rules, state particulars), please confirm with tax software or a CPA.

Which of the following would you like next?
- I’ll estimate your tax owed using the assumptions above (I’ll need you to confirm whether the $1,350 is business-related and the exact 2023 student‑loan interest amount).
- I’ll draft an email to your tax preparer summarizing your facts and asking what to do.
