Thanks — I can help. First I’ll summarize what looks deductible, then give a recommended approach (itemize vs standard), show a short example calculation using the numbers you gave, list the forms you’ll likely need, and finish with questions where I need clarification to be exact.

What you can (likely) deduct
- Self-employment business expenses (Schedule C): Your marketing income ($22,100) is self-employment income and you should report it on Schedule C. Ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work are deductible on Schedule C. That includes:
  - Home office: You can claim a home-office deduction for the exclusive 130 sq ft used for your business. You may use either:
    - Actual-expense method: business % = 130/620 ≈ 20.97%. If your only allocable expense is rent ($1,720/month → $20,640/year), the home-office share of rent ≈ $20,640 × 130/620 ≈ $4,330. You can also allocate utilities and other shared home costs the same way if applicable.
    - Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650.
    - Note: You may only claim a home-office deduction for the self-employment work (not for W-2 employment).
  - The $1,350 you listed as “unreimbursed expenses” — if those were expenses related to your self-employed marketing work, they are deductible on Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed expenses from a W-2 employer, they are NOT deductible under current law (suspended 2018–2025).
- Self-employment tax: You will owe self-employment tax on net self-employment earnings (Schedule SE). You may deduct one-half of self-employment tax as an adjustment to income (above-the-line).
- Student loan interest: The student loan interest deduction is an “above-the-line” deduction up to $2,500 (for qualifying interest actually paid in the tax year) subject to income phaseouts and other rules. You cannot deduct interest paid in prior years on your 2023 return — only interest paid in 2023 qualifies. Also, the $4,800 you quoted exceeds the $2,500 limit and thus at most $2,500 may be deductible (and only if you meet the MAGI limits).
- Charitable gifts ($1,350): Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There is no special universal charitable subtraction for 2023 (the temporary 2020-only above-the-line charitable amount does not apply).
- Investment interest/interest/interest income: The $920 (Chase) and $290 (TD Ameritrade) are interest (or investment) income and must be reported; any associated fees for producing that income could sometimes offset it, but typically these appear on Schedule B (if required) and are included in gross income.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- For married filing jointly in 2023 the standard deduction is $27,700.
- Your likely itemizable items given what you reported:
  - Charitable: $1,350
  - State and local taxes (SALT): you didn’t list state income or property taxes — these can be significant but are capped at $10,000 total for SALT.
  - Mortgage interest or large medical expenses: you didn’t list any.
- Student loan interest is NOT an itemized deduction (it’s above-the-line).
Given your numbers as provided (no mortgage interest, no large SALT beyond $10k listed, charitable only $1,350), it is very likely that the standard deduction ($27,700) will be larger than your itemized total. So in most cases you will take the standard deduction.

Brief illustrative estimate (rough)
- Gross income:
  - W-2 wages: $79,400
  - Self-employment gross: $22,100
  - Interest income: $1,210
  - Total ≈ $102,710
- If you choose the actual home-office expense for the self-employment business and treat the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses as business expenses:
  - Business expenses ≈ home office $4,330 + other business expenses $1,350 = $5,680
  - Net self-employment income ≈ $22,100 − $5,680 = $16,420
  - Self-employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × $16,420 ≈ $2,322 (approx). You then deduct half of that (~$1,161) as an adjustment to income.
- AGI (rough) ≈ W-2 79,400 + net SE 16,420 + interest 1,210 = 97,030. Minus half SE tax ≈ 95,869. If you qualify for the student loan interest deduction (max $2,500), AGI would fall to ≈ 93,369.
- Subtract standard deduction $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $65,669 (these are rough illustrative numbers).
- Income tax at current MFJ brackets (mostly 12%) plus SE tax would yield an approximate total tax before withholding of roughly $9–10k in this example — but your actual result will vary depending on exact figures, whether you take simplified home-office method, whether the $1,350 unreimbursed costs are business expenses, and how much was withheld on your W-2.

Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — net self-employment profit flows here and student loan interest adjustment may show here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $22,100 and business expenses (including home office share or simplified deduction)
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual-expense method for the home office (not required for the simplified method)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (unlikely from the info provided)
- Forms 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if you received them from clients) — you should include the income even if you didn’t receive a 1099
- Form 1099-INT (from Chase) and the appropriate 1099 from TD Ameritrade (1099-INT/1099-DIV/1099-B as applicable)
- Illinois state return (IL-1040) — state filing required for Illinois residents

Important caveats and notes
- Student loan interest deduction: you must have actually paid interest in 2023 to deduct it on the 2023 return. The $4,800 figure you quoted appears to be an aggregate over multiple years (2016–2020) — that cannot be claimed on your 2023 return. Also the deduction is limited to $2,500 and phases out at higher incomes.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: if these relate to your W-2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025. If they relate to your self-employed marketing work, deduct on Schedule C instead.
- Home-office limitations: your home-office deduction cannot create or increase a net loss in certain circumstances. The home-office deduction is only for the portion of your home used exclusively and regularly for business.
- Estimated tax/withholding: because you have self-employment income, you may owe self-employment tax and possibly underpayment penalties if you didn’t make estimated tax payments or didn’t have enough withholding. Check your W-2 withholding and any estimated payments.
- State tax: Illinois has its own rules and a flat tax; be sure to prepare an IL return.

What I need from you to be precise / next steps
